      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>7 ..,,i,.. . k,,</head>
        <p>7 ..,,i,.. . k,,</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Grand Duchess Elizabeth—lf the late Grand Duke Segius was the best hated man in Russia, the Grand Duchess was, and is,one of the best loved among, women in that unhappy country. Her Royal Highness was married to the Grand Duke when nineteen years of age, and was perhaps the merriest and handsomest of the Late Princess Alice's beautiful quartette of daughters. But the union was not a happy one the Grand Duchess soon began to lose her girlish gaiety, and in its place came a settled sadness and resignation, those near and dear to her found painful to contemplate. The Czarina the Princess Henry of Prussia, and Princess Louis of Battenberg, are devoted to their sister who has never let her own iorrows make her forget those of others, and whose gentleness and sweetness of disposition endear her to all with whom she csmes in contact. Princesses' Likes and Dislikes.—Princess Henry of llattenberg is the only one of the King's sisters who I attaches any importance to Royal state and ceremonial. This, no doubt, is to be accounted for by the fact that so much of her early life was spent in the presence of the Throne. For this reason the King and Queen have always been careful to study Princess Henry's wishes, and ber comings and goings at Court are attended with the same state and circumstance as in the days of Queen Victoria. Princess Christian, and Princess Louise— Duchess of Argyll—on the other hand, regard such matters with indifference, and prefer,vvhenever possible to put aside their Royal birth and station. This dis- like of ostentation is also freely shared by their three nieces—the King's daughters—who have not between them a single lady-in-waiting. Marriage Gossip in Court Circles.—The present year seems likely to witness several royal weddings. The marriage of the German Crown Prince and the Grand Duchess Cecile, has been definitely fixed for May 24th, and there is much talk in Berlin Court circles concerning a possible alliance between the King of Spain and the Roman Catholic branch of the Grand Ducal house of Mecklenburg Schwerin. King Alfonso has not a very i wide choice n looking for a bride, more especially ashe has been strongly advised to marry outside those families which have hitherto intermarried with the Spanish Royal House. These are the Bonrhons, and the Raps- burghs, but the Princesses of these two families whose age would make them suitable, are all near relatives of the young King of Spain, and it is not unlikely that there may be some truth in the Berlin gossip that he is after all, to marry a German Princess. Another Saxe Coburg Engagement.—The Princess Victoria Adelaide of Schlesing Holstein, whom the young Duke of Saxe-Coburg is to marry, is a niece of the Empress of Germany. and is a slight handsome girl not yet twenty. The Duchess of Albany, like her sister-in-law, the one time Duchess of Edinburgh, be- lieves in early marriages, and her son, the Duke of Saxe-Coburg, is not vet twenty-one. He was born at Claremont on July 19th, 1331, four months after the sudden death of his father, the late Duke of Albany. His only sister Princess Alice in his senior by about a year and a half, and recently became the wife of Prince Alexander of Teck. The Duke was somewhat delicate in his boyhood, but he has grown stronger, and has taken very kindly to German ways. It is not likely that he will be seen mnch in this country, as he now owes allegiance to the German Emperor, and his friend is the Kaiser's second son, Prince Eitel, who is recovering from his recent illness, in which his life was almost despaired of. A Society Engagement.—Lady Mary Lygcn, whose engagement to Major the Hon. Henry Forbes, is announced, is a very pretty, charming woman, very quiet, and something of a recluse. The Princess of Wales is much attached to Lady Mary, who is one of her ladies-in-waiting, and who accompanied her Royal Highness on the tour which she took round the Colonies shortly after the Coronation. Lady Mary Lygon when she went to Australia with the Princess, already knew the country well, for she had been out with her brother Earl Beauchamp when he took up the governorship of New South Wales. Lady Mary had a pleasant time during her stay in Sydney, and the Colonial folk would be delighted to see her there again. The Domestic Servant Problem.—The newly married Lady Barrington thinks that the domestic servant diffi- culty is in no small degree the result of the altered ways of masters and mistresses. Fifty years ago, she says, households were managed with a view to home comforts for the family and servants. Early hours were kept, the servants knew what was expected of them, they became attached to the home, and studied their employers interests. Now it is the exception to find ahousehold governed on any such regular old- fashionedllines. The home in the case of a great num- ber of families, has become simply a place of abode. All hours are kept, whilst a succession of dinner parties, card parties, and supper parties after theatres make constant demands on the servants. There is no regular breakfast time some wanting their meal at one hour and others at another, or requiring it in their bed- rooms. Instead of any settled family life, there is con- stant disturbance which discourages servants from trying to give satisfaction, or from taking any interest in the home beyond the affairs of the moment. All this may be true, but then unfortunately it is not the whole story, as Lady Barrington would appear to think. It is true that family life has greatly altered in the past generation or two but the change has effected all classes, and it may be doubted whether quiet old- fashioned ways would make domestic service more attractive than it is to the girls of the present day. Belts, Bows, and Buttons.—The belt is doomed, for all bodices and the best blouses, are being built to be worn outside the skirts. These new bodices all come to a sharp point in front, which has a most becoming effect upon the figure. Bows of all kinds, shapes, and sizes are in the greatest request, but one unvarying rule is that they must have long dangling ends. The little black bow is exceedingly chic, and much sought after by the woman who pins her faith to the old belief that no toilette is complete without one touch of black. Regarding buttons, these are distinctly more ornamental than useful, being rarely used as fasteners. Enamel, gun-metal, gilt, old silver, and diamante specimens, all appear, but the newest are those of velvetitrimmed with metal. Shopping Made More Easy.—The tendency to offer a variety of goods at a certain price is growing in all large drapery establishments. In one part of a show- room for example, there is a selection of hats and toques at say two guineas, in another part those priced at one guinea are set apart, while in other portions of the showroom, headgear priced at 10s. and ss. respec- tively, may be discovered. This idea undoubtedly saves both the customer and the assistant much time and trouble, and the same method is being followed in the blouse, and skirt departments, departments set apart</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>9 . • 1... IF Met flu L,Ii• lir • tiso ;TO</head>
        <p>9 . • 1... IF Met flu L,Ii• lir • tiso ;TO</p>
        <p>"A perfect beverage, combining Strength, Purity and -Solubility."—llfe*al Annual.</p>
        <p>I for underwear, and many others. Another excellent move on the part of the great emporiums, is, to allow people to walk from one department to another, and look at the goods set out, without worrying them to make purchases. They have borrowed the notion from the Americans, and they say it pays. Variety in Fashions.—ln the matter of spring wraps there is an infinite variety, widely different lengths and shapes being oft-red us. First and foremost come the redingotes which cover the gown from neck to hem; then we have the coats drawn in tightly at the waist, from whence they flow out to just below the knees ; and again the little boleros, very like those of last autumn only more tightly-fitting at the waist. The little three- tiered capes are delightful, as they can be worn over dainty bodices and blouses, without fear of crushing them, and in box-cloth they possess finite sufficient sub- stance to meet our March winds. Grey and fawn are the leading shades, which can be worn with almost any gown, but where rigid economy has not to be practised, it is better that the wraps and skirts should match. MADGE.</p>
        <p>tion Act in 1903 and also to the difficulty in getting any information as to how the county estimates were made up. The Board had passed a resolution, which they asked the Runcorn Guardians to adopt, that the County Councils of Derbyshire and Cheshire be approached with a view of getting them to supply the clerks to the several Boards of Guardians in the two countries with a copy of the estimates of the Clerks to the County Councils of county rates each half year in sufficient time for them to send the same out with the precept of the overseers. The matter was referred to the Parliamentary Com- mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>HAVE YOU CATARRH?</head>
        <p>HAVE YOU CATARRH?</p>
        <p>CATARRH IS THE MOST PREVALENT DISEASE IN GRE IT BRITAIN.</p>
        <p>IT AFFECTS THE NOSE, THROAT, AND EARS,A)n) CAUSES BRONCHITIS,ASTHMA,&amp; CONSUMPTION. 50,000 BOOKS TO BE GIVEN FREE TO SUFFERERS WHO APPLY AT ONCE.</p>
        <p>A scientific treatise by one of the most practical and advanced writers, dealing exhaustively with Catarrh and Lung and Chest Diseases, showing aufferers how to diagnose their own case and treat themselves with per- fect safety at home. It gives particulars of a prompt and inexpensive cure. This indispensable and highly important medical work should be in the bands of every sufferer. A copy will be sent on receipt of name and address (post card will do). Address, Dr. Cassell's Co. Ltd., 65, C., Ayton Buildings, King-st.,WestManchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Relieving Officer Resigns</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. Relieving Officer Resigns</p>
        <p>AFTER NEARLY THIRTY YEARS SERVICE.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, Alderman A. R. Norman presiding. There were also present: Messrs. T. Baxter, S. Woodward, C. E. Linaker, J. Mounfleld, .1. Hallows, J. Spruce, C. Roylance, J. L. Jones. G. Pilling, J. H. Davies, H. M. Davies, A. G. Guest, T. Guest, W. M. Morris. J. Douglas, J. W. Temperley, J. Whitley, G. Egerton Warburton, J. Smith, T. Wright, W. Harrison, S. Horton, E. Bratherton, C. Horton, R. Billington, W. Turner, W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), T. Cook, T. Riley, and W. R. de Prez (relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (master.) THE CH XPL A INC Y. Mr. %V. E. Knollys, assistant secretary to the Local Government Board, forwarded the sanction of that body to the appointment of the Rev. A. L. Temple to the 3ffice of chaplain at the Workhouse at a salary of £3O per annum. He added that no sanction of the Board was necessary to the appointments of the Rev. H. Donlevy and Mr. T. Tarran as religious visitors to the Catholic and Nonconformist inmates respectively. Mr. J. Cunnah, the Workhouse master, reported that the Rev. J. Trampleasure, vicar of Whitley. had under- taken the Sunday morning services until the newly appointed chaplain takes up his duties.</p>
        <p>TELE TRIALS OF GUARDIANS.-WHAT THEY NEED TO BE ACQUAINTED WITH. The Chorlton Union forwarded for approval and adoption the followning resolution: " Thlt this Board is of opinion that the time has arrived when a serious attempt should be made to consolidate the laws relating to the:reliet of the poor, and they consider the Local Government Board may reasonably be called upon to effect without delay the consolidation, of their own orders." It was pointed out that to carry out their duties efficiently the Guardians needed to be acquainted with 250 Acts of Parliament, about 5,000 decisions in the Courts and "no end "of general orders ; that many of these laws were obsolete, inconsistent, and out of harmony with present day requtrements and many were also so contraditory that they could not be admin- istered; the Guardians were prohibited from giving relief to an able-bodied man and his family, however, deserving and respectable, for more than a week, unless he submitted to the labour test,and when through ignor- ance of the law or sympathy with the poor, they broke some of these ancient laws, they came into conflict with the Government auditor, as the Chorlton Board had now done. .</p>
        <p>The Board decided to support the resolution. , RELIEVING OFFICER'S RESIGNATION. LONG AND FAITHFUL SERVICE. M. T. Riley wrote tendering his resignation of the various appointments he holds under the Board, namely those of relieving officer, registrar of births and deaths, vaccination officer, and inspector under the Infant Life Protection Act, 1897. It was with extreme regret that he took the step but the state of his health and advanced years convinced him that it vcas a necessary</p>
        <p>one.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he was sure they would all ragb. to receive that coaimunication from such an old and valued servant. The Deputy Clerk said Mr. Riley had held the posi- tion of relieving officer for nearly thirty years. Mr. Linaker said he wished to bear testimony to the very high appreciation the Board had of rvlr. Riley as a men and as an officer. He was known throughout his district as a high-minded, conscientious man. He had, however, reached the age of seventy-four and after such an active life it was time he took a deserved rest. In his retirement he would have the good feeling and heartiest wishes of the Board. He (the speaker) sincerely trusted Mr. Riley's superannuation would be made as high as possible. Se vend memoers spoke in very eulogistic terms of Mr. Riley's services, and the resignation was accepted with regret. A small committee was appointld to consider the matter of superannuation. It was decided to advertise for a successor, the age limit being fixed at forty years. THE INCREASE IN COUNTY RATES. The • Clerk read a letter from the Hayfield Union, Stockport, drawing the attention of the Guardans to the serious increase in the County rates in the counties of Chester and Derby, since the passing of the Educe-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WORKHOUSE BOILER,</head>
        <p>THE WORKHOUSE BOILER,</p>
        <p>...... On the recommendation of a special Sub-Committee it was decided that Mr. W. Timmins, engineer, be requested to meet the }louse Committee and confer with them as to the alterations necessary in connection with the Workhouse boiler. FINANCIAL. THE COST OF EDI,CATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward reported, the receipt of demands for County rate Co the amount of £3098 3s. 11d.and Educa- tion rate to the amount of £3761 13s. 10d., making a total of £6859 18s. 9d. That WAS a decrease of £179 165.4 d. as coinpared with the previous half year. The total payments for the day amounted to £706.3 us. 6d. and they had a balance to their credit of £2,222. In answer to Mr. Roylance. Mr. Woodward said the Education rate showed an increase of £3O is. 6d. upon the previous half year, accounted for by special ele- mentary education calls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>EMPLOYMENT IN 1904.</head>
        <p>EMPLOYMENT IN 1904.</p>
        <p>Summary.</p>
        <p>The downward movement in employment which com- menced about the middle of 1900, continued during 1904. Employment was good and better than in 1903 in the tinplate manufacture ; it showed little change in coal and iron mining, being moderate in the former and good in the latter ; but in most other industries it was dull and showed a decline as compared with 1903, especially in the metal, engineering, shipbuilding,and boot and shoe industries. In thelcotton trade employ- ment was bad for the greater part of the year, much short time being worked owing to the exceptionally high price of raw cotton, hut since the fall in the price of cotton full time has been resumed and nearly all de- partments have been fully employed. In the woollen trade employment improved during the year, but in the worsted trade it remained slack. Returns relating to about 570,00 e of Trade Unions show that the mean percentage of members unemployed at the end of each month of 1901 was 6.5, compared with 5.1 in the previous year. The figure for 1904 is the highest percentage sincel 1894, when the proportion was 6.9 per cent., while in 1893 it was 7•5 per cent. Although 1904 was much below an "average year," there have been several years in which employment was worse. Taking the previous 25 years, such was the case in 1879, in each of the years 1884.87. and in 1893 and 1894. Following each of these periods of depression there has been an improvement, culminating in years of good employment (such as 1882, 1889-90, and 1899-1900) and succeeded by a decline to the next period of depression. DETAILS FOR PRINCIPAL TRADES.</p>
        <p>BUILDING TRADES.—Employment was considerably worse than in the previous year. Generally it was bad throughout 1904, except with masons in England, with whom it was reported moderate.' The mean percentage of Trade Union carpenters and joiners and plumbers unemployed at the end of each month was 7.6, the highest figure in the period (e;ommencing 1888) for which these percentages have been recorded. In 1888 the percentage was 55; it fell to 22 in 1890, rose to 4•1 in 1894, and fell to 13 in 1898, since which date it • hes risen steadily to 4.3 in 1902, 4-9 in 1903, and 7•6 in 1 1904. PIG IRON. MANUFACTURE.—EmpIoyment in this industry in 1904 was bad, except in Scotland. From' 1896 onwards monthly returns have been received relat- ing to the liworks of 117 ironmasters, and covering nearly all the blastfurnaces in the United Kingdom. The average number of furnaces in blast at these works rose from 346 in 1896 to 379 in 1899, and then fell to 368 in 1900, with a further marked decline to 310 in 1901. In the two following years there was some recovery, the average number of furnaces in blast being 322 in 1902, and 321 in 1903, but in 1904 the average fell to 302, the be eat recorded. IRON AND STEEL WORKS.—The department receives monthly returns from employers, and in the case of 177 works these returns have been furnished throughout the seven years 1898-1904. The returns show that the number of workpeople employed increased in the three years, 1898-1900, and fell off in the next three years, 1901-1903. to nearly the level of 1898. In 1901 there was very slight increase on the previous year, but the number employed remained less than in 1902. The average number of shifts worked in 1004 was 534 per week, compared with 5.38 in 1903 and 5•36 in 1902. Taking account of both the number of shifts worked and the number of persons employed, it is found that the volume of employment was 03 per cent less in 1904 than in 1903 and 2.0 per cent less than in 1902. EN,GINEERING.—EmpIoyment showed a downward movement during the year, and on the whole was very dull and worse than in 1903, or in any year since 1894. On the North East Coast there was a slight revival in the early months of the year, and the mean of the percentages of Trade Union members unemployed in that district at the end of each month of 1904 was thus rather less than the mean for 1903. In every other dis- trict there was some decline,-the falling off being great- est in Lancashire and in Notts., Derby, and Leicester district. The mean percentage unemployed varied con- siderably in the different diptricts. In the Oldham, Bolton, and Blackburn district it was 11,1, in the East of Scotland 109, in Glasgow and district 9-4, in the West Riding towns 9'l, and in Belfast and Dublin 90, while in the London district it was 4.6. For the United Kingdom as a whole the mean percentage unemployed was 7.0 compared with 4'9 in 1902. For the eleven 1 years 1894-1904, the mean percentage unemployed I was 4.7.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>MEM. FOR MOTORISTS.</head>
        <p>MEM. FOR MOTORISTS.</p>
        <p>A chauffeur of Paddington was summoned at West London Police Court on Monday for allowing the engine of a stationary motor car to be in motion. The car was stopped outside a public-house,and the engine continued to work with a loud vibrating sound. The magistrate imposed a penalty of 18s., with 2s. costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>We Paid £20,000</head>
        <p>We Paid £20,000</p>
        <p>For Liquozone—Yet We Give You a 25./3d.</p>
        <p>We paid £20,000 for the British rights to Liquozone—the highest price ever paid for simi_ lar rights on ,rmy scientific discovery. We did this after the product bad been tested for five years, through physicians and hospitals, in several countries, it had been proved in thousands of the most difficult cases obtainable. It bad cured every disease which had been considered incurable We paid that price becanse, in germ diseases, Liquozone does what medicine cannot do.</p>
        <p>Now we ask you to try it—try it at our expense. Let us give you a bottle, to prove what it can do for you. A test is better than testimonials, better than argutnentq.</p>
        <p>There lies the great value of Liquozone. It is the only way known to kill germs in the body without killing the tissues, too. Any drug that kills germs is a poison, and it cannot be taken in- ternally. Medicine is almost helpless in any germ disease. That is why diseases which have resisted Medicine for years yield at one to Liquozone. And it cures diseases which medicine never cures. In germ diseases, the results are almost inevitable.</p>
        <p>Bottle Free.</p>
        <p>All diseases that begin with fever—all inflammation—a catarrh—all contagious diseases—all the results of impure or poisoned blood.</p>
        <p>In nervous debility Liquazone acts as a vitaliser, accom- plishing what no drugs can do.</p>
        <p>A 2s/3° Bottle Free.</p>
        <p>If you need Liquozone, and have never tried it, please send us this coupon. We will then post you an order on a local chemist for a 2s. 3d. bottle, which he will give you, on our account, to try. This is our free gift, made to convince you; to show you what Liquozone is and what it can do. In justice to yourself, please accept it to-day, for it places you under no obligation whatever. Liquozone costs Is. lid., 2s. 3d. and 4s. 6d.</p>
