      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>" WORAN " OR "LADY " ?</p>
        <p>As to the rather subtle question of "gentleman," "lady," "man," and "woman," there is a difference, writes a correspondent, between the sexes. A duke must always be a man, unless he be, for the purpose of the conversation, a "gentleman" with great emphasis of voice. Without emphasis, every man of gentleman's rank is a "man" always and in every social circumstance. But with women it is entirely a matter of the adjective. Without an adjective a woman is a "lady." Who ever asked, on hearing that a friend was to marry, "Who is the woman?" Nor do we say that we met a woman at dinner who told us so-and-so. But introduce the adjective, and the "lady" is at once a pretty woman, a well - dressed woman, rather a dull woman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>NEAT TWEED COAT.</head>
        <p>NEAT TWEED COAT.</p>
        <p>The spring brings with it a distinct rebound from the wide-sleeved, much-becaped Russian blouse coats that have lately claimed our affections to tightly-fitting coats, treated, with flat trimmings, and for the most part with plain coat sleeves. Our attention is directed to the present model, which shims a neat little coat in a light grey tweed: there is no collar, the small revers being faced with i black velvet. The front of the coat s strapped with the material, and ornamented with square</p>
        <p>pewter buttons. The sleeves are neatly finished with a black velvet cuff, and the basque is fulled on under a narrow strapped waistband. The full skirt is cut to touch the ground, and is trimmed with three rows of black velvet. The hat worn with this costume is a study in yellows. It is composed of rows of silk frilling, the row nearest the hair being very pale, and the outside row quite deep in tone, the feather, which forms the sole trimming, being also shaded from pale to deep yellow. •</p>
        <p>slAutlo. .-44111114$ „, .. lts - .f.,.. ''' . • „ ....., . . , i.e. . 1 a: 1 : - . ...:0' Alif: . vim 4, isvillijilietlit,W</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>The President (Mrs. T. P. O'Connor) and members of the Society of Women Journalists are to be "At Home" on the afternoon of the 26th inst. at His Majesty's Theatre, which has been lent for the occasion by Mr. Beerbohm Tree. Mr. T. P. O'Connor will deliver a lecture on "Parliament and its Personalities," ,and Mr. Tree will occupy the chair.</p>
        <p>The Royal warrant concerning the ladies of the Scots Lords of Session has corrected an anomaly, and supplied a long-felt want. Hitherto, while a Lord of Session was always "my Lord," his lady was merely a "Mrs." She did not like this, naturally. The announcement of: "Lord and Mrs. So-and-So," on festive occasions, was grotesque. But now my Lord's wife is, very properly, to be my Lady, during her husband's life, and for as long after as she rains his widow. French society differs from English in one essential point, says the Paris correspondent of the Pall Nall Gazette. Conversation in French drawing- rooms and dinner-parties is general, not particular. It is considered bad form to hold a tete-a-tite with your neighbour on personal tastes or other indi- vidual matters. Everything is supposed to con- tribute to the amusement and enlightenment of the company.</p>
        <p>Ladies foremost in philanthropic undertakings,and who make touching and really practical speeches on topics of the day, most moral and edifying in every way, see no harm, trenchantly remarks a correspondent, in giving large,dinner and luncheon parties on Sundays, and so deprive their servants of the rest which they can rightly claim, and pre- vent them from attending to their spiritual needs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>INDOOR- BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>INDOOR- BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>Our present sketch shews a dainty indoor blouse, which could be effectively made in quite a variety of materials. One fabric that deserves more atten- tion as a blouse stuff than it receives at the pre- sent time is mohair. Some of the finer and silkier makes of this are very lovely, especially those bearing a tiny spot or floral pattern. For instance, we will suppose that our model is to be realised in a flexible cream mohair. The under-bodice or deep</p>
        <p>yoke is vompletbly covered with fine gaugings, nut over it comes the cross-over part, which ties in a knot at the bend of the figure, the back being also rucked across and drawn down into a V-shape to display the gathered yoke. The sleeves are made with two puffs, separated by a band of gathers, and finishing in a long gathered cuff. The belt must ne made in one pie with the bodice lining, and might be of coloured Mind silk finished with enamel buttons down the front. NEW STYLE OF "WOMAN'S PAGE."</p>
        <p>A new style of "Woman's Page" appears in a Chicago paper. It.is given up entirely to divorcees ,--divorcies past, present, and prospective—all neatly classified for ready reference, and artistically arranged under an appropriate illustrated heading, shewing little crying cupids beaten and battered by the marital storms through which they have passed the whole profusely, illustrated, of course, with attractive pictures of the principals. This, remarks a contemporary, seems to us something like a woman's page, tar more interesting than the baffling descriptions of how to make chairs out of barrels, and delectable three-course luncheons for the unexpected guest out of last night's leavings. And it is altogether fitting that Chicago should be the birthplace of this new journalistic feature. That most pusliful city holds the world's record for the number of divorced persons.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>RAGOUT ore En +s.—Fry a teaspoonful of chopped onion in a dessertspoonful of clarified dripping, and when the onion is lightly coloured stir in a tablespoonful of flour and inix with the dripping. Add half-a-pint of milk, and stir till the sauce is tnooth and thick. Season with salt, pepper, and a little mace. Simmer for ten minutes, and strain. Cot four hard-boiled (~,„s into slices, put them in a saucepan, pour the sauce over them, and add a dessertspoonful of chopped parsley and six drops of vinegar or lemon juice. Take some haricot beans which have been boiled till tender, drain them, and heat with a little butter; arrange rounsi a dishl and pour the egg mixture into the middle.</p>
