      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MY GREATEST SUCCESS IN GOLF,</head>
        <p>MY GREATEST SUCCESS IN GOLF,</p>
        <p>HORACE G. HITTCHINSDN.</p>
        <p>Now that the amateur championship has en- joyed a flourishing existence for seventeen year,. so that it may be considered of the time of life ot promising debutante, . ready to be presented at a drawing-room,- I am asked to . write the story hi its beginnings, that is 'say of the earliest amateur championship. And this is rather a delicate matter, because it so happened that by the mysterious workings of fate I won the first two championships of the amateurs, so that it almost is necessary that the first persone! pronoun pill figure much in the narrative. t shall be as little obstrusive as my natural pride will permit. I can say no more. And yet,. in saying that I won the first two amateur championships, I am not altogether certain that I am right, for there was a kind of an amateur championship, or a meeting that xyas equiva- lent to it, and was in fact the origin of the inor, fully recognised thing, • held •at lioylake. in the year preceding any winning of mine. That wa4 in 1885—a fearfully long while ago now; and Mr. Macfle was the winner. , THE ORIGIN OF THE CEAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>It is not a bit too much to ally that the• omateur championship entirely owes its institu- :lion to the Hoylake Club, which is more cotrectly, and magnificently, called the Royal Liverpool Uolf.Club. So far as 1 remember, the beginning of it was a kind of circular note sent round to myself and one or twcr others by Mr. Potter, at that time honourary Secretary of the R.L.G.C.. asking .us to form -a committee to look after a :ig tournament—the word championship was rather left :out of it, just at first, until it should receive official recognition and become ft}l annual thing—to be held in the Hoylake medal week. We formed the committee—we did not look after the, tournament a bit, because Mr. Potter and the corninitee of the R.L.G.C. did all that. • The only question that came up. so far as I remember, for our decision, was whether Douglas Rolland should be allowed to play as an amateur. I was much in favour of.hts playing— he was a plasterer, then, or something of that kind—but it was found that lie had carried flimbs for hire lately (I think that was his offence), and so he was not allowed to play. When the time mine we set to work and played merrily. Exactly what happened all ilie :way down the lists it would be wearisome to set cl.)Wn. even if I could remember. it., Ik-sides are they net written in the chronicles ? One of the things that frequently happened was that Mr. Mactle amid Mr. Walter de Zoete kept halving their matches. The tale could not : then have been, aS it s now, that people who 'halve shall not play on till one wins. At that time both halves, ,so to :speak, must have gone into the next ronnd. Eventually Mr. Mettle won, chiefly. if I:remember right, by 'doing the short hole (the Rushes) in one. In the meantime I do not think I had any discomposing matches until I met Mr. ' Johnnie " Ball in the semi- final. Mr. Maefie had it bye—they did not have the BagualL•Wyld play as it is called, in those days-,-so in the morning': Mr. Mactle walked about, like a gentleman, while " Johnnie " Ball and I had one of the worst matches ever played. remember that I flaked a three at. the Cop " hole, after being in the banker off the tee, and also remembefthat be was two . up on the way home. flow I managed to beat him I do not know, but it happened ; and in the afternoon I had to .play Mr. Mactie, whom I expected to "heat. I had not expected to beat. Mr. Ball. . think Mr: Maafie beat me by seveh up mid six to play. It was something quite as satisfactory in the way of a beating—that Ido remember. lie played very well, andif very • badly that is a tally adequate explanation ot how it all hap- Iwned. It is a more common explanation of, the result of a match than people seem to suppose.</p>
        <p>THE FIEST RECOCINISED criAmpiossmr.</p>
        <p>This tournament started the amateur champion- ship. Rules were drawn up, Clubs appointed delegates, everything was arranged in the most beautiful manner, Mid the first formally recog- nised championship (though all the honours of champion rank belong to Mr. Madle) was played at tit. Andrews in the year following. Naturally one's own fortunes are those that come to mind most forcibly in the retrospect of these tournaments. This first regular champion- ship at St. Andrews excited uS all a good. deal —I think chiefly as being some new thing: and an occasion for measuring swords such as had not been known before. I forget who was my first opponent, but recollect being told by my caddie that it I defeated him I should have to meet "a man Fogey !' (more correct* and courteously, Mr. Foggo) "free Earlsferry—an awful fine player, an' dirma' mind the gallery wan bit." Whether much of a gallery would have bothered themselves about Mr. Foggo and myself I do not know, for when I came in from my r6mgl, I heard, to my vast relief, that " the man Fogey " had been beaten in his first round by Doctor McCuaig.‘ This was all very well, but if Mr. Foggo was all that my caddie said of him the defeat of him cast a lurid light on the • prowess of Dr. McCuaig. And the light grew more lurid when it was reported to me (pro- bably quite falsely) that Doctor McCuaig bad said, that night, that he would beat Horace Hutchinson, who, though a good, was a young player ! It seems impossible now to think that this last could ever have been tine, but no doubt it was once. However, Doctor McCrtaig did not beat me. In fact he played much below his game, and gave no trouble. There was comedy in those days as well as tragedy. -I do not remember all my matches of that championship, but one was with Mr. Dime Fergusson, and he was two up and eight to play. Then he pulled his approach to the short hole, altogether away to the left of the big bunker. Certain shots one remembers. I re- member .mjr own to that hole—a flukey, half. topped thing, skidding over the low bunker, just running up past the corner of the little pot bunker and actually striking the back . of the hole, at which poor old Mr. Bethune, of Bleb°, was standing, holding the flag. It was a lucky shot as ever was played, and might have been hi either Of the :two bunkers ; but after that luck went all my way.</p>
        <p>WOBEING THROUGH TO THE FINAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. " Johnnie " Ball and Mr. " Johnn e " Laidley were the two one • had to be especially afraid of in those days. • I do not know what happened to the latter, the Scottish " Johnnie," in ,any of these first three championships. I think he played in all of them, but he never looked dangerous. But "Johnnie "Ball did look dangerous. In the semi-final of that 1886 championship he bad to play poor Mr. Henry Lamb. It was just about this time that Mr. Ball was being a, sore trouble to his friends at Hoylake. He always could win their medalS there, always could bring in an uncommon fine score, was really (except •by a kind of fluke) unbeaten when be was playing his game on his own green, but he never could succeed in doing himself anything like justice when he went away from home, It was most distressing and disappointing. lie has done such wonderful things since- that one does not mind recalling these early failings. He has avenged them cruelly. But in 1880 that was bow matters stood. It was said- that on the night preceding this semi-final with Mr. Henry Lamb he went to bed at nine o'clock. On the next mornng. whatever be did over night, he played as if he never had been to bed at all. Mr. Lamb, playing steadily, beat bitn by a vast number of holes. Again,. as in the ease of " the man Fogey," this was it great relief to me, for, good player as 111 r. Lamb was, I would a great deal sooner have met him than Mr. Ball. I have a distinct and painful recollection that we both played heinously badly for the first hole or two of that final match. Then • 1 happened to find my game first, and. Mr. Lamb never findingvahis, 1 beat hint at the heathery bole coining in.</p>
        <p>It is rather dremlful to have to write so much about rme.,ol ls.,r ww• prepnred for that at the outset ; and, after all, no one need read it. The next championship of the amateurs was held at Hoylake' in the year following. , In this enconnter I have a most vivid recollection of a very terrible battle with Mr. Gry.ror Mac- gregor. I believe, that we ended the round .all square, and by the recognised rules which had come into force the previous year, played on, to settle the matter. The matter settled itself, against Mr. Macgregor, mainly by bad luck at the nineteenth hole. Mr. John !Ball, senior, with his singular style, was a very dangerous player on his own green of lloylake in those days. He was fond of issuing a challenge that he and his son would play any two ; but there were not many takers. I ran against Mr. Ball, senior, in the semi-final of that championship, and was never more badly frightened. The play was good too, I believe, on both sides. 'We either were all even. or Mr. Ball was one up and tint</p>
        <p>to play, coming to the seventeenth hole which then was much the same as the present ' nine- teeth. Mr. Ball made one of his few bad shots for the round—topped his iron approach into the little ditch bunker guarding the green (I think the gallery bothered him) and the last hole I won, and so the match. Then, having done with the father, it remained to reckon with the son—a heavier reckoning—in the final. No doubt I had made just the right libartion and invocation to St. Andrews before that champion- ship, began for I defeated Mr. Macgregor. as aforesaid, chiefly by accident, then Mr. Ball, senior, by misadventure, -he happening into the bunker in front of last but one hole, and rinally Mr. Ball, junior, when we were going along very evenly, had the excellent good luck, for me, to break his brassey. Then, when rye were all even and one to play, he topped his tee shot to the last hole into the bunker, which was not Very far in front of the tee. Even after that he Might have had a putt for a half of the bole and of the match if I had not happened to put in a rather good one, the two previous. strokes being weakly played.</p>
        <p>A GOLF STORY.</p>
        <p>That is the end of the story of the champion- ships so far as I had any ado with winning them and high time that a story so much about oneself came to an end. In the next year's meeting, which was held at Prestwick, 1 was .knocked out by Mr. " Andy " Stuart, who beat me by the simple and ci!witins means of playing a good deal better -than. I played. But, in course of that match. I played a shirt that no -other probably has played in any championship. At, the hole after the Himalayas' going out I played a lofted shot from behind the sand hills west of the green, after slicing the second; and the ball alighted in the breast pocket of the late Mr. James Kirk, who died at St. Andrews only the ether day: And what was even more cur:ous is that neither Mr. Kirk nor any of Uzi. thirty or so spectators had seen the ball come over and go into his pocket, for. I was out ot sight when I played .the shot. Only, when my caddie and I asked where • the ball was, the ground being perfectly open, we were assured it had not come over the hill. Of course -we knew better, and finally Mr. Kirk said "I did, feel something,tug at my pocket," and, , looking in, there was the ball. There are people living i.ho can vouch for this story.. I should not tell it but for their confirmation. After that it seemed but a small matter to lose the .champion- ship: . That year was he beginning, of Mr. Ball's victories the beginning of his breaking the spell of • that had Seemed to beset him and make it impossible for him to do him- self full justice away from borne. Hoyinke, that is to say the Royal Liverpool Golf Club, deserve the thanks of all of us who are interested m golf for starting this amateur championship, and the same club, in 1902, took the initiative in what is pretty sore to become no less popular as an .annual fixture' the International match. Hoylake is just at the right place, geographically, for taking the lead in affairs such as this —just a good °half- way boils° between . Edinburgh and London— and the Royal -LiverPool Club always does it in just the right Way-, never Arrogating any authority to itself in virtue of being the first to start these movements, but referring to St. Andrews and all recognised authorities as by law appointed There is not alwio this sweet reasonableness about the golfer.</p>
        <p>Next . Wee/: " BEHIND THE BARS." BY J. J. DITGOA.X.</p>
