      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>LOCAL HOCKEY MATCHES. ,</head>
        <p>LOCAL HOCKEY MATCHES. ,</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. CHESTER.</p>
        <p>Played at Stockton Heath. The visitors won the toss and from the bully-off the visitors had the better of the play, and the home goal-keeper had to kick out in the first few minutes. The game then took a more even course, both sides attacking in turn. At length W. Longdin scored for Stockton Heath from close in. There was no more scoring in the first half. Half-time: Stockton Heath, one goal; Chester, nil. The visitors should have scored in the first half, only bad play in the circle keeping the Stockton Heath goal intact. in the second half the home side had things nearly all their own way, and goals were added by C. H. Bond and S. Baxter (3), the final score being 3-0. IA LATCHFORD v. ORFORD BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>Played at Latchford. From the bully-off the horn team began to asert their superiority, which they main- tained throughout the game, scoring on 14 occasions. The visitors never got in - dangerous quarters, az the home defence was very sound. The goals were scored by Banks, four; Carter and Pearson three each, and Spenseley and Albinson two each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Burtonwood Vicar on Bazaars.</head>
        <p>Burtonwood Vicar on Bazaars.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell, of Burtonwood Vicarage, thus delivers himself concerning " Bazaar Religion' : „ . . . . . .</p>
        <p>The practice of "raising the wind" for church or chapel by means of bazaars, fancy fairs, etc., is still in 3bgue, and is as popular as ever. The bazaar, apparently, has come to stay, if it can. We are not cheered by any prospact of its speedy departure. This anti-Christian Institution has wrought much evil in its day, and the end is not yet, for the people lord it, and, alas ! the parsons love it most of all. Here is a nice little quartet of mis- chiefs which the bazaar has encouraged aud fostered :- 1. Unwomanliness—forwardness, pertness, flirtation, "smartness."</p>
        <p>2. Dishonesty—" the almightly raffle," " false pre- tences," "undue influence." 3. Drink—not always sub rosa. 4. Copper Christianity—almsgiving a matter of "Sunday pennies" only. "Bazaars are wrong in principle. Well, what of that? Get hold a the L's and principle will take care of itself." The solution of every church financial problem is pretty much one and the same—the anti-Christian bazaar. lam one of the foolish parsons who want to build a new church without resorting to the religious mark. People shake their heads, as if doubtful of one's sanity. "Bazaar religion" must be sent "packing."</p>
        <p>While a farmer, named W. B. Adams, of Drumraw, near Ballymena, was visiting Archibald Golloway. at Shieldstown, on Saturday night, the latter's house caught fire. When assistance arrived Adams was dis- covered to be quite dead. It is conjectured that Adams and his friend were asleep when the fire occurred. Oolloway was rescued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>HOW JEWELS ARE GUARDED.</head>
        <p>HOW JEWELS ARE GUARDED.</p>
        <p>It is very curious to observe with what jealous care some people will guard certain of their possessions, and at the same time leave other things of infinitely more value as prey for any accident that comes along. Not many winters ago, the jewel .laden daughter of an Earl drove to a ball, guarded by four men-servants but with her chest so exposed to the bitter night wind that she died from inflarnation of the lungs a foi tnight later. Most people take better care of their jewels and money than of their lungs or liver. Why? Jewels are pretty thing, to be sure; but life can be passed very com- fortably without ever possessing enything of the kind. Money one must have—at least a little of it—though to run out of it utterly does not necessarily mean im- mediate death,like the cessation of action in one's lungs or I iver.Verily,men think all men mortal but themselves. "In November 1895," writes Mr. Robert Lee; of Hainton Post Office, near Lincoln, in a letter dated February 22nd, 1898, • I began to get weak and low. My appetite was poor and the little food late gave me a deal of pain about the chest. I was unable to sleep well at I night, and in the morning felt tired and ex- hausted. My feat became swollen and painful, and a tenclAncy to gout manifested itself. I felt low-spirited, and fearc I I was breaking up. I remained in this miser- able condition for months, growing weaker daily,until began to use Mother Seigel's Syrup, a medicine of which I had heard much praise from friends. Its cura- tive power soon became apparent, and once more I , could eat well, the food agreeing with me. Continuing I to use the Syrup, my condition improved from week to 1 week, and it was not long before the gouty symptoms ; entirely disappeared, and my usual good health was I restored."</p>
        <p>This is a grand showing for a man of 79—Mr. Lee's age when this letter was written. It is surpassed, how, ever, by Mr. Lee's next letter, dated July 29th, 1901, I when Mr. Lee was 85,wherein he says: '1 have had no further trouble with my digestion, and feel as well as ever I did in my life." Pedide with gouty symptoms will do well to look to themselves. What cured Mr. Lee and 'keeps him well, will cure them and keep them well also. Gout is not an ailment that stands alone. It is a member of a dangerous combination, which may include affections of the heart, skin, nerves, brain, bladder and kidneys.</p>
        <p>A BURIAL SOCIETY'S SUCCESS. At the annual meeting of the Blackburn Philanthropic Burial Society, ,which,boasts of a quarter of million members, the President reported that it had been every eventful year, but there was oaly one dark spot, on the balance sheet, viz., law expenses £4,500: Notwithstand- ing that, they bad made a profit of nearly £23,000, which was nothing to be ashamea of. The accounts were passed with enthusiasm,</p>
      </div>
