      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>THE appearance of that unique little '' Diary rmi Dirv. panlon " Issued by GEO. G. BCSSEY Ft CO., for the use of Cricketers (which may be obtained from Sports Dealers, Boolcsellers.or Railway Bookstalls for 6d.), reminds us of the coming season. in anticipation of which GEO. G. BLTBSEY &amp; Co. have prepared a•• record" stock of Bate. We are told that the timter they hold for bat making alone exceeds 1.000 tons. •• Glorious Cricket follows the British Flag everywhere." (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>First Division. SATURDAY'S RESULTS AT A GLANCE.</p>
        <p>*Salford *Bradford lO Hull_</p>
        <p>--- *Hull Kingston Rovers.. 12 Hunslet 0 *Leeds 4 Halifax Widnes 7 *St. Helens 2</p>
        <p>*ii.;—Corn 6 Leigh *Oldham _ Wakefield _ . 0</p>
        <p>*gay— 7 Broughton Rangers 2 *Wigan l6 Warrington ____ _</p>
        <p>Warrington v. Wigan.</p>
        <p>The visit of Warrington to Wigan was one fraught with great importance for both clubs. Defeat put either team out of the running for the South-West Lancashire Cup,and unfortunately defeat was Warrington's portion. A lot of interest was taken in the contest, and quite 11,000 people assembled at Central Park, Wigan. War- lington played their selected team, awl there was only one change in the home ranks, Carey taking the place of Hector at centre. A strong wind prevailed and it had a marked effect on the play. Teams and officials : Warrington : Hallam, back ; Kenyon, Brooks, Isher- wood and Fish, three-quarter backs; Davies and Bate half-backs; Boardman, Thomas, Shugars, Cook, Belton, Naylor, Swift and Jolley, forwards. Wigan : Mason, back; Leytham, Carey, Harris and Green, three-quarter backs; Thomas and Hopkins, half- backs ; Brown, Barton, Tyrer, Osborne, Collier, Vickers, Whitaker, and Watkins forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Bruclishaw, Stockport. Touch judges Messrs. Davies' Flixton, and Jones, Runcorn. Warrington won the toss, and Brown started against the wind, Isherwood replying strongly. Penalties against Warrington, however, caused them to fall back, and the line was hotly assailed.Halla.m checking Thomas near the line. Warrington by the aid of short rushes carried the game to beyond the centre field,where Brooks tried to get away, but was held. Wigan again return6l to the attack, and Leytham had a futile shot at goal, whilst Fish ably dealt with a movement of the Wigan backs, Green being forced into touch before he could make any headway. Bate and Davies got away and Wigan were compelled to fall back beyond the centre line, but the respite for Warrington was not sustained, for Hallam failing to take a high kick, the Warrington goal was in jeopardy once more. The visitors had by no means shown their form, and Leytham almost got in from a pass by Carey. Kenyon again proved a barrier, hut success attended the Wiganers near quarter time,for Mason kicked a penalty goal. Encouraged by this success Wigan still made strong play near the Warrington line and Thomas opened out the game in the direction of Green, but the Wigan favourite could not escape the vigilance of Fish, and a safe tackle saved the situation near the corner. An encouraging cheer greeted retty combination between Bate,lsherwood, and Fish, but the Warrington flier did not pass Green,and thus the compli- ment was exchanged in the space of two minutes,the spec- tators cheering themselves hoarse when Fish was pulled up. Warrington improved as-the game proceeded, and brilliant combination by the Wire-pullers " was applauded. Bate handing on to Isherwood, the centre made an opening for Fish, who shot past Green like a flash, and Mason being easily defeated the Warring- tonian scored at the corner. The goal kick was a failure. Rain now fell in torrents, but it did not mar the exhibition, and clever work by the Wigan forwards caused Warrington to fall back. In saving a rush Board- man was badly hurt and retired, taking no further part in the contest. With their forwards weakened, the 'visitors were rushed on their lines, and Hopkins and Thomas were afforded opportunities galore. From a movement by the Wigan half-backs the ball travelled across to Leytham, who dribbled over and Wigan thus bad the lead once more. Warrington got dangerously near after this and after Bate had almost dodged over Kenyon was pulled up at the corner. Wigan rallied once more, and Leytham. tricking several players scored another smart try Half-time : Wigan, eight Points; Warrington, three points. The attendance had been largely augmented when the interval arrived, and Wigan with the assistance of the wind soon attacked. Fish, however, changed the scene, and punting across to the open Isherwood took the ball over near the posts and Fish placed the sides on level terms once more. Davies, who had been hurt in the 'first half, having sustained a bad kick over the eye, now made an opening for Brooks, but the chance was not taken. Wigan pressed as a result of strong foraard work, and after Thomas had tried to make an opening for Greea, Leytham got over and scored near the posts for Masan to land a goal. In the closing stages of the game the play became rough, and Shugars was given marching orders. Green scored a further try for Wigan, and the end arrived with the score as follows : —Wigan, two goals four tries (16 points) ; Warrington one goal two tries (8 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY'S GAMES.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. , G. T. P. Widnes 3 2 12 St. Helens 0 1 3 Leigh 0 3 9 .Runcorn 0 0 0 'Warrington 1 414 Wigan 1 2 8</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " v. WIGAN "A." There was a large attendance at the Wilderspool enclosure on Saturday, to witness this match, which will certainly have an importanthearing on the issue of the championship. The first game had resulted in a victory for the Warringtonians, and it was no secret that the colliery men were bent on avenging that, unex- pected reverse.l3oth sides were representative,and there must have been quite 5,000 spectators present when Wigan, having won the toss, started a game of eighty minutes' duration, which was divided into four quarters, consequent upon the high wind which prevailed. The visitors were the first to become aggressive, bnt mainiy through the clever work of J. Davies and Berry the Wiganers were held at Bay. A smart movement by W. Bate led up to Warrington becoming dangerous, and Dickenson made adhold attempt to cross. Wigan were bard pressed in dealing with a dashing effort of the for- wards, led by Forster, but Sharrocks got clear, and found touch at centre. The visitors rushed the game to 'the other end, and Berry having his kick charged down,</p>
        <p>the first moiety Wigan led by a try. Turn- ing round, Warrington made strong play inside the Wigan quarters, and twice Harris made bold attempts to cross. The game rarely escaped the Wigan quarte:, and another essay by Harris had a futile ending. War- rington were driven out, only to regain the attack with renewed energy, but there was faulty passing, andWigan escaped. Dicicenson tried to break through, but without success; and The game was interesting to a degree. From a pack near the corner, Davies cleverly dodged over. Warrington pressed nail the interval, and, from a rush, in which several backs combined. Harris 'scored, and Dickenson landed a goal. Half-time : Warrington 1" A," eight points ; Wigan "A," three points. Turn- ing round INigan were soon aggressive, and Tresize pass- ing to Voisey, then player crossed, and added a goal. The sides were now on a level footing, and the excite- ment was great when Warrington worked their way to the opposite end. Sharrocks repulsed them, and the end of the third quarter arrived with the totals eight points each. With the wind in their favour, Warrington forced the pace, and after Harris had made a fine attempt to score, Berry took a pass from Davies,and dashed through the Wigan backs in fine style. This gave the locals the lead, and Wigan, showing signs of distress, the game rarely escaped their quarters, and near the close Berry scored his second try from a pass on the line, and War- rington thus won, a tine game by 14 points to eight points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
