      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY UNION.</p>
        <p>Challenge Cup Competition.</p>
        <p>DRAW FOR FIRST ROUND.</p>
        <p>The draw for the first round of the Northern Union Challenge Cup Competition took place at the Grosvenor Hotel, Manchester, on Monday, and resulted as fol- lows : Warrington V. Morecambe. Swinton v. Rochdale Hornets. Marvport v. Hull Kingston Rovers. St. fielens v. Rochdale Rangers. Batley v. Barrow. Broughton Rangers v. Runcorn. Halifax v. Dewsbury. Leigh v. Wigan. Hunslet v. Parton. Bradford v. Castleford. Hull v. Leigh Shamrocks. Keighley V. Salford. Widnes v. Wakefield Trinity. Leeds v. Ossett. Huddersfield v. York. Oldham v. Normanton. The ties will be played on March 4th on the ground of the first-named club in each instance. REPORTED PLAYERS—SHUGARS SUSPENDED._ _ ;</p>
        <p>- At a meeting of the Northern Union League in Man-i chester on Tuesday the players undermehtioned were reported for the alleged offences named, and dealt with as follows :—J. Pickles (Keighley), rough play at Roch- dale, suspended to March 4; W. Coleman (Warrington i A), n match against Oldham A, disputing referee's decision, sending off field sufficient punishment; F. H. Shugars (Warrington), match Wigan v. Warrington, rough play, suspended February 25; F. Gorman (Hull Kingston Rovers), Broughton Rangers v. Hull Kingston Hovers match, kicking, suspended March 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ZIO PRIZE COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>ZIO PRIZE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Births In 76 Great Towns.</p>
        <p>RESULT.</p>
        <p>Readers will recollect the Offer made recently of Prizes, amounting to £lO, for a skilled forecast of the Births in the Seventy-six Great Towns of this country during four weeks ending February 11th. We are now in a position to announce _ THE RESULT</p>
        <p>and all competitors will receive within a few days the names and addresses of the winners. We take this opportunity to call attention to another remarkable cure by Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people the manufacturers of which are giving these prizes. Mr. Henry Windmill, 19, High-street, Ivinghoe, near Tring, was brought so low from the effects of influenza that he could hardly stand. His head swam, and he shivered from head to foot. The heart, as in most cases of influenza, was seriously affected. The first box of pills gave him new strength. He felt the aches and pains leaving him. "Hope," he graphically said, "came with strength and restored ability to eat "—for his health and digestion bad been so shattered that food made him sicken. "Before I took Dr. Williams' pink pills," he said, "I was almost starved. My tongue was fevered and furred. My weak- ness was increased by frequent 'sweats.' I can now eat almost anything with keen relish: a contrast to the time before I took Dr. Williams' pink pills! Until I tried them I was in agony every meal-time. Sickness and pain always came on When eating, and my heart would violently palpitate, causing me much distress. "I soon realised that I was benefiting from the pills. Altogether I took about six boxes. I was rewarded by feeling that my health and vigour were restored. Very soon I was able to work again. "My brother's case was almost as striking anexample of what these wonderful pills can do. He, too, suffered terribly from the after-effects of influsnza,and was cured by Dr. Williams' pink pills. He has unbounded faith in them., Such a medicine cannot be too widely known." _ .</p>
        <p>The nthuber of births recorded by the Registrar- General in the four weeks indicated for this Competition was 35,513. Ten competitors gave the exact number, and they have each received by this time a Postal Order for £l. from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., Holborn- viaduct, London. This Company will always send, post free, a box of Dr. Williams' pink pills for two shillings and ninepence, or six for thirteen and nine ; but they can be bad at all medicine shops, awl will IA genuine if they bear Dr. Williams' name, Anwmia, bile, consump- tion, decline, eczema, fits, gout, heart disease, indiges- tion, kidney complaints, locomator ataxy, paralysis, rheumatism, sciatica. St. Vitus' dance, and ladies' ail- ments and weaknesses have been cured by them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ANOTHER FATALITY AT BOLD COLLIERY.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER FATALITY AT BOLD COLLIERY.</p>
        <p>Another fatal accident happened on Monday at the Bold Colliery, near St. Helens, which last month was a scene of a cage disaster, when five colliers were killed and thirteen injured.—James Flanagan and James Smart, haulage hands, were working at their usual place in No. 3 pit when without any warning an extensive fall of roof took place completely burying the two men. Assistafice was procured as quickly as possible, but they were dead when unearthed.</p>
      </div>
