      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 25, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 25, 1905.</p>
        <p>THE ROMANCE OF AN OMNIBUS,</p>
        <p>LADY HELEN FORBES (Author of " Rio Eminence," 4v.)</p>
        <p>It was one ot those dismal days in which Lon- don seems to revel, when the Spirit of Fog is hovering in the air and laughing at the' appre- hension caused by. his delay to come down and blot everything out into one comprehensive blur. Indoors nervous people fiddled with their gas burners and made excuses to remain indoors. Outside everyone, from the crossing sweeper blowing on his fingers, to , the huddled-up lady in her fur-lined brougham, looked resigned to misery. The hooves of the cabhorses had churned the wood pavement into mud, and the sidewalks were dank and slippery. This was a first prize specimen of our best of climates, " matchless for the complexion," the sort of day by which for- eigners judge " perfide Albion," under the sickly light of a gaslamp, with the obscurity of things without fogging their judgment. Such a foreigner took 'bus at Oxford Circus, signalling to the conductor with an ill-rolled umbrella, whilst he picked his way with deliber- ate dignity over the mud. Circumstances spoke in his clothes and shabby Homburg hat, but heredity in his manner, slow with an inheritance of generations from those who were accustomed to be waited for and waited on. As he stood on the step of the 'bus, it moved on with a jerk, and a young woman running up breathlessly, caught at the handrail and, pulled out of equilibrium, slipped in the mud fringing the kerbstone. The foreigner's band was instantly at her service; no king's was ever extended to a curtseying debu- tante with greater grace, as he half lifted, half dragged her into safety. In panting confusion, she thanked him, and sat down in the first seat by the door with more suddenness than elegance. She was a pretty little thing, not very dressed and with a certain surface refinement, such as is often seen in very young girls of every class before years and knowledge have rubbed off the bloom; her 'face, with the wide eyes of a simple hearted child, was flushed and her hair was prettily dishevelled under her taw- dry hat. On her arm she carried a dilapidated little bag, and her shoes and gloves showed 'signs of hard wear, but she was withal neat and fresh, and brought with her a strange atmosphere of sweetness.</p>
        <p>They two were alone in the 'bus. The for- eigner, with deliberation, put a large roll of music tied up with blue ribbon on the seat opposite, sat down beside it, as if it were a cherished infant in a cradle, and with the calm of absolute uncon- sciousness, began to talk. Her flush deepened a little at his first words, but only with the natural timidity of a child. The foreign timbre of his voice, the unfamiliar periods, the "grand air," reassured her that he wtbs erring- unwittingly against the customs in which 'she had been brought up, and., discarding shyness, she answered his platitudes in the spirit in which they were meant. " But I don't think London is so very bad after all," she remarked, when after exhausting the weather he began on the diatribe of the alien against the world's metropolis. " Perhaps it isn't very beautiful, especially to. day, but still—" "Beautiful? Loudon beautiful Ali no, dim tie dieu Have you seen Paris f" • " No, never." " Or the sweet-smelling trees of Berlin, or the wide streets of Vienna, or the ghosts that stalk by daylight through. Rome? lladeitioisello, I tell you it is the scent, the space, the sentiment that make the beauty of cities." " Yet historical things," she said vaguely; " have taken place in London as well as in Rome; and 1 daresay i.eople can feel sentiment ever when they don't look like it." " But of what good is that, if they do not breathe it._ How benefits it we others that pass by P You, mademoiselle, have a nature of senti- ment —"</p>
        <p>" Oh, no, I don't think so," she " It isn't very nice to be sentimental," she add• ed, the sleeping Puritan in her nature lifting ith head.</p>
        <p>" It is the sauce of that bad dish we call life, the colour without u bleb the world would only be a photograph, the setting of the song that angels sing," he returned.</p>
        <p>ghe shook her head. "It does not help one to earn one's bread," she said with a dreary.' little lane!.</p>
        <p>- - " Ah, pardon ma! you speak to a musician," with a hand on the ribbon-tied roll, "and a musician must keen his heart young with the whispers of Parade. You, mademoiselle, do not know music ?"</p>
        <p>" I used to play the piano at home in the country," she admitted.___</p>
        <p>, " And that was joy ?" " It was useful," she said, with another little laugh, " for I am now a typewriter." At this point the 'bus stopped and he arose. " I descend hero," he remarked, "and made- ?"</p>
        <p>"Oh no, I live much further ; it is only my offices tleit are at the west end."</p>
        <p>" It is my academy that is in the west," he said, and raised his bat as be stepped off, still matelv, on to the kerbstone.</p>
        <p>" What a funny man!" she exclaimed half aloud, and forgot A Sunday and several week days passed, and then fate brought them together again. Her ex- pression on seeing him was one of evident and slightly unflattering surprise; his, ho*ever, that of one confronting the expected. " Everyday 1 thought I might see you once again in this Omnibus," he remarked. " Oh, hut I don't get away at this time of the any except Int Saturdays," she answered naively. " Saturdays 'r" he echoed, and added irrelev- antly, " I come at this time each day in the ,week."</p>
        <p>On this occasion the 'bus was full, and they hml 110 opportunity of conversing. There was :he woman with tha inevitable baby, to which she talked in strident tones in order to attract the ootiee of the others to the superiority of that whieli she bad produced ; there was the elderly elrk in it shiny top hat, with a face showing even mere wear than his clothes ; there was the impudent-looking shop-girl with her curly fringe and impossible bat ; there were a couple of boys -naittir oranges between aimless hicks at each other's shins ; there was the shabby genteel 'Tirader of uncertain age, trying to pretend that she was not clasping is brown paper parcel.; there was the representative of the' back row in the Omni., redolent of a cheap scent and jingling with meretricMus ornaments. These types, which repeat themselves to satiety to. the habitual tra- veller in omnibusmes, climbed over the toes of the little typewriter and drove their elbows into the unchanging face of the foreigner ; and when the omnibus stopped and emptied itself out into the tottring street., their counter Parts only took their places, so that the two who had met by accident, or the workings of Kismet, were its far apart as it the width of the city were between them. The seeker after adventures would do well not to frequent lire metropolitan omnibus, for therein the exciteMents of lift are few and far between, and its commonplaces are unending. Saturdays ,aine and Saturdays went all through the, dreary London winter, and at first the little typewriter and the foreigner often travelled home in differ- ent '1)12,.50,4, but sifter one or two MOTO meetings, they. left the matter ito lonuer to uncertain w„„ her first assignation, and dm did not recognise it its such. When one chilly February day • tat offered to tithe her home, she accepted with alacrity. She knew of nothing to hide in .1 he poor little rooms which she and an in- valid sister caked by that endearing name,- and she was amused by his elaborate manner, so in- emtgrucnis with the circumstances of dheir meet- ing. " Styli a funny man !" she nred ber sister, " you will' be entertained with him," and lit- tered herself on obtaining so exquisite an enjoy- ment for the invaliit latiall expense. kite ti valid herself, aeenstomed to hark on at life from di hew., item. otly scented it romance, but Inept the idea to herself. " %%Asia d." remarked the Foreiuncr, playing 11,0 wile allotted him by the little typewriter to ...conscious perfection, to see the home of is dower."</p>
        <p>" 1 arn armlet that flowers don't grow tnnol. Jr, cramped lodgings," she observed, eontrollg 4wile, and the invalid sister felt that her coil- .</p>
        <p>, -Mitre as to a romance was correct. The For- •krner seemed unaware of her presence. " Flowers grow even in the desert sometime," rejoined seriously. "1. love flowers," she aid. irrelevantly, " hut :1 I see as a rule are in the baskets of the flowel ! at Oxford Circus." " Why do you not walk in the parks f" he ig'gested. " I have no one to walk with," escaped her, and following it a fine crimsoning, for her words seemed to her to savour of the hint she would not Inc worlds have given. "Let that trouble von no more. and permit me</p>
        <p>to be your escort," he returned, with his air of</p>
        <p>ancien rEgime. A: Court curtsey was the 014 .iiituble reply, and she did not know how to nude:</p>
        <p>g me. " Oh, I did not mean that !" she exclaimed iu distress. " I did," ho said with firmness. The invalid smiled mysteriously. Her romance was sprouting vigorously: ,</p>
        <p>The invalid's.;7iews cif life were nnobsenred by lie contemplation of her own persomdity. be said of her that she knew nothing o: ha world, because she had no bodily experiew: the tussle of tha streets, and had scarcely short life met her fellow men and wont.... '.1.0 to face. But in the. Buddhist creed it is I,</p>
        <p>..tm:linumit from the actual world that gives ha spirit the powers of divination and prophee, ',al the instincts of the invalid, though itufoumt d on knowledge, were rarely wrong. The little typewriter, though she was one e ,10 great sisterhood of workers, was so inmate', ',at she took much longer than a more wiper ~eed woman would have done to awake to U.</p>
        <p>element in her life. For an incredibly sidle she honestly thought that their weel.: •leclings in the omnibussev mune ((bent is inec, and even when she knew that they no erred by studious arrangement, she did ie.. :lasi) the significance of it. It was not (ill h, :une for her every Sunday that it dawned up, •,,,r that they two were making of Regent's no: t Garden of Eden.</p>
        <p>" But are you not afraid, knowing so little 0 ?" be asked her, after he had first spoken t</p>
        <p>, r of love. -" Are you not afraid tool' 'for you know v, r: We of WC,' she answered, yet trembling shock, for she was unused to look through 11. of Paradise, and the vision which she NV, cr toed to her incomparably fair and dazzling. " I P What have I to fear from my flower he desert P But what am IF A voice in lA. larkness, a wind in the night, a star that shoot, !cross the sky; you know not whence I come 311,1 where I go. You do not even know my uatiini. wa the very name I have told you is not nij •wn." . " Tell me," she whispered tremulously. Ti ,vadiness of love to take alarm was upon her. "I am a Russian," he said with sudden wonted simplicity, " and my name is only too we) Not perhaps to you, fair child, but ti -4cret Police of his Majesty the Czar have bee,. wpon its track for Years."</p>
        <p>"The police ?" she gasped "I am a Nihilist and proscribed," be au .wered, " if what I will whisper in your ear we ;,liown. I should be now in Siberia."</p>
        <p>" Oh, but isn't that very wicked and dread. iil?" site cried desperately, I suppose you Itink everybody ought to be blown up. But wouldn't that be very wicked?" Her vocabu- ury was not large, nor her ideas clear. " If thy hand offend thee, cut it off," he said " But not somebody else's hand ?" she ex- imed.</p>
        <p>.• I cannot talk to you of these things, dear •:hild," he went on tenderly, " you are so young ,sd so English, and can know nothing of the ,dglit in which we live. How can you imagine ii lnid where the neighbour that sits at your board, the bi other that gnaw up with you, the sweet!. heart you have kissed, the child you have cher- ished, may be a spy to hand you over to death and worse than death P Where all friendship, all kindliness, all love, is overshadowed with be- trayal?" " Oh, stop !" she cried, "it sounds so horri- ble !"</p>
        <p>"It is what makes that which you shrill': from," he returned. "To free our land from this aightmare by whatever means, is the dream the Nihilist you think t4O wicked." " Please," she pleaded, in her homely words, "try and forget all about that. I—l will try to help you if you will let me. Make England your home, and forget the dreadfulness of Russia." "There you ask what is impossible." he ex• ,•laimed with a sudden overclouding, "to nil Russia is breath of life, core of soul ; to abaudon !ler and her misgrable people, impossible. If el. ,•alls, I must olley—even if it rends my hear from my body." "Then you do not love me ?" the woman's de</p>
        <p>notion. " I love you truly," he answered gravely, " but, Russia is my mother." "But a man must leave his father and hi, mther, and cleave to his—" _ _ _</p>
        <p>She faltered and was silent. "She is more than that, queen, goddess, aim of life, and end of reward. Child, you do not knou what his country is to a patriot." The sun had gone out of her sky, and the gates- ,4 Paradise were closed. She did not understand</p>
        <p>him and was unversed in the arts of coquetry : •.vbile she was with him, she appeared content. .md he supposed that she understood and was not mwardly grieving. It was on the way home that -ho went crying, and to her invalid, sister that she poured out the pain and disillusionment that had stricken her. "He is not what I fancied him," she sobbed. on her knees by the other's couch, "and I think mind that most of all." The. invalid for a while was at fault. This was scarcely her idea of romance, that it should die at .03 very birth. h 1 think he must* be a poor creature," she said at length,</p>
        <p>"I don7t know," said the other, sitting back an her heels. "Oh, can this be Love? For it is, I don't think love is so vetry pleasant after :al, and I thought it was Tleaven." But the revulsion came next day when lie pante back and brought a large bunch of violets as a peace-offering. " You like these flowers ?" he asked tentative- you know their meaning ?" " I love them," she cried with her face, as fresh as they, plunged into their fragrant dewi- ness, "but I didn't know they meant anything, except that they are beautiful." "They mean Eternal Constancy," he said in his solemn way. She looked at him half whimsically. " Ah ? is that to your country or to me 4" she asked. But she was happy again and smiling. The invalid watched from her couch and she filo mailed. She was at ease now about the little 'sister, to whom she • felt in some measure con- -trained to act as a guardian angel; the romance would do the little one no harm ; it was het imagination, not her heart, that was touched. So the days passed, and the question of creeds and partisans did not crop up again to disturt their peacefulness. -.The little one WS happy. and left in the morning singing ; she came beet smiling at evening, and most days the Foreigine came back with her ; and they three would spent their evenings together. The invalid was con- tent ; her instinct told her that romance wa. evanescent and better, that it should be so. Sh did not take note of the unsatisfied, hungry leo' if the matt, and the restlessness of his tones, and would not have eared if she had ; it was her lilt:, sister that concerned her. It was the dream of Spring, and when &amp;unmet ?lame there was an awakening. One day his step was heavy as he came up flu narrow stairs leading to their rooms, and the $1 onanity of his face was deepened into gloom. Ye his manner was as elaborate and un-English ttver ; he bowed to the sisters and it should inn</p>
        <p>.„.. heen a three-cornered hat pressed to his heart it, ..tead of bis shabby black Homburg. The Merit side roll of music in his bnd should have been tng stidc with ta‘sels barg:ng. " I TIM afLil Ia V lii ba%c had bad news," veil tared the scale 1. ...enthuse trouble. "Good or bad, I have had news," he said slowly. " I told you that I belonged to a eoret society, belonged to it body and soul. I hare re- ceived a summons. I must ifo. Choice is not mine."</p>
        <p>Then be turned to the little oue arid Lis voice broke.</p>
        <p>" Child," he said, " I have loved you much, much. But this is the end."</p>
        <p>• She cried from sympathy, and thought her heart was broken,, though in truth it WM, not touched. The invoilid breathed again, tor hc knew that the end lad best come in it gentle rain of healing tears. She turned away tel face and silently thanked Heaven, while the little MCP 'tears ran down her lover's coat, and lie gazed and gazed on her like one who has lost Paradise.</p>
        <p>_ " We met through fate, and by fate we are parted," he said; " it may be we shall meet again, but I dare not confide we shall. And so it would be better to let me pass front your life, little English flower ; you were not born to grow where the four winds of heaven are blowing. Some other than I shall gather you, my violet; and it will be better than this. You will forger,. but I shall remember." Of course she asseverated again and again, and each time more tearfully, but the in 1,4 Rifled on her coach. Yet when he was gone, her sym- pathy was the tenderest and oot ft nord of IR, thoughts either then or thereafter came ia.ar the little one. rhi, is the least loving sous C:111 ), tI at they should not disturb the luitury c. grief. We little know the risks we may run with hearts, for a careless word, .a want of sympathy at the right moment may do more to break them than the grief I .elf, and a heart cin only break once. But the b ;•• one was blessed, for though sho was a wore. • • was shielded from the rough world by th, . ...t wisdom of her sister, so the little</p>
        <p>heart was only softened by sorrow and not wounded, and this was well, for the wounds of the heart leave scars for over. The little one was to go and sec her lover off by the boat, which started from Southampton ; she scarcely knew , where he was going, only that he would never return, and as she went to the station to meet him, she bought him a hole Minch of violets to wear in his coat. This Was an • innocent lie on her part, for in six mouths she . would forget him ; but this she could not know. It is little things like these which make the toy tragedies of this little world. . . _</p>
        <p>Whenshe met him on the platform, lie seemed troubled and distrait, and this softened the part- ing to her. When lie. was talking his set Eng- lish with the " grand air," it seemed to her some- times • that he could not love her very much after all ; but when the elaboration of his. manners seemed all worn away, and he whispered to her in broken snatches of his own language, which she could not understand, though she could very well guess their meaning, she was comforted again and turned from her doubt.</p>
        <p>• Ou the quay she heard a word that frightened her.</p>
        <p>" They say that there's a well-known Russian conspirator going by this boat," said a careless voice at her elbow, " and there are a lot of de- tectives in plain clothes on the watch to msb Good business too! to get rid of the vermin." . _</p>
        <p>She looked up at her lover trembling, but lit; seemed not to have Lena</p>
        <p>There was a crush on the quay, and she was pushed a little aside. So it came about that their parting was hurried and almost wordless. Once she tried to speak, to warn, than she yielded to the pressure of the crowd, and seemed to he Swallowed up by it. Only one glimpse did she get of the launch whieli took the passengers out to the steamer. She „saw him stand straining his eyes towards her add tried to flutter her hand- kerchief, but the crowd pressed her hands to her side. Then she saw a stranger come up and tap him on the shoulder, and as he turned away, be gave one last glance at the quay, took her violets 4.roni his buttonhole and lifted them to his lips.</p>
