      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>EVERTON ELECTION.</head>
        <p>EVERTON ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Attack on a Tory Stronghold</p>
        <p>REDUCED MAJORITY.</p>
        <p>The election in the Everton Division of Liverpool to fill the vacancy caused by the retirement of Sir J. A. Wißox (C) resulted on Wednesday as under : Mr. Harmood Banner (C) 3,854 Mr. Hanbury Aggs (L) 9,543</p>
        <p>Majority 1,543 The division has been only twice previously con- tested, in 1885 and 1892, when the figures were :</p>
        <p>1885. 1892. E. Whitley (C) F. Davies (L) 2,063 P. W. Atkin (L) ... 2,165 Majority (C)</p>
        <p>It will be seen that the Conservatives have done worse and the Liberals better than at any preceeding election in the division. Since 1900 there have been three contested by- elections in Liverpool, and it may be interesting to give the general result thus : Previous Election. By-election. L. C. L. C. East Toxteth 1,706 ... 3,628 ... 3,233 ... 3,610 West Derby 1,686 ... 4,622 ... 3,251 ... 5,455 Everton</p>
        <p>Total 5,557 12,204 9,027 12,919 While the aggregate Liberal vote has increased by about 3,500, the Tory vote has increased by only about 700. THE NEW MEMBER.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. S. Harmood Banner, the new member, is 'principal of a Liverpool firm of accountants, chairman of the Finance Committee of the Liverpool Corporation, a magistrate, and Deputy-Lieutenant for Cheshire. He is also chairman of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>WOMEN WITH SORE BACKS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN WITH SORE BACKS.</p>
        <p>Severe, Ulcers Healed by Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>When one lain a depressed state of health the skin itself not infrequently begins to suffer. Small sores break out in a mysterious way, and these, unless checked, may develop into severe ulcers. A case of ulcerated back just reported from Manchester is useful as; illus- trating the wonderful healing %eine of Zam-Buk. Mr. J. Mansor of 44, Elias-street, Miles Platting, Manchester, says :—" lam glad to be able to send you our wonderful experience with Zam-Bilk. For six months my wife suffered from an, ulcerated back, for which she had to be attended by a doctor. Notwith- standing all we did, she constantly suffered from a burn- ing pain, and night after night was scarcely able to obtain any sleep. We spent a lot of money trying to cure her. Ono night my wife showed me a sample box of Zam-Buk which she had obtained. I advised her to try it. We were astonished how very soothing the application of Zam-Buk proved. It seemed to stop all the pain at one, and prevent the ulcers spreading. By and by the places healed up, and Zam- Buk has now been the means of effecting a complete cure. Her back is as healthy as it possibly could be." Mr. Manser testifies to the healing virtues a Zam-Buk from his own experience, too. He adds: "I bruised my leg very badly while at work, and it began to fester and turn to blood-poisoning. Zam-Buk quickly stopped the festering and lessened the inflammation and pain. Now new skin has completely formed over the injury and there is no trace of it left. A friend of mine has also been completely cured of barber's itch through using Zam-Buk. We shall never be without it in our house."</p>
        <p>Lam-Buk stands alone as the best application for ulcers, bad legs, diseased ankles, sore backs, poisoned or festering sores, piles, eczema, psoriasis, scalp irrita- tion, erysipelas, barber's rash, abscesses, boils, pimples, ringworm, running sores, sore breasts, chilblains, raw, chapped hands, sore faces and lips, chafed skin, stiffness in joints or muscles, cuts, bruises, burns, scalds,punctured or lacerated wounds, scratches, sprains, strains, swell- ings, and all injuries to, or diseases of the skin. All chemists sell Zam-Buk in Is. V.A. and 2s. 9d. boxes (2s. 9d. size contains nearly four times as much as the Is. lid.), or it may jae obtained post free from the Zarn-Buk Co., Red dross-street, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>- LIM • • .• . .• • . • .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>Ma. ROTCH, of the Blue Hill Observatory, near Boston, U.S., and Mr. Casella,. of London, have brought out an instrument for determining the true velocity and direction of the wind at sea relatively to the ships in motion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>LONG-FOCUS MICROSCOPE.</head>
        <p>A LONG-FOCUS MICROSCOPE.</p>
        <p>The ordinary microscope does not admit of watching the free natural life of insects, because they are too near the eye. Dr. De Gespartz, of the University of Naples, has, therefore, invented a long-fo'cus microscope or "bioscope," which reveals the hitherto unseen romance of creeping things, the loves and hates of the gregarious ant, or the cunning little ways of that queer fellow, the spider.</p>
        <p>A "HYTHER" SCALE.</p>
        <p>Captain W. F. Tylor, F.R.Met.S., regards temperature and humidity as the leading factors in our sensation of comfort from a climate. He has formed a "hyther " scale (a new word compounded from hygrometer and thermometer) to estimate this comfort. A pleasant day is the zero or "0" of the scale, and an insufferably oppressive one is 10deg. Barometric pressure and electrical conditions pro- bably influence the sensation.</p>
        <p>WHY VARNISH' TURNS WHITE.</p>
        <p>It has often been wondered why paint and var- nish turn white for a while after a heavy rain- storm. This is probably due to molecules of mois- ture being driven into the minute divisions of the paint or varnish, and thus giving them the colour that we see on foam on the water. The natural colour of the paint or varnish can be quickly restored by rubbing on a little fine oil, which takes the place of the vapour in the paint.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WHY AIRSHIPS FAIL.</head>
        <p>WHY AIRSHIPS FAIL.</p>
        <p>Baldwin, the American aeronaut, who was most successful at the St. Louis Exhibition, says that aeronauts sometimes fail in their trials of airships because they are unable to restore their balance when the ship lurches. He is an old gymnast, and used to balancing on the tight-rope. He believes that the motor balloon, or airship, may become a form of sport like yachting, but not an ordinary vehicle. The aeroplane, he thinks, is destined to come into regular use, and he intends to work at that in future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>DURABILITY IN TIMBER.</head>
        <p>DURABILITY IN TIMBER.</p>
        <p>Some interesting experiments have been made to ascertain which wood lasts the longest. It was found that birch and aspen decayed in three years, willow and chestnut in four years, maple and red beech in five years, and elm and ash in seven years. Oak, Scottish fir, and Weymouth pine decayed, to the depth of half-an-inch in seven years; larch and juniper were uninjured at the aid of the seven years. In situations so free from moisture that they may be practically called dry, the durability of timber is unlimited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>VALUE OF CHEAP OZONE.</head>
        <p>THE VALUE OF CHEAP OZONE.</p>
        <p>Ozone is not a common substance in nature, but it can be produced, says Science Siftings, by a surprising variety of forces. One agent is heat and another is the use of various chemicals. Ozone may be formed by electrolysis by the influence of a silent electric discharge and by the newly-discovered radio-active substances. The more familiar uses of ozone are the sterilisation of air, water, food, and liquids, and the oxydation and reduction of colouring substances, perfumes, itc. The produc- tion of cheap ozone by more economical methods than those hitherto employed may open a wide field for its use in metallurgy.</p>
        <p>HEAT PHENOMENA IN PLANTS.</p>
        <p>_The fast has been slowly brought out that plants,</p>
        <p>like animals, generate a certain degree of heat, by their life processes, although the amount is rela- tively very small. It has been noted that the sap of trees does not freeze in winter like other liquids, that the interior of fibrous plants is sensibly warmer than the outside air in cold weather, and that snow melts more rapidly at the foot of living trees than at the foot of dead ones. A more careful study has lately shewia M. Dutrochet that certain vege- tables,with temperature independent of that of the atmosphere, grow warmer until the middle of the day, then become cooler as the sun declines. Cer- tain plants shew increase of temperature in flower- ing, the rise being half a degree in one case observed, while the stalk of the arum of Italy may have a daily increase of lOideg., and the stalk of one plant has been known to be as warm as 43deg. Cent., in an atmosphere of only 21deg.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>TELEGRAPH ANTICIPATED.</head>
        <p>TELEGRAPH ANTICIPATED.</p>
        <p>The subjoined extract has been, it seems, an interesting anticipation of one of the most beautiful of modern inventions, and may be found, says Science SOings, in a work entitled "Travels During 'the Years 1787-1789 in the Kingdom of France," by Arthur Young : -"In electricity, M: Lomond has made a remarkable discovery: you write two or three words on a paper; he takes it with him into ‘a room, and turns a machine enclosed in a cylindrical case, at the top of which is an electro- meter, a Sinai!, fine, pith ball; a wire connects with a similar cylinder and electrometer On a distant apartment; and his wife, by remarking the corre- sponding motions of the ball, writes down the words they indicate, from which it appears that he has formed an alphabet of motions. As the length of the wire makes no difference in the effect, a correspondence might be carried on at any distance, within and without a besieged town, for instance; or, for a purpose more worthy and more harmless, between two lovers prohibited or prevented from any better connection. 'L</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>STUDIES IN WEATHER PREDICTION.</head>
        <p>STUDIES IN WEATHER PREDICTION.</p>
        <p>Colonel H. E. Ratvson, C. 13., F.R.M.S., has addressed the Aeronautical 'lnstitute on "Anti- cyclones as an Aid to Weather Prediction, far in advance, and their importance In Aerial Navigation." The colonel is well-known among meterologists as having made a prolonged study of the movements and significance of anticyclones. These large high- pressure areas move slowly over the earth's surface, and Colonel Rawson shewed that, given the law of their m9vetnents, it would become comparatively easy to forecast the character of the probable weather for long periods in advance. Incidentally Colonel Rawson made an announcement which will be new to meteorologists, and of much interest, whatever may be the explanation. It was derived from an analysis of the Greenwich observations since 1841, which shew that for a certain long period of years the barometer at Greenwich is highest when the moon is in southern declination, and to this period follows another when the read- ings are highest when the moon is in northern declination—the climates of the two cycles differing considerably. The colonel did not anticipate this effect necessarily to lunar influence, but it certainly corresponded to lunar periods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WANTED-COLD LIGHT.</head>
        <p>WANTED-COLD LIGHT.</p>
        <p>Anyone who has watched fireflies and glow-worms has been face to face with one of the greatest mysteries of nature. How the light is produced is still obscure, in spite of all the investigations that have been carried on. The nature of the light is I equally puzzling, for heat rays and chemical rays are practically absent, the spectroscope shewing that the intensity of the light is mainly in the green. Such alight as this would be a boon in our homes, but how to produce it—that is the question. Electricity presents the matter thus: Problems which call for a solution present them- selves in the marvellous cold light given out by various insects and glow-worms. That is the idea at present—to produce cold light. As the saying goes: "It is easier said than done." But the fact of the matter is that considerable has been done. A review of the situation with regard to lighting in general discloses the fact that progress has not been confined to the application of electricity for light- big, but remarkable advances have been made in the use of oil and gas in connection with rare oxides, such as the Welsbach mantle, for the purpose of producing a more brilliant and more efficient source of illumination. So the list goes on increasing from the torch to the candle, from the oil lamp to gas, iroin the incandescent lamp to the vacuum tube. Here we begin to meet with the. possibilities of cold light, or heatless light, as some prefer to call it. All the means of illumination above quoted are heat producers, and the light is only a secondary effect. A gas jet wastes about 99 per cent, of the latent energy of the illuminant in heat: only 1 per cent. reaches the eye. Ln incandescent lamp wastes 97 per cent, in beat and the arc lamp, the Most ,efficient of all known sources of artifi..al light, dissipates 90 per cent, of the energy of the current in beat, and only 10 per cent. appears t.• 3 light. This means that in electric- light plants supplying power to incandescent lamps, for every 1001 h. of coal burnt in the boiler from 21b. to 31b. appear as light. Surely the :::.tonomy of nature is greater in the light-producing insects, who give out enormous quantities of light relatively, without impairing their vitality or activity to any noticeable extent: and the light they produce is the kind sought for so cold light. light.</p>
      </div>
