        <p>endeavoured to pursue a different policy, but has not had the strength for it, and has not had the backing in the Cabinet. He has put aside the old ascendency ideas,_.. but when it came to a real tussle with their champions he had not the courage to say "Here we part definitely; I prefer to govern Ireland ny finding a medium of that popular consent which our system has hitherto lacked. If you will not agree, then I shall resign, and the country shall choose between us." With his party already dis- tracted by the fiscal question, Mr. WxxollAm has no relish for another conflict, and his striving to patch up his differences with the ascendancy faction. As for the Nationalists, they scoff, or Mr. HEALY scoffed at devolution. But we should have been very much surprised if they had rejected the overtures Sir ANTONY MASJDONNELL was ready to make. The situation has turned out badly for the Government, but it may have the ad- vantage of preparing the country for the extension of local control in Ireland which is bound to change the traditional character of Dublin Castle.</p>
        <p>Though the non-appearance of Mr. Charles Fenwick, MT., at Tuesday's Liberal-Labour demonstration, was a source of keen disappoint- ment to. the audience, they nevertheless went home well-satisfied with the proceedings. It was an excellent meeting in every respect. The speeches of Mr. Crosficli, Mr. Samuelson, and Mr. Howes revealed these politicians to be in their best form, and the firm line they toot c on the topics of the day gave general satisfaction. Mr. Crosfield went somewhat out of his way to reply to anonymous critics. In our view he is too courteous to these individuals, who are far from meriting serious consideration on account of the animadversions they may see fit to make on the Liberal candidate's public acts. The very fact that the hostile criticism comes from the opposite camp ought to be enough to condemn it as unworthy of a reply. Friendly critics are always welcomed and it is often an advantage to discuss matters with them, but malicious fault-finders are best left alone. The individuals who have lately been taking every opportunity to belittle Mr. Crosfiekt's words • and actions are of the type calling for contempt rather than courtesy. They are undeservingly honoured by Mr. Crosfield's attention.</p>
        <p>We were pleased to note that Mr. Crosfield replied to one of the very few objections which can, with any show of reason, be urged against him by the more advanced socialistic Labour men. That they should prefer a candidate of their own is a natural desire with which we can sympathise, and towards the gratification of which we shall be pre- pared to assist at the proper time. But the prospects of direct representation in the next Parliament are nil, and it behoves Labour men generally to get what they can from the candidate who is pledged to oppose—and will oppose—class legislation, and tampering with Free Trade and the privileges of the Trades Unions. To dub Mr. Crosfield a capitalist is to paint him rather black in the workman's eyes, and the charge, in its political' sense, is absolutely unde- served. Mr. Crosfield is too earnest a student of social questions and too ardent a reformer over to belong to the capitalist class—the class which fattens on Protection aril the fostering of in- dustrial combines. As he remarked on Tuesday, if this was the trend of their thought, "they mis- judged the kind of education on which he had been ! brought up." Mr. Crosfield is the sincerest of Liberals and, as was evident, has ever before him that grand old maxim of John Locke's " the end of Government is the good of mankind."</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuelson, at the close of his address, recommended Mr. Crosfield to the workers in terms which are, we venture to think, worthy of emphasis. "Did they ever know," said he, "of ths Tories helping to put a workingman into Parliament? Never. They might as well try to change the spots of the leopard. Where the Liberal candidate was prepared to support Labour representation and an extension of Labour repre- sentation—like Mr. Crosfield was—they could not do better than return him. In conclusion he sincerely hoped they would be suczessful in returning a worthy eon of a worthy site as their representative in the House of Commons." The audience heartily said " Hear, hear " to these sentiments, and we are sure they will be endorsed by all the wisest progressives in Warrington.</p>
        <p>"When will the immorality of it end ? " was a pertinent question put by Mr. Howes in alluding to the fiscal farce kept up by Mr. 1141 four and Mr. Chamberlain. Mr. Balfour professes to have "an open mind," but as the speaker pointed out it is too late in the day for statesmen to have open minds on so important a question. Mr. Balfour fears that Mr. Chamberlain's scheme may increase the cost of living, but he hopes that it will not. Therefore he keeps "an open mind" rather than admit himself in agreement with the "missionary •of Empire's views. The public are xearied of the political immorality of such dissimulation, and they have shown their disapproval at every by- election since Mr. Chamberlain declared for Pro- tection in July 1903. The number of bye-elections since then is 31, and all cf them have been re- markable for the increase in the Liberal vote,while in ten cases the seat was wrested from the Tories. In these 31 constituencies the Liberal poll has increased from 115,657 to 153,908, while the Conservative vote has been practically stationary,viz.,l32,7s9 at the previous elections and 133,102 at the by-elections. Taking the 43 by-elections since the close of the war we find that the Liberal poll has been swollen by 61,919 votes, while the Tory vote has actually decreased by 5,153. No matter bow the figures are examined they cannot be persuaded to admit of any deduction at all favourable to the Government, and if that heterogenous conglomera- tion of mis-ma Lagers had any self-respect it would resign. The day of its defeat cannot now be far off. A majority which is dwindling at the present rate will not take long to descend from the forties to zero and then—the nation will have its oppor- tunity</p>
        <p>The work of organizing the local Liberal forces proceeds with encouraging enthusiasm, and there is not the slightest doubt that when the general election does come it will find .he earnest sup- porters of the party fully preparad to do battle against the upholders of the policy of dear food and monopoly. In every quarter of the town intense interest is being manifested in the prepara- tions, and during the next few weeks the annual ward meetings foi the election of officers and the transaction of other business are to be held. The first of these gatherings, for the workers of St. Austin's ward, will take place on Monday evening in Thewlis-st. schools. Wednesday, March Bth will witness the annual meeting of the Liberal Association. The occasion is generally characterized by interesting addresses on national and local topics, and it is safe to predict a large muster of those who are anxious to further the progressive cause in Warrington.</p>
        <p>It speaks well for the enthusiasm of our local teachers and their desire for enlightenment on the various aspects of their wotk, that so many attended to hear the addresses from prominent educationists at the Technical Institute on Saturday. For the efficient working of our edu- cational system, a satisfactory understanding between the authority and its servants is one of the first essentials, and amicable relations have been the rule in Warrington up to the present. Occasion was taken to offer several friendly but candid criticisms of the scale of salaries, and these will doubtless receive consideration in due time. It will, we think, be goner. ally conceded that teachers have little cause to complain of their treatment at the hands of the Warrington Committee. As Mr. Jolley pointed out, probably not a single member of tbe profession is at all worse off through the operation of the Act</p>
        <p>here, while the majority have materially benefited. In return it behoves teachers to give their ablest and most loyal service, and if we judge their inten- tions aright those services will be rendered to the utmost of their power, and with a single eye to the welfare of the children committed to their care.:</p>
        <p>The iaddresses, which were listened to with interest arid pleasure, were timely and well con- sidered,and will repay tholghtful perusal by many outside the immediate circle to which they were addressed. Alderman Bennett put forth ievigorous plea for the instruction of the children in matters appertaining to local history and traditions, and we should like to see some of his suggestions acted upon. Nothing is more calculated to encourage a spirit of local patriotism than a knowledge of the careers and work of the great men who have reflected honour upon the town in the past, and a realization of the antiquity of the ' place. The Rev. Morley Stevenson's admirable address on "a teacher's ideals " set before his hearers what should he the true aim of all their endeavours—to get the children to live their lives in the best possible way. Dealing with the physical I training, he made it quite clear that the Warring- ton Committee is fully alive to the value of teaching in hygiene and temperance, and further he was I able to announce that Sir William Broadbent had 11 assured him that Warrington was not the one place he had recently mentioned as having put itself in opposition to the medical men's memorial. 1 Mr. A. J. Jolley rightly urged the importance of caution in the matter of educational expenditure, for the imposition of too heavy a burden upon the ratepayers would in all prob ability result in the reaction of sentiment to which he referred. By an instructive paper on "The working of the Edu.istion Act in Warrington." Mr. J. Moore Murray showed his thorough grasp of the detailed workings of the local scheme, and advanced several important suggestions. He is particularly anxious that there should be no "marking time" on the part of the scholars, but his proposal of more frequent promotions would undoubtedly require very careful weighing before a workable scheme could be evolved</p>
