        <p>The Irish debate THE IRISH MYSTERY. leaves us wondering</p>
        <p>whether the Cabinet will have courage to sanction the publication of the correspondence between Sir ANTON IVIACDONNELL and Mr. WYNDHAM. If the Under- Secretary were dismissed, he might seek to set himself right with public opinion by •giving the correspondence to the world. But there is evidently no desire to force him to this extremityi, His conduct has been called "indefensible," but the censure is really milder than the censure which was passed upon Sir FRANCIS YOUNGLIUSBAND for having exceeded his instructions in Tibet Sir. 'ANTONY MACDONNEL is supposed to have exceeded his instructions ; but unless we see the correspondence it will never be clear what the instructions really were. To the plain man Mr WYNDHAM'S explanation is simply bewildering' He held deep confabulation with Mr. Under- Secretary/about the Irish administration, but never suspected that Sir -ANTONY wanted to set up an elective body for the control of Irish finances on the Indian model. Mr. WYNDHAM never sus- pected this, he says, b.,cause he was " colossally ignorant of Indianiaffairs." So we are virtually asked to believe that the exact method of dealing with Irish finances was never discussed between these colleagues ; neither knew what was in the other's mind; but Sir Antony thought that his chief approved of his scheme. It was a series of "melancholy misunder- standings." The correspondence might show us exactly what it was. But shall we ever see those letters ? The Cabinet has been blamed for pub- lishing the Tibet correspondence, and may take the hint to use every endeavour to keep the other dark.</p>
        <p>Apart from the mystery, the debate is not very instructive. We may take it for granted that the Ulster Unionists will never forgive the Govern- ment or trust them again. But this has really been their temper ever since the passage of the Land Purchase Act. They were furious with Lord DUN- I nAvEN's Land Conference, because it brought about an understanding between the moderate Unionists and Mr. WILLIAM O'BRIEN. "We are sick of con- ciliation," said Mr. WILLIA.m MoonE with perfect candour. What the Ulster Unionists want is a Chief Secretary who will treat the Nationalists as enemies, and keep up the factious spirit that divides the two section of the population. This is the old a of "law and order." To do him justic t say that Mr. WYNDHAM hail</p>
      </div>
