        <p>PAGE</p>
        <p>--27 Women's Chat and Fashions. "My Greatest Success at Golf," by H. G Hutchinson. 3. "Romance of an Omnibus," by Lady Helen Forbes. Football. Science Notes. 6. Meeting of Teachers. Education Committee. Police News. 7. Billiards. Free Church News. Runcorn Rural Council. Coming Events. Under the auspices of the Warrington Women's Liberal Association, a social meeting will be held at the Liberal Club on Monday the 27th. Refreshments at 7 pm., and afterwards a paper on " Thrift " will be read by Miss Bessie Dutton. A special service will be held at Wycliffe Church to-morrow (Sunday) evening. The choir will render an anthem and Mrs. Pearson will give a solo. The subject of the sermon, by the Rev. John Yonge, will be " God, our Father." "Is Baptism essential to Salvation ? : or a Baptist minister's confession of faith " will be the Rev. H. Dunnico's subject at the Parr Hall to-morrow evening. A concert arranged by Mr. Harry Berrey (con- ductor and voice trainer to the Warrington Apollo Prize Choir) will be held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey street, on Monday evening. The programme is one of exceptional interest and there should be a large audience. In addition to the Apollo choir, the Lyric Prize Quartette, Mr. Harry Berrey ane several other vocalists will take part, and there will be humorous selections by Mr. Willie Milne. The annual meeting of the Borough of Warrington Incorporated Permanent Building Society will be held at the office of the manager(Mr. Thomas Sutton),Legh- street, on Wednesday evening. The final subscription concert of the season in con- nection with the Musical Society will be held in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening when a miscellaneous programme, mainly contributed by members of the Society, will be submitted. A Band of Hope festival will be held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening when the ceremony of crowning the Temperance Queen will be gone through. The juveniles will also submit a very attractive pro- gramme of songs, drills, dances, &amp;c. The annual dance in connection with St. Mary's, Latchford, will be held in the Parr Hall on Monday, March 6th. The annual general meeting of the shareholders of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Company Ltd., will be held at the Warrington offices on Thursday, March 9th.</p>
        <p>A public meeting in connection with the Knutsford Division Liberal Association will be held in the schoolroom adjoining the Church at Stockton Heath on Wednesday evening next. Mr. Henry Roberts will preside and the speakers will include Dr. John Massie of Oxford (hon. treasurer of the National Liberal Federation), Mr. A. J. King, the prospective candidate, and Mr. A. H. Crosfield.</p>
        <p>Warrington and the Ship Canal. It transpired that there had been a .aisunderstand ing when the litigation between traders and the Manchester Ship Canal Company in connection with the dredging of the river at Warrington came before the House of Lords on Thursday and the case was put back. Social Service League.</p>
        <p>- At the weekly meeting of this league held last even- ing the Rev. H. Minnie() presided over a large attend- ance. Mr. C. .E. Parker read a paper on " Land Nationalization" and the question was well debated. We hope to publish Mr. Parker's paper next week. "PREMIER" CYCLES at 7 guineas startling value.— _</p>
        <p>J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.— Advt. Mr. T. J. Down's Will.</p>
        <p>Probate of the will of the late Mr. Thomas John Down, of Underclyffe, Appleton, Managing Director of Greenall, Whitley, and Company, Limited, has been granted at Chester to his son Mr. Arthur Down, Mr. William Owen and Mr. John Burgess,the executors the net amount of estate being sworn at £'53,173 5s 4d.</p>
        <p>"PREMIER" CYCLES at 9 guineas for Best Grade, unsurpassed.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55,Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. Dr. Gornall and the Butchers.</p>
        <p>The annual dinner in connection with the Watring- ton Butchers' Association was held at the Lion Hotel, on Thursday evening and was largely attended, Mr. E. Bowcock presiding. The letters of apology for I non-attendance included one from Dr. J. Guest Gornall, Medical Officer of Health, who wrote thank- ing the Association for their kind invitation, but stating that he could not he present as lie made it a rule that neither he nor any members of his depart- ment should attend dinners of associations with which he might at some tithe come into unpleasant relations. He hoped he would not come into hostile collision with any of the members of that association and he hoped they would take it that he ' thanked them very mach for the invitation.—This letter called forth several strong comments during the evening. Mr. Knowles, who represented the Liverpool Butchers' Association characterised it as a gross insult to every member present, while Mr. Turner, of Wigan, contrasted Dr. Gornall's action with ti at of the medical officer for Wigan who last week attended a dinner arranged by the Wigan butchers and discussed questions of interest with them. Mr. J. Mainwaring, of Warrington, also held that Dr. Gornall had no right to send such a letter.— Alderman Bennett, who responded to the toast of" The town and trade of Warrington" said Dr. Gornall was an honourable man and he thought the letter was due to his punctilious desire to be like Ceesar's wife above snspicion.7.</p>
