      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR CONVENTION. A Local Re-viva . Needed.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR CONVENTION. A Local Re-viva . Needed.</p>
        <p>The remarkable revival of religion in Wales formed the chief topic under consideration at the annual Con- vention in connection with the Warrington and District Christian Endeavour Union which was held in the Primitive Methodist Chapel, Legh-street, on Thursday. At the afternoon meeting there was a fair attendance presided over by the Rev. F. Vaughan. The Rev. J. S. Hughes (president of the Union), and the Rev. F. A. Rees, Manchester were also present. Special prayers , were offered for a revival of religion in Warrington. The CHAIRMAN observed that the religious revival was the m3st important topic of the day. To him it was far beyond anything in the municipal or political world, or in the world which was immediately connected with the several churches. What was needed to-day before anything else was a revival of religious interest in the town and district. He rejoiced that it was pos- sible to gather together under the auspices of the Chris- tian Endeavour Union a number of people for the special purpose of prayer for a revival. The revival spirit was in the air, but they must beware lest it should be merely emotional. They must bear in mind that God had always given special preparation for any spiritual blessing. He hoped that would be a clay when hearts would be sanctified and lives would be set apart to God's service. _ _ _ . • .</p>
        <p>The Rev. F. A. REES, of Manchester, also spoke on the revival. He alluded to the great opponents in the form of materialism,. militarism, scepticism, drunken- ness, gambling and Immorality which the Church had to combat. There were now, however,distinct evidences of a quickening of God's work not only in Wales and in different parts of this country, but also in America and several continental nations. He urged Christian Endeavourers in Warrington to bestir themselves. The revival would be brought here not by working it up, but by "praying it down," EVENING MEETING.</p>
        <p>There was a very encouraging meeting in the evening under the presidency of the Rev. J. S. Hughes. The other speakers were the Rev. F. A. Rees, and Councillor A. Black, president of the Lancashire and Cheshire Christian Endeavour Union. _ _ .</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY (Miss Vanda M. Taylor) presented .her annual report. She was sorry, she said, to have to report the loss of societies, two young people's and one junior society having discontinued holding their meet- ings. The membership of the union had not, however, suffered much thereby. At present they had connected with the Union 19 young people's societies and 9 junior societies, the total membership being 949. A street collection was made on May 9th, in the War- rington district on behalf of Dr: Barnardo's Homes and about £l7 were realized. Miss Taylor detailed the chief events of the year, and said monthly open air meetings were arranged by the Evangelical Committee and some were very well attended. In conclusion she briefly alluded to the Welsh revival and asked the mem- bers to pray and labour that the showers of blessings might descend upon the Sunday schools, Endeavour Societies, and churches of Warrington. Might God grant that the much needed revival might come to our town during the year. (Applause.) In his presidential address the Rev. J. S. Hughes cecdered the members his warmest thanks for the confi- dence they had shown in electing him to the office for the third time. Since their autumnal convention at Lymm a marvellous change had come over the aspect of the Christian world. The Spirit of God had moved with startling suddenness, and had mellowed and ripened souls in such a way as to bring tens of thousands of them into the Church. Councillor A. BLACK delivered an interesting address, comparing the beginnings of Christianity with the commencement of a Christian Endeavour Society. He appealed to his hearers to exert themselves to increase the membership of their societies. The Rev. F. A. BEES narrated numerous incidents of the Welsh revival, which, he said, was is revival of ' rayer, song, and joy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 25, 1905</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 25, 1905</p>
        <p>from their connection with the Society, will not forget its fundamental aims, and will seek to make the Academy, above all else, the home of local culture, taste and true refinement, and the centre of the best and most enlightened local patriotism in the town. THE FINANCES.</p>
        <p>It was reported that the income fortheyear amounted to £167 3s. HA. and after paying expenses and allowing a certain sum for depreciation there was a profit on the year's working of £5 28.71 d. That, said Alderman Bennett, was very satisfactory when it was considered that last year there was a deficit of £27 ss. lid. The report and statement of accounts were adopted on the motion of Mr. Lofthouse seconded by Mr F. G. Taylor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>THE DISAPPEARANCE OF LOCAL LANDMARKS. "Keeping Alive the Town's Traditions.'</p>
        <p>The record of a useful year's work was presented to the members of the Warrington Society on Thursday evening when they assembled in the Old Academy for their annual meeting. Mr. Chas. Madeley presided and there were also present :—Messrs. J. Fulton Smith. J. Longland, H. G. Roberts, H. S. Page, J. Birtles, J. Moore Murray, J. Slack, F. G. Taylor, 0. Lofttionse, J. Richardson, G- Smith, B. S. Newcombe, W. Dimelow, J. Fairhurst (hon. treasurer), Arthur Bennett and E. J. Hall (hon. secretaries.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT submitted tbe sixth annual report of the hon. secretaries as follows :—The most impor- tant incident we have to chronicle in our review of last year's work is the celebration of the centenary of the death of Dr. Priestley, which took place at Northumberland, in the United States, on the 6th February, 1801.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DR. PRIESTLEY</head>
        <p>DR. PRIESTLEY</p>
        <p>Icame to Warrington in 1761, as a tutor in the Academy, then domiciled in our headquarters, and followed it in 1762 to the larger premises erected to provide for its expansion and increasing popularity. He is said to have resided in the red brick house on the left hand side of the present Academy-street, now occupied as oflices by the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., which stood just within the gates of the new building. The marks of the palisades are still visible upon the front of it. The gates themselves were re-erected on the demolition of the Academy, in 1865, at Cairo-street Chapel. A smaller building to the rear, upon a site now occupied by one of Messrs. Richmond and Co.'s warehouses, is said to have been used as Dr. Priestley's laboratory, and is commemorated by Miss Aikin in her "Inventory on the furniture in Dr. Priestley's study " and probably referred to in her well-known poem, "The Mouse's Petition." Here Dr. Priestley wrote several well-known books, including bis monumental "History and Present State of Elec- tricity," and all of them were published at the famous Eyre s' Press, Warrington. It was thought that the tablet which it has long been intended to erect upon the house, might be appropriately associated with the Centenary, and subscriptions were accordingly solicited from members of the Society and others likely to be interested. The response, as will be seen from the accounts, was not by any means so satisfactory as the Executive desired ; but, tanks to the kindness of Mr. W. H. Sherburn, who designed and modelled an artistic tablet, and the generosity of Messrs. Rictunond and Co., who made a beautiful bronze cast of it, they were able to secure a memorial in every way most worthy of the man and the event it was intended to perpetuate. In further celebration of the Centenary, a " Priestley Night" was held at the Academy on Friday, 29th of January, when the following papers were read ; Priestley at Warrington," Mr. ET. Stuart Page ; "Priestley as a Chemist." Mr. F. G. Ruddock ,• "Priest- by aud the Riots," Mr. IV. H. Sherburn.—On the eve of the Centenary, a lecture entitled "Dr. Priestley : A Centenary Survey" was delivered by the Rev. K. F. Freeston, of London, (formerly of Warrington) and editor of to ohs mint' little reprint of Priestly's " Memoirs " published just about this time. The Centenary itself was celebrated by the unveiling of the tablet. This interesting little ceremony was appropriately performed by Dr. T. E. Thorpe, F.R.S., Head of the Government Laboratories, London, and author of anew life of Priestley, who delivered a most eloquent and exhaustive panegyric on Priestley's life and work in an adjoining room, and was afterwards entertained to lunch at the Academy by the subscribers to the fund. Mr. William Long presided on both occa- sions; and the guests included his Worship the Mayor (Alderman William Bolton, J.P.), Principal Gordon (Author of the life of Priestley in the dictionary of National Biography), Mr. H. Roylance Kent, M.A., another authority on Priestley, and Mr. James Charles, the' well-known Warrington artist. The house in Bridge-street lately occupied by Mr. A. P. Butterfield, "three doors from Dolman's-lane," for ever memorable as "the lodgings" of an Englishman not less illustrions (John Howard, the Prison Reformer) who printed all his works in Warrington through William Eyres, is about to disappear, and it is hoped that funds will be forthcoming to enable the Society to acfix on the new building an appropriate reminder of the great philan- thropists's association with the town. As we have said in previous reports, the need for some such record con- stantly becomes more urgent. Scarcely a month elapses without</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>DISAPPEA.RANCE OF SOME INTERESTING LOC it</head>
        <p>THE DISAPPEA.RANCE OF SOME INTERESTING LOC it</p>
        <p>LANDMARK, and if a comprehensive scheme of street improvement has become imperative in fano of the town's growth, it surely is not less imperative to keep alive in it the old traditions which will always be its crowning glory. T e work of demolition, which has just encroached on Market Gate, and will eventuate in a splendid central circus, has lately swallowed up the original Blue Coat School, behind Trinity Church, and it involved last year,'the loss of the Old Crow, a curious hostelry en- tirely built of timber and of Aicks and clay, which according to Mr.,Williarn Beansout, was formerly the town house of Captain Crompton, of Crompton Fold ; and of a very quaint and curious edifice in Fennel street which Mr. Bearnont speaks of as "The oldest Warrington building except the church, and the patriarch by many generations of every house in the town, it having been standing, as there is every reason to believe, in 1465, when the Lyme manuscript was written." It is. he says "a long, low, thatched building built of wood and plaster and whitened, with one sharp projecting gable and a small dormer or star window in the roof. The long building which has an open roof has been the hall, and the gable probably the parlour. You do not wonder," Mr. Beamont adds, to " see a building of such age and such materials show signs of old age, weakness and decay." A portion of the build- ing has fallen since Mr. Beamont's death, but tve cannot refrain from expressing our regret that the remain der has not been preserved and renovated, as in some respects, it was unique and it has only vanished to make way fora row oehouses of a very ordinary type. We also note with much regret the removal, last summer, to Darley Dale, of THE CLERGY DAUGHTERS' SCHOOL,</p>
        <p>a local institution of which we were most Pardonably proud. The charity of which it is an offshoot dates back to 1697. and the school itself to 1841. The removal was largely due to the prevailing smoke and the polluted river, and it emphasizes the necessity of taking active steps to cleanse our streams and atmosphere, and to couple our industrial progress, with a due regard for sanitation and the public health. The sister institution (the Training College) has, we are glad to say, extended its borders by taking Orford Hall,on lease, a step which will secure that fine old mansion, for the present, from the doom we had began to fear. The public response to the appeal for funds to erect a monument in honour of Colonel O'Leary and</p>
        <p>THE SOUTH LANCASHIRE REGIMENT for their services in the South African War was not by any means as liberal or spontaneous as might have been expected, but, thanks largely to the generosity of the Officers themselves and to the untiring exertions of the Workingmen's Committee, a sum of a littld over £l,OOO has at last been realised, and the Committee have instructed Mr. Alfred Drury to design a bronze monu- ment for Queen's Gardens, with Colonel O'Leary at the battle of Pieter's Hill as its central figure, and the names of all the members of the Regiment who lost their lives in the War recorded at the base of it. If any funds remain, it is proposed to record the names of other Warringtonians who fell in the great strife in some appropriate way. Mr. Thomas May continues his most useful work at Wilderspool. and has recently pub- lished in "Warrington's Roman Remains," an exhaustive account of it. The whole of his most interesting finds have been deposited at the Museum. The Museum Committee have recently acquired several excellent specimens of the work of Mr. Charles for the Art Gallery; have purchased George Sheffield's "Cheapside" a characteristic picture of Old Warrington; and, we are particularly pleased to notice, contemplate a definite collection of pictures of this kind. They are also obtainin., copies of existing prints of old local build- , lugs and taking steps to secure a systematic photo- 'graphic record of such interesting and historic buildings as remain. It is hoped that both the Warrington Society and the Warrington Photographic Society will cordially co-operate with them in this important work THE ACADEMY ITSELF. has been improved in several minor details during the I year. The coffee room,in particular, has been re-floored and plastered and artistically decorated,and every care is being taken by the executive, whilst carefully preserv- ing all the old features of the place, to improve its appearance and to add to its attractivenessand comfort. Amongst the more important of the recent gifts to the Society is a copy of the very valuable paper on "Warrington in 1580" recently read before the Historic Society of Lancashire and Cheshire by Mr. J. Paul Rylands. "The Book Club" flourishes, and the Reading room has been well supplied with magazines and papers. A Chess Club, under the leadership of Mr. F. G. Taylor, is the latest addition to the attractions offered by the Society. The Photographic and Polyglot Societies continuetheir tenancies of the Academy, and the former had a second and most successful exhibition in the spring The Warrington Esperanto Society is now likewise I appropriately installed at the Academy. Among our recent visitors have been the Wesleyan District Synod,, the St. John's Rambling Club, Altrincham the founders of the Conservative Labour Party, the Cancashire and Cheshire Antiquarian Society, and the District Museum Conference. The Society. now numbers on its books about 150 members; but, In consequence of the state of its finances, it was decided, in the early part of the year, to impose an entrance fee of half a guinea on I members who use the Academy as a Club. It is hoped that these measures will ultimately enable us to make ends meet and gradually wipe out the deficit. If funds allowed, there are many ways in which we might increase our usefulness. We trust that nll our members will realize their responsibilities; will do their best to worthily perpetuate the old traditions and whilst deriving much legitimate social pleasure</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Alderman l3EssErr proposed the re-election of Mr. William Long as president, remarking that he was the best and most loyal friend the society had had. It was largely through his generosity that the Society became practicable and that the saving of the Academy was brought within the range of practical politics. Mr. LONGLAND seconded and the motion was heartily agreed to. The other officers were elected as follows :—Hon. treasurer, Mr. John Fairhurst ; hon. secretaries, Messrs. A. Bennett and E. J. Hall; hon. auditor, Mr. C. D. Parkinson; committee, Messrs. J. Birtles, H.Chambers, J. Dimelow, L. Greening. J. Longland, C. Madeley, J. Moore Murray, B. S. Newoombe, W.H. Woodcock, F. G. Taylor, J. A. Ransome, and 0. Lofthouse. THANKS.</p>
        <p>Hearty votes of thanks were accorded to the officers and to the members who have takena prominent part in the organization of whist drives and other social functions</p>
        <p>The Chairman havineabeen thanked for presiding, the meeting closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>-- Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>(By " Ovallst.";</p>
        <p>For once in a way the least shall be first. This week I will deal with the " A " team before alluding to the performance of the seniors. So much interest was taken on Saturday in the Combination game at Warrington between Warrington " A" and Wigan " A "that very few people went to Wigan for the League match. " Ovalist " was among the unfaithful stay-at-homes and I cannot say he had any cause to regret the change. Twelve a side second team foot- ball is often more attractive than fifteen a side first team football, and the game here was certainly good enough to keep the 5,000 spectators in a state of lively interest from first to last.</p>
        <p>The match was a most important one for both teams and victory was absolutely essential for Warrington to stand any chance of robbing their opponents of the Combination medals so nearly in their grasp. As they had already beaten Wigan "A" at Wigan they were confident of success, but the other side was equally sure of its prowess gaining the day. The Wiganers had not lost a match, with the exception of the one to Warrington, and they were natutally very keen on avenging themselves and making- the Com- bination championship a safe thing. The task proved too big for them, however, and Warrington's triumph was decisive.</p>
        <p>A. very high wind caused four "twenties " to be agreed upon and the home side were fortunate in losing the toss in my opinion. Wigan naturally chose to have the assistance of the elernsnts at the outset, and Wariington consequently had the last spell on the attack which was a considerable advantage, for they knew exactly how much was demanded of them. At the end of the first moiety Wigan only succeeded in registering a try, which was entirely due to an error on the part of Jordan. On turning round the locals soon wiped this off and put up 8 points, so that at half-time they were leading by 8 points to 3.</p>
        <p>Wigan made a great effort on resuming bat could only get on level terms, so that when positions were reversed for the last time, Warrington were only called upon to score once to win the inttch co mfort- ably. Before the close they scored twice, and had easily the best of matters, finilly running oat winners by 14 points to 8 points.</p>
        <p>Quite half the men on either side were oh first- teamers, so that there was a good standard of foot- ball maintained throughout. J. Davies and Berry were the pick of the homestera, the latter scoring the last two tries in fine style. his powerful build stood him in good stead on both occasions, and there is the making of a fine centre in this tall youth. Warring- ton have long required a man of his physique in the three-quarter line, and the hope is general that experi- ence may give Berry the polish which is alone required to make him a player of repute.</p>
        <p>Dickenson and Harris showed glimpses of their old form, but the latter's bad habit of "playing the man" instead of the game was again conspicuous.. He must make the goal line his objective if he is ever to retain a place among the seniors. W. Bate is improving every week, while there is plenty of work in young Jordan. On the Wigan side, W. Anderson was by no means the success anticipated, and his partner, Tresize, played the bstter game. Voicey was conspicuously smart on the left wing, but the centres were only medium. Forward, Wigan had an advantage, and the Warrington scrummagers still appear to have something to learn in the way of gain- ing possession.</p>
        <p>At Wigan, the Warrington men got a trouncing which practically puts them out of running for the South West Lancashire Cup, unless there is a slip by Wigan. They were not so badly beaten as the scope 16-8 would indicate,for Boardman early retired through an jury to the side, Shugars was sent off in the second half, and D. Davies Wa g prac- tically a passenger through tbe knocking about he sustained. Warrington did very well to score a, goal and two tries. Fish and Isherwood were in fine form and a bit ahead of any of the Wigan three- quarters. The weakness of Warrington was again at half-back, where Bate and D. Davies were ni match for Hopkins and Thomas. The !atter lair were very smart and frequently took the ball right past the Warrington backs. Their- 'efforts were splendidly supplemented by Leytham who, scored three grand tries on Kenyon's wing. lie was altogether too clever for the Warrington man and eluded the wonid-be tackler very easily. I wonder how often Warrington legret not having closed with th e opportunity they once had of securing Leytham from Lancaster? lie would have been a grand acquisition to a team possessing such an artist as Fish.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire Comptition up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>• For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts. Wigan 8 6.. 9 0 .TO 17— 71... 8 I— 37... 12 W'gton Leigh St. Helens 8... 2... 5... 1... 5 9 37... 8 12— 52... 5 Widnes Runcorn 6... 1... 5... 0... 6 57- 27... 7 12— 51... 2</p>
        <p>The surprise of the Northern Leagne games on Saturday was the defeat of Broughton Rangers by Batley. It must have been a shock to the Rangers just as the latter were viewing the prospects of the League championship so complacently. They are still in the running but Oldham remain consistently at the top, with Bradford a close second. Runcorn, as well as Batley, were successful, hut they occasioned no surprise in beating Leigh at Irwell-lane.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows :- -For— —Against— P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pts Pts. Oldham 26...19 ... 6... 1...213 48-216...22 17— 95...39 Bradford 26...18... 6... 2...32 56-232...21 28-126...38 Brou't'n R 25...18... G... 1...30 52-216_32 19-121...37 Hunslet 26...16...10... 0...51 32-198...22 30-134-32 Leeds Wigan 25 .14...10... 1-25 37-161...27 27-125...29 Waregen 25...13. 10... 2...27 35-159...21 25-117...28 Salford Hull K.R 25...13...12... 0...31 33-161_27 33-153...26 Hull 25 .10...11... 4...23 34-148...27 33-153...24 Leigh Widnes 25...10...14... 1...16 24-104...30 47-201...21 Hal:fax Swinton 26... 9...16... 1...15 28-114...31 34-164...19 Wakefield 26... 8...16... 2...29 12— 94...20 37-167-18 St. Helens 25... 8...16... I_3o 28-144...36 59-249...17 Batley 26... 8...17... 1...27 20-114-36 40-192...17 Runcorn ---- To-day sees the last match before the Cup-ties. Swinton are the visitors to Wilderspool and should prove easy victims if the locals produce anything like their proper form. There are important changes in ' the team, the most interesting of wide].) is the appear- auce of Berry at centre. He has certainly earned his promotion, but I hardly think he is fit for first-class</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
