      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>JUST IN T RODUCED</head>
        <p>JUST IN T RODUCED</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S GOLDEN BUTTER</p>
        <p>BISCUITSI</p>
        <p>"Why not state explicitiy what you mean, in clear unmistakable terms ?" I asked. '7 Surely, it would be best."</p>
        <p>" Would it ?"—thoughtf ally. " I would like to see one woman perfectly logical," I continued. "It would be the achievement of the age. Let a woman be what she may—educated, a thinker, trained to reason on logical lines—the moment that her emotions are touched, reason, logic falls away as if they had never been. Where a woman's feelings are cpncerned, she is not to be trusted to reason."</p>
        <p>" You say," Phyllis said, " a woman cannot be both emotional and logical. Which would you wibh me to be? "</p>
        <p>" You have not told me," I said, " why you were sure I would come, notwithstanding your letter. " Because "—she hesitated, giving me a swift look. I waited.</p>
        <p>"Because—did I ask you to come ? " " Phyllis! Of course you asked me xot to come,' "You are unkind," she said. I took the note from my pocket and re-read it. " I would not read it literally." she suggested. Phyllis was right. She really asked me if I would stay away, and said that she ought not to see me— not the reverse. So much for reason, where the feel- ings are concerned. I could not see from her face what she was thinking of, and I was curious to know her reason for writing the note. It promised to be interesting. "I am sorry, Phyllis. You didn't tell me not to come ; but you certainly didn't ask me to." "It was arranged yon were to come." " Exactly, and, I remembering it, your letter sug gested that I should stay away." "But you came," she said. _</p>
        <p>"Did -y o u wislime to cone?" Phyllis was a long while answering me. She must know; she couldn't, really, he in doubt. Perhaps my,attitude wasn't quite appropriate to the occasion. Sot:allow, it seemed that to stand a distance away from where she was sitting appeared entire detachment.</p>
        <p>I threw a hassock at her feet; it offered a more advantageous position. "This is refined cruelty, Phyllis; and I am tired of doubt and misunderstanding." "I did want you," she said at last. "Then why 'did you say you ought not to see me?" "Don't you see ? " Of course 1 didn't. She explained : "1 wanted to see you—so very much."</p>
        <p>It was joy to know Phyllis could not quite keep it from me. Then recollection came, stealing the sweetness.</p>
        <p>Phyllis had said she 1-ras not herself I took the note from my pocket again and pat an accusing finger on the phrase.</p>
        <p>"How could I be myself if I wanted to see you more than ever?" she murmured.</p>
        <p>I couldn't have believed there was anyone nicer than Phyllis. "Perhaps," she suggested, " if you Come again I shall be myself." " I won't take you literally," I said. (To be eontintusel.)</p>
        <p>(These articles originally appeared in the " London Echo.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MR. STANLEY WILSON'S NEW APPOINTMENT.</head>
        <p>MR. STANLEY WILSON'S NEW APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerkship of Leigh.</p>
        <p>We have pleasure in announcing that out of twenty- seven applicants, Mr. Stanley Wilson, Deputy Town Clerk of Warrington, has been selected for the Town Clerkship of Leigh. The appointment was made at a special meeting of the Council on Tuesday evening after the following applicants had been interviewed: —Mr. Leonard Hewlett, senior assistant solicitor for the Leeds Corporation ; Mr. Fred E. Ensor, assistant solicitor for the Cardiff Corporation ; Mr. S. Wilson, of Warrington ; and Mr. Thomas Holt, formerly Deputy Town Clerk of Leigh, and now Town Clerk of Winchester. The commencing salary is £4OO per annum, rising by annual increment of £5O to £OOO. Mr. Wilson, who is thirty-nine years of ate, is the youngest son of the late Mr. Thomas Wilson, a promi- nent townsman, who for over half a century carried on business as a silk mercer in Sankey-street, Warrington, and was a relative of the late Mr. Joseph Perrin, the sixth Mayor of Warrington and a large glass manu- facturer in the town. He received the early part of his education at the Warrington People's College and after- wards at a private school. Entering the Town Clerk's office in December, 188 2, he has had a wide and varied experience in municipal work. Step by step his ability resulted in his passing through the various grades of promotion, and each year found him growing more indispensable to his able and energetic chief, Mr. J. Lyon'Whittle. In 1890 he was appointed Assistant Town Clerk and he became Deputy Town Clerk in 1898. Turning his attention to the study of the law he was articled to the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), and admitted as a solicitor in the early part of 1901. It is interesting to note that during his term of office the Borough has almost doubled itself in area, population, and assessable value, and Mr. Wilson has taken a prominent part in many of the progressive works carried out by the Corporation. He has had , charge of the conduct of Parliamentary and municipal 'elections, and has acquired considerable experience in the Parliamentary work affecting borough schemes and in the preparations for Local Government inquiries ' Mr. Wilson has always been to the fore as an active organizer in such public functions as Mayoral banquets, receptions, fancy dress balls Sze. In the midst of official pressure he has found time for valuable walk in the promotion of public charities such as efforts on I behalf of the Lifeboat Institution, the hospitals, and the organizations designed from time to time for the relief of the distressed poor. During the colliers' I strike in 1893 he acted as hon. secretary to the Local I Relief Committee which collected and disbursed to the sufferers through the strike the handsome sum of nearly I £3,009. In his early days Mr. Wilson was an athletic of some note, and the winner of many prizes. He was a popular member of the Cycling Club in the days of "the spider." and has also been connected with the Swimming club and Lacrosse club. Of late years he has been an enthusiastic golfer. He was married in 1894 to the eldest daughter of the late Mr. Charles Twiss, the respected headmaster of the People's College. By his geniality not less than his ability, Mr. Wilson has gained the respect of a very large circle who will regret his severance from War- rington. They will, however, be gratified to know that he has obtained such an important appointment and I will wish him every success in his new sphere of work. CONGRATULATIONS FROM THE WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>SOCIETY. At the annual meeting of the Warrington Society held at the Old Academy on Thursday evening, Mr. J. LONGLAND moved a resolution of hearty congratulation to Mr. Wilson on his appointment. Mr. Wilson, he said, I was a member of the Society who was held in great affection by all his fellow workers. He had been an exceedingly useful member of the committee, and he (Mr. Longland) did not think it would be possible to say anything too complimentary about him. Mr. J. MOORE MURRAY seconded. He said he had only known Mr. Wilson for a short_ time, but he very soon found him to be "a jolly good fellow "in more respects than one. (Hear, hear.) Alderman BENNETT supported and paid a specia tribute to Mr. Wilson's work as a leading public official who had the art of doing his duty in a genial, pleasant and attractive way. The resolution was heartily agreed to. MR. WILSON AT LEraii. Yesterday's issue of the "Leigh Chronicle" contains portralt of Mr. Wilson together with a brief biographyl There is also a report of the proceedings at.the Counci meeting at which Mr. Wilson was appointed. In the course of a brief speech Mr. Wilson expressed his thanks for the honour the Council had conferred upon him and said he was conscious of the responsibility attached to the office, He Could only promise that he would do his best faithfully to perform the duties not only to the satisfaction of the Council but of the rate- payers of the Town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>An Industry Revived.</head>
        <p>An Industry Revived.</p>
        <p>The flat-building industry which at one time flourished at Sankey Bridges, but which has, for many years, been practically abandoned has been revived. For some months past, Messrs. Clare and Ridgway have been building a large flat for the United Alkali Co., and the work has just been cotn- pleted. Nest Thursday afternoon the boat will be launched.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Russia's Struggle for Freedom.</head>
        <p>Russia's Struggle for Freedom.</p>
        <p>An appeal" for financial assistance from the English working-classes on behalf of the opnressed Russian revolutionaries has been issued by the Labour Repre: sentation Committee, and taken up by the local branch. Collections in workshops and at meetings are sug- gested, and subscriptions may be forward to Messrs. Edward norrocks, president L.R.C. ; Oswald Buckley, secretary L.R.C. ; Fred Stott, secretar. Trades Council, 160, Battersby-lane ; J. Podmore jun, reasurer of the Trades Council. f466K</p>
      </div>
