      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>TRAVELLER'S CLAIM FOR COMMISSION. Action against the —erasmic Co. Ltd.</head>
        <p>TRAVELLER'S CLAIM FOR COMMISSION. Action against the —erasmic Co. Ltd.</p>
        <p>MEE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>INTERESTING POINT RAISED.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING POINT RAISED.</p>
        <p>An interesting action was heard at the Warrington County Court on Thursday when John Borstal! Barry 48, Old lklillgate, Manchester, sought to recover from the Erasmic Co., Limited, \Varrington, the sum of £56 balance of commission earned by him whilst in the employ of the defendant company. In answer to the claim the defendents paid into Court the sum of £ll 3s. 3d.,together with £2 Os. Gd. proportionate costs. Mr. A. Browne appeared for the plaintiff, defendants being represented by Mr. Byrne, Manchester, instructed by Mr. It Evans. Mr. Brostne stated that since the claim was origin- ally entered an application for discovery had been made upon th 3 defendants as a result of which it was found that provided he was right in his contention, he was entitied to a far greater sum than £56. This the defendant admitted and if his Honour found for the plaintiff they would consent to judgment for the larger sum. Plaintiff entered the service of the defendants on the 7th October 1901 being appointed under agreement as a commercial traveller. Two subsequent agreements were entered into and he continued in their employ until October 1904, when he resigned his position. In the agreements his duties were defined and were to call upon chemists and hotels in Lincolnshire and Yorkshire, to solicit orders and collect accounts. Unfortunately throughout the whole time differences of opinion had arisen between him and Mr. Baxter, one of the defendant company, which had led to disputes which had been got over by other persons. Plaintiff claimed that he was entitled to commission upon a number of accounts and he now found that he was entitled to commission upon a much larger number of accounts. In the case of one firm—Messrsa Taylors—they had found that the amount of goods supplied to that firm instead of being £72 4s. as shown by defendants, it was £llB and they clainied commission on the larger sum. The most particular item was however as to whether the plaintiff was entitled to commission upon goods supplied to the branch shops of• customers whose head offices were in his district, but whose branch establish- ments were in another district. As an illustration he (Mr. Browne) would quote the case of Boots whose head office was at Nottingham and who had 80 branch shops within the district in which the plaintiff travelled. In the case of those shops plaintiff was not entitled to commission upon goods sold to them as the head office of the firm was in Nottingham. Messrs. Needham, Huddersfield, were within the plaintiff's district and were in a similar position to Boots. Plaintiff therefore claimed that he was entitled to com- mission supplied to Messrs. Needham's branch shops which were outside his district. Defendants, however, refused to recognize the same conditions with regard to Needham's branch shops as they did with regard to Boots. Although the defendants maintained that the plaintiff was not entitled to commission upon Needham's account they from time to time sent him slips of paper which showed the amount of goods purchased by that firm. Another inconsistency on the part of the defend- ants was that while they refused to pay the plaintiff commission upon business done with Needham's branch shop they had paid him commission under similar circumstances. In the case of Messrs. Mason of Leeds, goods had at the order of that firm been supplied to London, upon the sale of which the plaintiff had been paid commission. That, Mr. Browne con- tended, showed that the defendants recognized that a traveller in whose district the head office of a firm happened to be was entitled to commission upon the whole of the business done with that firm. When he signed the agreement with the defendants,plaintiff failed to notice that he had not to canvass for orders for glycerine exceeding ten cwts., and secured a contract from Messrs. Goodall, Backhoase and Company for the supply of from ten to fifteen tons of glyserine. In spite of the agreement the firm wrote to the plaintiff con- gratulating him upon his.success and crediting him with £7 IGs. 3d. commission upon the order. That sum the. plaintiff had never received and was included in the £56 claimed that day. He (Mr. Browne) submitted that the case was a very serious one and one which affected every commercial traveller in the country. Plaintiff gave evidence bearing out Mr. Browne's statement. He had, he said, ascertained that the total amount of business done with Needha ms was £404 9s. 4d. and upon that sum heclaimed 7d. per cent. commission On the 23rd of September 1904 he resigned his position as he could not any longer stand the disputes with Mr. Baxter. The same month he received a letter from Mr. F. 11. Roberts solicitor claiming from him on behalf of the defendants the sum of £2 16s. 4d. Plaintiff sent a cheque for £2 Bs. and then found that instead of his owing the firm money the firm owed him a considerable sum in commission to which he was entitled. When plaintiff entered hi S claim in the Court defendants at once'showed theyhad no right to claim the £2 16s. by paying £ll 3s. 3d. into court. Cross-examined: The £404 business done with Need- hams was business done outside his district, and was solicited by another traveller. He claimed that he was entitled to commission upon any order from Needham's no matter to what part of Great Britain the goods were sent.</p>
        <p>Mr. Byrne: Suppose one of Needham's shops in Southampton purchased soap to the value of .£lO through the Southampton representative of the Erasmic Com- pany and the £lO WAS paid through the head office would you claim commission? Plaintiff: Most decidedly. Mr. Byrne: Therefore you claim that because the £404 for business done through defendants' Birmingham representative. On what Mr. Harris was paid at Huddersfield you cls im commission?</p>
        <p>Plaintiff : Yes. His Honour': What would become of Mr. Harris' Plaintiff : He would have similar claims on his ground. His Honour : Sappose Mr. Harris gets a lot of orders from Needham's branch shops in Birmingham are you entitled to commission upon those orders ? Plaintiff : Most decidedly. ' Illis Honour : Where is Mr. Harris to get his com- mission from.</p>
        <p>- Plaintiff: Certainly upon business done with firing w hose head offices are n his district and whose branch (Wines are in my district. Further Cross examined plaintiff said that in the agreement it was stated that he was not to canvass orders for more than ten cwts of glycerine at a time. There was however, an understanding that upon larger quantities he was to have 2..11 per cent. commission upon the first half ton and 11 per certt upon the remainder. For the half year ende May 30th, 1902 his aettml commission amounted to £62 10s. and ex- penses to 00 11s. That was Rl4 short of his guaranteed salary which had to be made up by the departments. The commission upon the sale of glycerine to Messrs. Goodall Backhouse and Company had gone to make up the difference.</p>
        <p>Mr. Byrne read the agreement entered into between the plaintiff and defendants which said that plaintiff should receive commission on all orders receive from customers in the district in which be was placed, whether obtained personally or sent direct to the defendants. That, contended Mr. Byrne, did not entitle the plaintiff to commiss;on upon sales to Needham's branch shops outside his district.</p>
        <p>- By Mr. Browne, rillintiff islid that for the half year ended May 30th 1902 he actually received .£l5 over and above his guaranteed salary, Mr. W. M. Watson. manager for the sole agents for the Liebig Company sad it was the custom amongst travellers for commission to be paid to the traveller in whose district the actual turnover took place. In his firm it was never any different. The commission was paid to the tiaveller in whose distfict the money was , paid. • I Similar evidence was given by Messrs. J. Fairbairn .T. Bolton and J. T. Johnson, gentlemen connected with the soap trade. Mr. Byrne contended that the plaintiff was not affected by the usages of commercial travellers but was bound by the agreement and that by that agreement he was not entitled to commission on goods ordered and suppled to branch shops outside his district. Mr. Browne contended that Needham's head"-0 constituted the customer within the terms of the agrel- ment. All accounts were sent to the head office and paid from the head office. Mr. Wardle, office manager for the defendants denied ttsexistence of such custom amongst travellers as was spoken to by the plaintiff's witnesses. His Honour held with Mr. Byrne with regard to the commission upon business done with Messrs. Needham. Mr. Browne : In that case you have no evidence as to what portion of the £lOl was for goods supplied within the plaintiff's district. Mr. Byrne : It is for the plaintiff to prove what comes from his district.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: He cannot prove anything because during the conduct of this case the defendants have placed every possible obstacle in our way. They have shown that by wrongly charging us .£2 which we paid and which we never got back. Mr. Byrne : That attack is very unjustiftable. Mr. Browne never examined the books himself. Mr. Brown : They were examined by my clerk, who has had 18 years' experience. Mr. Byrne: The ledger will show where theaccounts come from. Continuing, he said that his clients were prepared to forego the other parts of the claim in dis- pute. His Honour said it appeared to him that the plaintiff should be repaid the £.2 8s which he paid to Mr. Roberts, and with the consent of the defendants he would include that sum in his judgment, which was for the plaintiff for £2l Bs. 7d., including the amount paid into court, with costs.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES.—According to gemp's Mer- cantile Gazette, the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, Feb. 17th, was 204, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 106; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 98. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 91 ; Deeds of Arrangement, 76—Total 167 ; being an increase of 37. The total number of Commercial Failures recorded during the 7 weeksjof the present year is 1,257 ; the total number re- corded in the corresponding 7 weeks of last year was 1,193 showing an increase of 64.</p>
        <p>.. • •</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
