      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>INFLUENZA INCREASING</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING</p>
        <p>--7</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the country. The visitation of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainly on the increase. The origin of the disease or rather the circumstances which bring it about is as mivh a mystery as ever. You cannot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greater extent than almost any other disease and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines he has only a mild form of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual. It can hardly be doubted that the prevalencelof Chest and Lung Complaints must be ascribed to the baneful influence of Influenza, whose after-effects so frequently take this form. It was a bad day for this Country when this malignant scourge first visited our shores. Year after year it has never since failed to revive at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravages, and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will, regain their former health. As a Preventative, we know of nothing equal to Gwilyrn Evans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken in time have often proved effectual in warding off attacks of this Malady, and always succeed in mitigating their severity when a patient is under their influence. Do you suffer from Chills, alternating with Sudden Flushes. Severe Head- ache, accompanied by difficulty in breathing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest, under and between the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you suffer from any of these symptoms, know that they are the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. By common consent Patients every where admit that they feel more depressed and miserable after an attack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The best way to banish these miserable feelings is to take Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. We can strongly and confidently recommend this Preparation, as it has treated severe cases of Influenza, Heavy Colds, Extreme Weakness, and f,angour, with greater success than any other known remedy. We may also add that as a remedy for Indigestion,Sleepiessness, Low Spirits, Nervousness, and Loss of Appetite, Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters stands unequalled. It is sold in bottles 2s. 9d, and 4s. 9d., by all Chemists and Patent Medicive Vendors, or sent direct, carriage free, for the above prices, flay the Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanellv, South Wales. Avoid Imitations. See the name "Gwilym Evans "on Label Stamp and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Pollee Court. Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (presiding), Alderman Smethurst, Messrs. J. W. Hayward. H. Thornton, G. Pilling, S. Wright, and W. Roberts. SLEEPING OUT.—James Henry Worthington, a stranger, was fined ss. and costs for sleeping in an out- building in Black's-yard, off Bridge•street, on Sunday morning. A WOMAN'S THREAT.—Mary SOll9, a stranger, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Arpley-street on Sunday afternocrn. P.C. Leonard said prisoner took off one of her clogs and threatened to break all the windows of the Police Station with it.—She was tined ss. and costs. _ _</p>
        <p>FIGHTING ABOUT FOOTBALL.--John Loyden,labourer, 2, Buckley.s-court, and Bernard McNamee, labourer, 19, Dolman's-lane, were summoned for fighting in Butter- market-street.—P.C. Cooper said he heard the defendants arguing about football and they commenced fighting.— The Chief Constable said that kind of thing was very frequent after football matches. Men would stand arguing at street corners until the early hours of the morning and would end by coming to blows.—Defend- ants were fined ss. and costs each. _ _</p>
        <p>BAD LANGUAGE.—John Stanton, or Griffin, labourer, 6, James-street, who made his 31st appearance, was fined ss. and costs for using indecent language within the hearing of James-street, to the annoyance of passengers on February 12th.—For a simtlar offence within the hearing of Dial-court, on the same date, William and Jane Lloyd, 37, Dial-court, were only fined ss. each as this was their first offence.</p>
        <p>"I firmly believe that if the children. were well fed our workhouses, gaols and asylums would soon be en.pty." Sir John Gorst, MP.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>Another of these cheerful meetings was held on Sunday I last, Mr. D. Owen presiding. He spoke upon the lesson from Hood, the artist, who in his pictures, got hold of the spirit of the subject,and so made them speak power- fully to the admirer. So in Christian life we must study the character of Christ in order that we could in some asure, reproduce that character in our own life upon earth. Mr. F. Fletcher of Crmston,was the speaker and drew usefulflessons from the two Mary's at the Sepulchre " Who shall roll away the stone ?" First he alluded to the difficulties of life, and how they may be removed ; difficulties,often worked out for us great good. He said it was a great mistake to anticipate difficulty, as many times, the difficulty was only imaginary. Some people, were al days on the look out for things which never happened. The best way was to live a day at a time trusting in God to do all things for the best. The speaker urged upon all, to have Job's spirit for in all trouble he could say "The Lord give, and thehLord hath taken away, Blessed be the , name of the Lord.”ln that,were all difficulties removed. I Mr. Payton,Mus. Bac. played an organ solo,Mr. J. Owen ; and Mr. Owen of Manchester took part in the meeting.l Two new members were welcomed. (Total number 586).1</p>
        <p>Delicious</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>DOCK F ITALITY AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DOCK F ITALITY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>a</p>
        <p>Albert Birchwood, a sailor on the schooner, Water- bird, of Towey, was killed at Weston Point Docks, near Runcorn on Monday evening. He was in the hold a.ssisting to discharge, when the tub struck the jibboom, became unshipped from the hook of the crane chain, and fell upon his head. His neck was broken and death was almost instantaneous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>ONE CUP</head>
        <p>ONE CUP</p>
        <p>SMOII</p>
        <p>Cocoa</p>
        <p>:N NUTRIMENT TO ILn. BEEF OR</p>
        <p>N CUPS OF .ORDINARY COCOA.</p>
