      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MEETING OF TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT SPEECHES BY LOCAL EDUCATIONISTS.</p>
        <p>Criticism of the Scale of Salaries.</p>
        <p>THE TEACHING OF "LOCAL PATRIOTISM" AND HEALTH SUBJECTS.</p>
        <p>The largest meeting of elementary school teachers ever held in Warrington took place at the Technical Institute on Saturday afteroon. It was promoted under the joint auspices of the local branch of the National Union of Teachers and the Warrington Class Teachers' Association and wits rendered particularly interesting and important by the fact that four prominent local educationists delivered addresses on matters pertaining to the work and life o. a teacher. Members of the profession to the number of about two hundred attended, the chair being taken by Mr. Edward Wallington (president of the Warrington branch N.U.T.) who was supported on the platform by Alderman Bennett (vice-president of the Education Committee), the Rev. Morley Stevenson (chairman of the School Management Committee), Mr. A. J. Jolley (chairman of the Finance Committee), and Mr. a. Moore Murray (director of education.) CRITICISM OF 'ME SCALE OF SALARIES.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that he was sorry to say he had received a letter from Mr. F. Monks, Chairman of the Education Committee, apologising for his non-attend- ance through illness. He was sure they all very much regretted the absence of Mr. Monks. (Hear, hear.) The , subject of the address which he had promised to deliver —" Self-improvement "—was one that was peculiarly appropriate to him, for Mr. Monks was a most con- spicuous example of what could be achieved by self- improvement. Many of those present were familiar with his public career, with the good he had conferred upon the town, and teachers especially were indebted to him for the strong support he had given for many years to the cause of education. They were to hear an address from the Vice-Chairman of the Committee on "The Teacher's place in civic life." Rightly or wrongly the doors of the Town Council were closed against the teacher, and in many parts of the country he had been excluded also from the Education Committee—a position in his (the speaker's) opinion, for which he was peculiarly fitted. They in Warrington felt grateful to I the Town Council that they had not been so exclusive and that they allowed the Warrington Teachers'Associa- tiou to send a representative to the Education Committee The work which Mr. Upson had done on the committee had fully justified his appointment—(hear, hear,)—for it was generally admitted that he was one of the most useful members. (Applause). A happier subject than "A teacher's ideals" could not have been chosen for the Rev. Morley Stevenson, by reason of his position as a clergyman and the principal of a Training College. In regard to "The financial aspect of education" upon which Mr. Jolley would speak, up to the present they had not had very much fault to find with the financial position taken up by the Education Committee, for the scale of salaries was not below the average of the country. He sincerely hoped that the committee would not think they had gone as far as they had any need to do—(laughter)—because their scale was capable of expansion and improvement. Several faults might be found with the scales not only in Warrington but in many places throughout the country. They were all far too rigid; they were lacking in elasticity. Teachers were all reduced to the same dead level ; their value depended upon the number of years they had lived and not upon the quality of work they did. Education Committees had found different ways of showing their disapprobation of the negligent or indifferent, but they had not found out any way of rewarding those who were most successful. In that respect, teachers very much ,resembled the scholars—a little encouragement would go a long way. Another fault he had to find with the scale was that no difference had been made between the teachers of the lower standards or classes and the teachers of the upper classes. Speaking from his own experience he knew that to take proper charge of an upper standard involved a very large:amount of private time in the preparation of lessons and in the marking of papers which up to the present, received no recogni- tion whatever from the Education Committee. Then, in order to seduce the shortage of teachers Education Committees here and in other places were inducing boys and girls to become pupil teachers by offering them large salaries out of all proportion to their useful- ness and responsibility. In his opinion the inducement ought to come at the end of a teacher's life and not at the beginning, and it was very wrong to tempt boys and girls with , large salaries and then to disappoint them at the close of their career. Let municipal authorities hold before the teachers some inducement, let them have something reasonable to look forward to when their years of active service were over ; let them be placed on the same level as members of the civil service and the shortage would disappear very quickly and men and women of the right stamp would be attracted to the profession. The Edu- cation committee had affected a wonderful improve- ment. in the state of education in the town, but there was much to be done before they could regard Warring- ton as an ideal town in the matter of education. In the meantime, he would appeal to all teachers to make the best of their opportunities, to strive to lay the founda- tion of a thoroughly sound elementary educatior, upon which a good secondary and technical super-structure could be built, never forgetting the fact that a large number of the scholars would not have a chance of entering the higher institutions. Let them try to cul- tivate in the children a love of learning for its own sake and not for the sake of scholarships,prizes and rewards, and at the same time let them seize every opportunity to inculcate into the minds of their pupils right principles of character,so that whatever positions they were called upon to fill they might become good and useful citizens and members of society. (Applause). ALDERMAN BENNETT ON LOCAL PATRIOTISM. Alderman BENNETT said it was with peculiar pleasure that he rose to address a meeting of teachers under the chairman's presidency, because Mr. Wallington was once his teacher. He proposed to address them on the subject of local patriotism which, he thought, was somewhat neglected in their schools. His remarks would be prompted by the desire to inculcate a spirit of local patriotism in the teachers, in the hope that through them it would descend to the scholars, and they would get abetter general level</p>
        <p>i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 25, 1905. THE COST OF EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 25, 1905. THE COST OF EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>f local patriotism throughout the town. No doubt many of those present were not born Warriugtouians, and character of the child would begin to come out. I he could only pity them and express his regret. (APPlatise)* (Laughter.) Most strangers who visited thetown formed MR. JOLLEY ON THE FINANCIAL ASPECT. the impression that it was not very attractive at first sight. What they wanted to do was to cultivate the imagination—(laughter)—both retrospectively and pro- Mr. A. J.*JOLLEY then spoke upon "The financial aspect of education," remarking that personally he pectively. This somewhat squalid and smoke-covered welcomed the criticisms Mr. Wallington had put for- blot on the face of civilisation—if they liked to so call ward. He did not think the majority of members of I it—had a history of which any town in England might the Education Committee were afraid of criticism; on Ibe proud. He was very mucn afraid that even Warring. the contrary they welcomed it when it was fair and ton citizens did not know half as much about the history candid. He would like them to remember that the of Warrington as they ought to do, and it was important scales of salaries now in force in Warrington were not that teachers who had to train the rising generation hastily arrived at, and to some extent they were the should have some knowledge of local history. How result of compromise. He thought he could claim for many of our Warrington teachers, he wondered, had been to Wirderspool to see the Roman camp ?—probably them that whatever defects or deficiencies there might none of them. He would like to feel that they had all be in the scales, they at least marked a distinct advance been, because the excavations conducted under the on the methods of remuneration for teachers which skilful charge of Mr. May showed that Warrington had obtained in the town before the new Act and authority an came into operation. One particular principle which had n antiquity not very much less than two thou that no individual years. Alderman Bennett went on to refer to other been in the minds of the committee was employed in educational work should suffer a penny- places of historical interest, remarking that round the old worth by the working of the Act. No-one, at any rate, Academy centred some of the most memorable tradi- was worse off for the change,and he thought the majority tions. To him the memory of past ages was not a mere of the teachers had materially benefited. The question memory. He felt almost conscious sometimes as he of salaries had again recently occupied the attention of walked to and fro in the frarroco streets of the presence the committee, but for perfectly valid reasons further of the great men who had rendered Warrington life illus. consideration of the matter had been deferred. They trious in the past. When one felt disgusted with the were still in the transition stage, and any alterations smallness of present day life and conditions it was made at the present time would probably have to be i pleasant to. reflect that n Warrington some of the revised before more than two or three years had passed. greatest men who ever adorned the pages of English his- What was quite as important was that the committee tory lived and did their work. He did not know whether had made liberal provision to enable acting teachers to there was any provision in the curriculum of any of our improve their qualifications by classes in that institu- schools for the teaching of local history, but to hismind tion. One danger which some of them had had it was just as important and imperative that unpleasantly forced upon them was that, not we should know the history of our own town only in Warrington but all over the country, and neighbourhood as that we should know the of some reaction of sentiment in regard to education, history of the country and the empire. It was only by owing to the increased burden which fell upon the rates. studying the past, learning its lessons, end avoiding its The Committee wanted to be verycareful not to attempt mistakes, that we were likely to make progress in the to go too quickly, but cautiously, to make sure that present and build up the future. If the teaching could every step they took was a real step forward. He was not be given systematically and specifically In school inclined to think that teachers might help to some extent hours, the teachers might adopt the plan of taking the to prevent reactionary tendencies in one or two direc- children on pleasant summer .rambles to the ancient tions. He took it for granted that every teacher really places he had mentioned. Then again,the more intelli- strove to carry out the purely teaching side of the work gent and,capable children should have more frequent to the best of his or her ability, but in addition every opportunities of visiting the,museum under the guid- teacher could help by strict care in regard to the use of ance of teachers qualified to point out the facts sag- materials, the prevention of waste Sta. The question gested by the numerous relics. In Canada and which presented itself to the mind of every intelligent some of the colonies " empire day " was person was : "We are spending an enormous annually observed, the children in the schools amount of money in this country on education. being taught specifically .on that occasion to Is this 'expense justified ?" Personally he had not celebrate the fact that they were citizens of this great ' the slightest doubt about the answer. He had not the empire. He did not see why the same idea should not ' slighest doubt that vast as were the sums being spent be applied here in regard to civic life. They might on annually, we might profitably spend more. The im- a special day celebrate the incorporation of the borough , portance of it from the point of view of personal profit or, as he would prefer it, the forming of the Roman ! was recognized by growing numbers of people who camp. He would ask his hearers to consider how much realized that in the keen competition of commerical and teachers might do to get children to love healthy business life, there was an increasing feeling that we pleasant, and agreeable surroundings. It was not were not as much to the fcire as we should be, that enough even that a town should be healthy. We wanted Germany, America and other countries were running us to have some signs of artistic life. It was a positive ' very hard indeed. His own keen desire to see real and reflection on Warrington that it had so few beautiful lasting educational progress rested rather on the buildings, and it was enough to make any enlightened ' patriotic lines put forward by Alderman Bennett. For citizen positively groan to go through the streets I good or for ill, we in this country had got democratic and see how ugly the generality of the houses I methods of governmentsnd surely it was of vital were. They should endeavour to make the children i importance that we should have a highly determined that they would not live in some of the educated democracy. It was not merely for our "hideous caricatures which passed as houses." Alder- I own town or country; it was not merely for the sake of man Bennett proceeded to urge the value of the cultiva- the vast Empire to which it was a source of pride to tion of knowledge for its own sake—the love of books, ' everyone of them to belong, but he held very firmly pictures, statues, music, and everything that made for ' that it was in the interests of humanity as a whole. a higher, nobler, and fuller life. As to teachers being I Whatever money was spent on edacation was going to debarred from entering the troubled arena of municipal ! bear fruit, and we might profitably spend even more in life, they might be very sincerely felicitated upon the the future than we had done in the past. (Applause). fact. It was a curious thing to him, although he was 1 THE WORKING OF THE ACT IN WARRINGTON. in the hurly-burly itself, ihow people should have any ambition to mix in the unseemly wranglings Which! I:IIPORTANT SUGGESTION. from time to time disgraced our civic Parliament and in the sordid anuabbles which formed so much of modern municipal life If teachers themselves could not enter the Town Council and try to improve things, they had the making very largely of the citizens of the future. If they wanted to better the Town Council, Education Committee, and Board of Guardians, the way was to train the children from youth upwards to have such high ideals that they would not be content with anyone to represent them except those who were in every way qualified and fitted to do so. (Applause.) Mr. Wa.m.fsGroN remarked that teachers could do a great deal to direct the reading of the scholars, but unfortunately they could not put the required books into their hands as they were not in the Library. They had been agitating for some time that a portion of the Library should be laid out specially for school children, but they had met with considerable opposition. Alderman BENNETT said that at their last meeting the Committee decided to inaugurate a school children's department. (Applause). A TEACHER'S IDEALS. THE TEACHING OF HYGIENE AND - TEMPERANCE. The Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON then addressed the gathering on "A teacher's ideals." He said that in books on the subject they generally found a very long string of virtues which were all massed together, and which they were informed every teacher ought to possess to the full (Laughter.) A teacher must be absolutely the soul of punctuality ; he must always be the perfection of neatness in his dress and behaviour ; he must, of course, be the pink of courtesy ; he must be always prepared to make his lessons intensely interesting ; he must have thoroughly prepared everything he was going to teach; he must—be he well or ill—always be bright, energetic and earnest in his work. (Laughter). His discipline,of course, while it was firm must none the less always be kindly—(laughter)—and he must never suffer himself to get impatient under the greatest provocation. (Laughter) As for losing his self-control, that must never be thought of for a Moment; and should he be called upon to punish, it must be done, not with any spark of even righteous indignation, but with measured forethought and absolute prudence. (Loud laughter). The effect Of that on his (the speaker's) mind was always a little bit depressing. As an old teacher, of many years experi- ence,perhaps,he had not come up to that yet,and he did not see very much chance of it,either.(Laughter.)The question he would put before them was, what could they—as men and women of the world, not angelic beings, not saints,Thut as people who, wished to do their best for the children, who wished to take the very highest view of their office and who fully realized the responsibility the office entailed—do to take the first step to make themselves a little better and fitter for the office than they had been in the past. What was the ideal of educa- tion in our country? He did not think anyone in the room could answer that question for it seemed to him that England was the only country in the world almost which had not got a recognized ideal for the education of its people. That being the case, they were all at liberty to make their own ideals, and every teacher in England had a right to search out and make an ideal for himself. The ideal he would place before them was to try and get their children to live their lives in the best possible way. They had beard a great deal lately about physical train- ing, and he thought it was a very good thing they had. In regard to the recent discussion on the teaching of hygiene and temperance he ventured to think the mem- bers of the committee Wad no difference as to the value of the subjects, but only as to the way in which they should be inculcated. A very important document had been sent round to the schools, bearing the names of almost all the great medical scientists and practitioners of the county, impressing upon teachers the necessity for giving special lessons on hygiene and temper- ance. In one of the local papers the Education Committee was a little bit taunted with the fact that it was not at all unlikely that the one authority which had put itself in opposition to Sir William Broadbent's memorial was that of Warrington. He was sure they would all be pleased to hear that he had in his possession a letter from Sir William Broad- bent which made it absolutely clear that Warrington was not the authority to which henlluded in his speech, and he quite understood the Education Committee's position, that while they did not see their way to make a regular time table lesson of "how to improve a child's ' health and physical culture" they sympathised with the Object and wished it to be carried oat, thpugh not in that particular way. In his (the speaker's) opinion, the personal influence of the teacher should be brought to bear upon the child for the improvement of its health and physical development, and all those things which made for health, such as cleanliness. Teachers were very much to be congratulated upon the fact that they had now a system of drill which was very ' much superior to that which they had a few years ago and which had been received with distinct approbation. In regard to mental training, were they not getting to see that the great thing they required in making their children fit to live their lives in the best possible way wes the developing of their intelligence? Were they not getting to see that it was not that they wanted to teach them in so many years so many facts ? What they desired was to put the children in a position which would enable them to know how to learn for themselves. and give them a thirst which would not be satiated till they had quenched it at the fountain of knowledge They ought to be thankful that the age had passed when a school was measured by the inspector with re- gard to the number of facts contained in the heads of the children. The inspector of the future would look to the teachers to see that, tho children showed intelli-</p>
        <p>IMe J. MOoRE MURRAY submitted an interesting ; paper on " The Working of the Education Act (1912) in I Warrington." He detailed the various duties of the Education Authority and its sub-committees and pointed out that the local committee was subject to the control Of a Central Authority which exercised particular vigilance in the matter of auditing accounts. As to the work of the School Attendance Department, nothing demoralised a school more, or caused greater worry and inconvenience than the absence of children, and any suggestions that could be made to improve the existing plan of enforcing school attendance would be eagerly welcomed. In June, 1902, the number of scholars on the books was 13,290, in June 1903, it was 13,577, in Juns 1901 is .vas 13,916, whilst the last return showed 14,038 in addition to which there were 200 children in the secondary, school who, had that school not been opened, would have been in the elementary schools. Thus, in two years time, the num- ber on the books had increased by, at least, 656, whilst within the same period the number of children under five years of age was less. Unfortunately, the average attendance had not increased at the same rate as the number on the books, but when he told them that some 236,430 attendances were lost last year end were accounted for by medical certificates, he thought they would agree that this want of increase in the average attendance was net due so much to the lack of energy as to the ravages of sickness. Whilst the increase in the number on the books had been 656, the number of attendances lost by children who had been away through members of the family being sick was equivalent to 225 children being absent every day, and- the total loss of attendance from sickness was equivalent to 576 children being absent every day during the school year. Daring the year 1904 the cases visited by the officers in carrying out this part of their duty amounted to 71,818. (Applause.) With reference to the policy of the com- mittee, it had been in the direction of organization of education in the borough. Their efforts had been devoted to establishing a system of education by which able and bright boys and girls of the artisan class might receive a good education up to the age of 15 or 16, and that there might also be an opportunity for the excep- tional boy or girl to go on and receive a good education up to the age of 17 or 18, then pass through to a luniversity; but the chief work of the committee was not to legislate, and endeavour to bring through the few bright children—they must not be forgotton—but to raise the ability of the average child. That must be done ; chiefly in the elementary schools. (Applause). Refer- ' mice was made to the working of the Act of 1902 inside the schools, and to the fact that a large number of scholars were found in classes considerably below those in which they should be for their age. The speaker contended that there should be co-ordin- ation of work between the infants school and the senior school, so that when a child was seven years of age, and was qualified for promotion, he might be transferred to the senior school at the end of the quarter, and thus avoidany delay in his progress through the school. The child might be nearly seven at the beginning of the year and be nearly eight when he entered the senior school. If the child entered the senior school at the age of seven he had at least six years of school life before him. If ho was an average boy, at present he passed up the school in yearly stages and finished in the atxth class or standard. 'Was it necessary that a child should wait until the end of the school year before he was promoted ? Was it not possi- Me that children were marking time waiting till the' end of the school year ? He did not advocate whole- sale promotions, say after quarterly examinations, but what he asked was that the better children should move more 'quickly through the schools. (Applause). The greatest danger new to the children was the over- crowding of new ideas. Whatever was done in that direction must be done in moderation ; and the same might be said of' all the proposals before them. He was not one of those who had lost hope of our educational system. They had that-material which would produce results that would compare with any other coun- try in the world. What they required on the one hand was a devotion to duty, and a realization that in them j rested, to a large extent, thd future welfare of this great Empire. On the other band, they must arouse in the populace a real understanding of educationmarrying I with it an interest which would go beyond the more saying and talking of education, an understanding which would assist the parents to exercise such an influence on their children that the children would themselves I realize the benefits of education. By education they I must understand not merely/the acquisition of know- ' lejge, not merely the power to express oneself, but the acquisition of all those qualities, mental, social and moral, which alono could bring real benefits to the individual and to the nation. Let them hope that the working of the Education Act, 1902, in Warrington would lead in the course of time to such results. (Applause.) The speakers were cordially thanked, on the motion of Mr. F. H. LONGS HAW, seconded by Mr. A. CORNEY and supported by Mr. J. DEWHURST. Mr: JOHN UPSON, in moving a vote of thanks to the Chairman, said that while there had been some mistakes in educational matters they had been fewer in Warring- ton than in other towns, and that was almost entirely due to the energy and influence of the co-optative mem- bers of the educational authority. (Hear, hear.) Mr. STANSPIELO seconded, and this proposition was also unanimously carried. After the meeting a tea and social were held in the Gymnasium, the catering being in the hands of the Winrnarleigh Cafe Company,Sankey-street.M us; cal items were rendered, and a whist drive proved a great success</p>
        <p>gence. As to the teaching of history, surely they could get out of the old rut. Let them teach the children the history of present (lay institutidns,—such for instance as the Police Force—passing from the known to the unknown and interesting the child in the history of his surroundings. lie cordially agreed with what had fallen from Alderman Bennett on this subject. Then again, how many things which they taught in arithmetig were absolutely useless. What the children needed was to know how to do certain things which would be useful to them in their lives. Lastly:he would remind them that _</p>
        <p>a change had come over public thought of late years as to the necessity of moral training. All the books now told them that the great function of the teacher was the formation of character—and it was a very great step that they should have got to see that. What was more, they were beginning to see how wonderfully the life of a school tended to form character, how each sub- ject, if properly taught, had a special bearing not only upon the mental but upon the moral side of the child. Teachers should all realize that every child had a great deal of good in him. When the child felt that the teacher recognized he was not altogether a "bad lot," but that there was good in him which would respond to the sunshine -of the teacher's encouragement, the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>CHILDREN'S COUGHS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S COUGHS.</p>
        <p>MRS. BALLIN, TEIE GREAT AUTHORITY ON CHILDREN'S DISEASES, STRONGLY RECOMMENDS VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
