      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WARRINGTON COMMITTEE'S ESTIMATES APPROVED.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COMMITTEE'S ESTIMATES APPROVED.</p>
        <p>Mr. MAY : And the results are worse. (Laughter.) r Tile estimates were then carried. TEACHERS AND RESIGNATIONS. THE USE OF THE GYMNASIUM. The Rev. MORLEY STEVESSOS, in moving the confir- mation of the minutes of the School Management Com- mittee, alluded to the resolution, "That all teachers wishing to resign their appointments be instructed to forward their resignations to the managers and to the committee at the same itime." A little ambable con-</p>
        <p>troversy had been goin.g'on between the managers of one of the schools and the committee on the point as to whether teachers, when they sent in resignations,should forward them to the managers or to the committee. A teacher should send his resignation to the managers of the school as a matter of order and courtesy, and the committee felt that it would be no hardship to send a copy of the same letter to the committee. The speaker alluded to the minute which read as follows :—"A letter was submitted from Miss E. Butterworth, assistant- mistress in the infants' deparment of the Warrington Parochial School, in explanation of her recent absence from duty through the illness of her sister. A com- munication was also submitted from the Managers of the Warrington Parochial school, calling attention to the absence of teachers from duty without sufficient cause. The Committee decided to take no further action in regard to the absence of Miss Butterworth, and a draft circular letter, which had been prepared by the Director in accordance with the instructions of the Committee at their last meeting, calling the attention of all teachers to this matter, was approved." A great deal of inconveuierice had been caused in schools by the I absence of teachers from duty, not from ill-health, ! which was provided for, but from other causes, and it ; was obvious that if a person was taking a I salary for a certain work, that work ought to be carried , out. There were exeeptional cases which might arise, ; and which would render it perfectly right that a teacher should leave on businnast The committee felt that somel communication should§ pass between the teacher and,' the managers, and that leave on business should be I properly applied for and granted when the circumstances were of such a character as to render it desirable. A sub-committee was recently appointed to consider the salaries of head teachers. Some of the committee felt that the scale had not worked in every case quite as smoothly as they wished, and it was intimated that before long the accommodation now, recognized in , many of the schools would be curtailed. When that I came into operation it would affect the whole scale, so. I far as head teachers were concerned, was based on the 1 number of children in attendance. They felt it woulc/ be unwise to open the question and unsettle the present scale when in all probability in a ft.w weeks it would be incumbent to re-consider it again, (Hear, hear.) L, regard to the proposed reorganization of Fairfield School the committee felt strongly, in the present state of the.; town—schools being overcrowded, and it being difficult to find places for the children—that it would not be a I wise step to lessen the accommodation in any given I school. They hoped, however, to carry out some I arrangement when the new sclnol was opened in Orford- I lane district. In the meantime they suggested to the m'anagers that from an edacational as well as from an economical point of view, it might be a wise step, to i group the upper standards n one of the schools and the lower standards in the other. The Rev. M, Stevenson. then alluded the following resolution.:—" The follow- ing recommendation of the sub-committee appointed to meet the Gymnasium Sub-committee was submitted, viz :—" That the School Management Committee be. recommended to make a payment of £2OO to the Gymnasium Committee in respect of the use of the Gymnasium and the instruction of the scholars from -the public elementary schools during the year ending the 31st Marc!, 1905 ; this payment being made witout prejudice to any future arrangements that may be decided upon." It was resolved that the sum of £2oo' mentioned in the above recommendation be reduced to £lOO, and that the recommendation, as amended, be approved and adopted." The Gymnasium had been used by some of the schools situated close to it for drilling , purposes. The School Management Committee did not feel justified in recommending a sum of £200.1 for the use of the Gymnasaim during the I . . . .</p>
        <p>past year, and they thought that if the £Bo' previously paid was raised to £lOO it would fairly represented the equivalent to the use of the institu- tion the pupils of the schools. As a rule the drilling of the children was better carried on in their own schools under the superintendence of theirlown teachers. That at least was the opinion of the committee. In the training colleges every teacher, male and female, was receiving the most careful instruction in the best principles of physical training,, and while they very much valued the use ot the Gymnasium for pupil teachers and the elder people, who would profit more lay gymnastic exercises, the physical instructiGn of the young children was best kept in the hands of their own. teachers, to be given in the schools and playground. Councillor CRAIK seconded the confirmation of the minutes. Councillor TINNION moved an amendment to the effect that the resolution relating to the payment of £lOO be not confirmed. It was not his intention to go into the question mentioned by Mr. Stevenson that there was better drilling in the school yards. There might be under different conditions but not under the various conditions that obtained in Warrington, They might have a teacher here and a teacher there, who knew some- thing about drill ; one might have a class certificate and another know practically nothing about the require- ments of the department. If drill could be done during certain times of the year in the playground it would be a great surprise to him. The sum of £2OO, he would point out, was for services already rendsred and would not fully recompense the committee for the amount expended. If the sum of £2OO was not paid in this case he should bring the matter under the no-ice of the Town Council. He had not the least hesitation in saying that if he did not get satisfaction at the meeting of the Education Committee he should get it at the 'meeting of the Council. In answer to Mr. Upson, Councillor TINNION said the schools making use of the Gymnasium were Trinity, Warrington British, Wycliffe, Heathside, and S. James's. Altogether last year there were over 100,000 attendances and if the work was not worth £2OO he did not know what was. After some further discussion, the amendment was carried with one dissentient. Mr. FiraCLotrox alluded to thefollowing minute : " It was noted that the infants' department of the War- rington Parochial School had been closed by the local sanitary authority through the prevalence of infectious disease in the district for one month." It was thought the holidays were excessive, and he would like to ask the committee to consider whether any school that had to be closed on account of disease' was entitled to the ordinary holidays recommended. There would be 10 weeks' holiday altogether during the year. It seemed to him there would be a serious loss td the town if they closed the schools for disease and for the usual holidays as well. The DEPUTY-CHAIRMAN said there were practical difficulties in the way of carrying out the suggestion, but it would be considered. The Rev. M. STEVENSON, in reply, said he was sure the committee would be very pleased to consider the matter, but if the Medical Officer ordered them to close 'a school they were bound to do so. The practical diffi- culty of giving holidays to all the schools except one was very great, because it meant that the education and 'attendance officers had to be kept to their duty and it would be no easy matter to get the children of one 'school to attend while others were enjoying holidays. Mr. UPSON : The holidays are given to the children and not to the teachers. (Laughter.) Mr. EAIRCLOUGH : They get them too. (Laughter.) The minutes were then agreed to with the exception named. ATTENDANCE INSPECTORS AND UNIFORMS. At a meeting of the School Attendance Committee some discussion having taken place as to the desirability of providing uniforms fr r the schcol attendance inspec- tors, it was resolved ti tt no action be taken in the matter. ICouncillor ELLISON moved the confirmation of the minutes, and said he was pleased to be able to report that there had been an increase in the average attend- , ance during the month. Mr. BIBBY seconded, and the motion was carried. THE "BOLTON" COUNCIL SCHOOL. At a meeting of the Sites and Buildings Committee held on the 10th inst., the competitive plans for thenea- .. _</p>
        <p>" Bolton " Council 'school were opened, and it MN decided to request the Borough Surveyorand the Direc- tor to consider and report upon them to a special meet-</p>
        <p>ing of the committee. At a meeting on the 17th February, in accordance with the decision of the com.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Position of The Grammar School.</head>
        <p>The Position of The Grammar School.</p>
        <p>The Elucation Committee's estimates, involving a l total education rate of Is. were approved at the monthly meeting of the autuority held in the Town Hall, on Monday evenin,g. In the absence, through illness of Mr. F. Monks, the chair was occupied by Alderman Bennett (vice-chairman). There were also gresent :—Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Burton, Barton, Craik, Ellison, Evans, Lord, Maginn, Sankey, Fulton Smith, Tinnion, The Revs. M. Stevenson, N. Wilson, and J. Yonge, Messrs. W. Bibby, J. D. Buckton, It. 'f. Fairclough, A. J. Jolley, EL Kingdon, T. May, C. E. Parker, E. Plinston, C. H. Unsworth, J. Upson, and G. Walker, with Mr. J. Moore Murray (director) and Mr. John Fairclough (treasurer). THE ESTIMATES EXPLAINED. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY moved theadoption of the estimates which showed a total deficiency of £11843 7s. Id. as against £10,407 19s. 7d., in the previous year. Explaining the estimates of each sub-committee in I detail, be pointed out that the salaries of school I attendance officers showed a slight decrease as com- pared with last year. That was due to the vacancy caused by the death of Mr. Jones, the chief officer, not having been filled up,the duties of the department being re-arranged. On the income side they would MO that the Attendance Committee expected to receive £2O 14s. as parental contribu- tions in respect of blind, deaf and dumb children. The committee, he would like to say, were giving attention to that item, and there was likely to be a larger sum demanded from the parents in future. It was very im- portant that consideration should be given to the mat- ter, because it was no part of the local authority's duty tto do anything which would lessen the sense of parental responsibility. ([leer, hear). What- ever the committee did in that direction would not materially affect the amount at preseneset down. In- the School Management Committee's higher education estimates the salaries showed a small increase, mainly due to increases of staff, consequent upon a larger number of pupils. With reference to other estimates the Secondary School expenditure was estimated to increase from £1,138 to £1,381; the School of Art from £307 to £387; and the Technical Institute from £1,014 to £l,lBl. The amount required for Evening Continuation Schools was £536, as against £660 last year, the decrease being due, he was sorry to say, to the proposed discontinuance of one of the schools. The members would notice that £390 had been set aside ai a possible grant to the Grammar School. He thought they would agree with him that if the Grammar School wig to take its proper place in the educational system of the borough some expense mast be incurred, and the School Management Committee and the Finance Committee were of opinion that it was desirable to insert some amount to make provision for possible requirements. It must be clearly understood,however, that before any grant was paid the scheme of the school would have to be amended to meet the requirements of the committee, and he apprehended that the committee and Town Council would insist on being thoroughly satisfied that what they considered proper reqtffrentents were fully met, and that any changes that would take place should be real reforms,atid not mere changes. There had been a change of sentiment, he believed on the part of the governors of the school and of their attitude towards the Educa- tion Committee as compared withseven or eight months ago, but from what he was told by those who attended the recent conference there seemed to be a prospect of the governors acting in what the committee considered a more reasonable manner—that meant they were com- ing round to the committee's view. Alderman SMETHURST : You are ccraing round to their's, _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY. continuing his remarks on the School Management Committee's estimates, said that on the receipt side for higher education the amount expected in grants for the Technical Institute, had gone up from £3OO to £4 5O, and was partly due to increased numbers, but mainly to the better results obtained of late. (Hear, hear.) Altogether the deficiency was £2,292, as against £1,113 last year, but had it not been for the the £3OO set aside for the Grammar School, that defici- ency would have been decreased instead of increased. With regard to the elementary education estimates,they required for :teachers salaries £25,7,79, com- pared with £2,017, an increase of £1,700. That was partly due to the normal increase of salaries according to scale, and partly to the staffs of the school having been improved and brought to the required standard. There was only one school in the town now which was below the minimum requirements of the Board of Education, and he thought that showed a very satisfactory condition. He did not think the estimates were likely to be exceeded. No day school prizes would be given this year as the committee did not think they were needed. Instruction in drill, for which £2OO had again been put down, as a question which had given rise to some discussion, and he was sorry to say there was some friction between that com- mittee and the Gymnasium Committee. There was a conference recently and the members agreed upon cer- tain terms. Those terms, he was sorry to say, had not proved acceptable to the School Management Com- mittee. The matter would come up rater on for con- sideration, but whatever was decided it would not affect the estimates so far as the ratepayers were concerned, being merely a question of adjustment. On the receipt side for elementary education the grants were estimated at £12,197, as against £12,342 last year, as there :was reason to believe that their previous estimate was not going to work out as they had expected„owing to the prevalence of sickness, ecc. Ho thought the present estimates, however, would be within the mark. There was ho change in the amount put down for fees (£1,698). Altogether the estimated deficiency in respect ok elementary schools was £1,720, an increase of nearly £1,390. That was due almost entirely to improvements of staff and remuneration. He ventured to say that they treated teachers well in Warrington—(hear, hear)—and he thought they were entitled. in return, to receive their best and most loyal service. Turning to the Finance Committee's estimates, the officers' salaries showed an increase from £1,173 to £1,337, which was partly due to £lOO of the borough surveyor' i salary being charged to the authority. They had put down £985 for interest and instalments on the Technical Institute loans, £3OO for interest on loans for the new Bolton and fleamont Schools, and £925 as an instalment on account of the working balance. Regarding the daet item, it showed , a alight increase. When the new authority came into existence it started with an empty exchequer, and power was granted to them to raise a loan until such time as the earliest grants became payable. As a matter of fact 1 they managed to get along'without the loan, but they 1 were under an obligation to build up a working lenience lof £6,000 in the chum of nine years, and the £925 was lan instalment. They would have to provide a similar amount for the next six years. The total deficiency of the Committee was £7,218, As, against £6,957. In the Sites and Building Committee's estimates there was `nothing of special-interest, but there waa a net- decrease in theamount, required of £2OO. Taking the estimates as a whole there was a deficiency of k. 11,813, compared with £10,407. which, he thought, was satisfactory, con- sidering the lee-way theyhad to make up in educational matters in Warrington. Mr. Jolley quoted figures show- ing that the grants estimated for would be equal to about two and a half times the amount raised for education out of tha local rates. Perhaps the grants they would receive would exceed the estimates, but it was not safe te build on it. die would remind the members that the methods of grant payment were precarious, and any marked ciaange might seriously hamper them. Hitherto the system had been very helpful.So far as could be judged at the present time, the net result of last year's working would, he thought, be satisfactory. It was impossible to speak with perfect confidence, but so far as he could judge, after conferring with the director and the Borough Treasurer, their last year's estimates would work out pretty closely. There would be a discrepancy in the amoant of the grants and also a small difference in salaries, but altogether the estimates. would work out</p>
        <p>about right. II conclusion, he would like to thank the Director and the Borough Trelsurer for their valuable assistance in getting out the estimates, which had been</p>
        <p>- . most carefully prepared. (Hear. hear.) Mr. R. T. FAIRGLOUG.I/ seconded the estimates.</p>
        <p>Mr. IVl.tv remarked that the ability with which the linittee 'Fit their last meeting, the Surveyor iinci-tlTe estimates were drawn up was quite evident, but it I Director reported upon their inspection of the competi- seemed to him—and he had read and studied many a tive plans received for the new " Bolton "Council budget statement—Oat they could not discover from school, Latchford. The envelopescontaining the specifi- them in any way what the actual cost of education cations were then opened,and the meeting was adjourned during the rtast year had been. Nowhere in the estimates until four o'clock on Friday, the 24th February, in order could they get the actual cost of education, or any item that they may be examined by the Surveyor and the of education. • Director. The DEPUTY-CIIIIRMAN: It is not possible to give On the motion of Mr.FAIRCLOTJGH, seconded by Conn- it because the financial year does not end until March 31. oilier TINsioN, the minutes were carried. Mr. MAY: Then I take it we are promised that , GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE. statement when the time comes? I At a meefing held on Monday evening, it was noted The DIREcTott: The state;uent is published and that the following gentlemen had been appointed loon- autlited by the Local Govarnment Board Andterl every dation managers and correspondents to the managers of year. the schools mentioned, viz. :—(a) Fairfield School : The Mr. l‘elxy, continuing his remarks, said he noticed Rev. G. A. Dearden, 2, Salisbury-Erreet (in place of the that th 3 grants were estimated to produce less than last I the Rev. A. S. Cameron, resigned) ; (b) Ladies year, and yet they found that the cost of education was School of Industry : The Rev. T. W. Barber. a, to be increased by £1,400; the earning power of the I Bewsey-road (in place of the Rev. G. W. Dickinson 2 education I resigned. The following report of the special sub- teachers was diminished, but the cost of education, I committee appointed to consider the proposed transfer mainly for their ealaries, was to be increased. That of the Silver-street Wesleyan School to this committee seemed to him a very extraordinary state of affairs, and I was approvqd and adopted, viz.:—" Since their last he thought they were beginning at the wrong end. They I report the sub-counnitieo have met once, viz., on the "ought to improve the schools and education generally 130th January, 1905, when the Surveyor presented his before they launched out on increases,of salary. With I vivation of the Silver-street Wesleyan School, and reference to the £3OO in respect of the Grammar School, 'affer careful consideration, it was resolved that, in the he was present at the conference, and he saw no attempt opinion of this committee, it is inexpedient at present ; on the part of the gentlemen who represented the to to entertain the suggestion to purchase the Silver-street governing body to pauperize the Grammar School or $ Wesleyan School.' to bring it on the rates, but there was a strong desire The minutes were confirmed, on the motion of the to benefit education, and tO meet the committee's DEPUTY-CHAIRMAN, seconded by Mr.PLINSTON. representatives. The inspector strongly objected to a THE ANNUAL MEETING. reduction of the fees paid by the boys. The DEPPUTY-CfIAIRNIAN moved :—" That, in view Mr. UpsoNsaid that with regard to Mr. May's question of the fact that the Ist April falls this year on a as to who had benefited from the increased expenditure. Saturday, the standing orders of the committee be he would liko to say again that the children had benefited varied so sto allow of the annual meeting of the because they were now taught by properly trained :and Education Committee being held on Wednesday, the certificated teachers, instead of by partially-trained or 20th March, instead of on that date." unqualified teachers or pupil teachers as previously. I Councillor ELLISON seconded, and the motion was ' Hear, hear.) I carried.</p>
