        <p>BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME.</p>
        <p>31E1X.234130 XAM-STX.</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>xxvi, RUN-THROUGHS.—Continued.</p>
        <p>The nearer the cue ball is to the first object C. hall th . greater the allowance that must bE made for paCe, when playing a strong run- through stroke. When the cue ball is only twc u• three inches from the object ball unless a • 'pry full contact be made a strong stroke will throw the cue ball off almost like an ordinary half bail stroke.</p>
        <p>Thi ean heeßsily tested hy plaeillg the ball as shown in diagram 64. Played with a stron; i=troke, UlilCss the red ball be struck almc,4 full, the cue ball will pass on the ontside of the white\ ball (as shown by the intersected line).</p>
        <p>DIACIRAM 65. :ENTZE POCKET STRONG R.</p>
        <p>A forclng run-through in-off. 0b.,....ct :1 inchas from the side cushion and CZ inch ,:•A the tan cushion. Cue ball on the @claml line of the table and 37 inches from the top cmilikm.</p>
        <p>'The stroke shown on Diagram 65 is a very good example of the allowance that ran 4 b.? inade for high pace when playing a ittn-throu;li strok&lt;?. The position is such that a slow or medium pace run-through would lenvr.4 tiv-s objet white in baulk. If, however, the stroke he play-A with plenty of pace and the object ball be Int exactly the same as it should be for a slow or medium pace rub-through, the cue bill will lie deflected very markedly, and instead of finding the pocket will strike the cushion several inches above the pocket. . .</p>
        <p>In the position shown on the diagram the run- through should be playea with plenty of pace and a very full contact should be made with the obiecr ball, in fact aim should be taken all but full on to the object ball. The cue hall should be struck above the centre and without side. -</p>
        <p>lIAGRAM 66.</p>
        <p>very pretty fancy stroke.—A cannon aftcr I'U:titling through two object balls. Cue ball 9 inches from the side cushion and 12 inches from the baulk cushion, first object ball touching the side cushion. and M inches from the baulk cushion. Second object ball touching the side Cushion and 35- inches from the baulk cushion. Fourth b 41.1 3 inches from the side cushion and 62 inches from the ha.ulk cushion.</p>
        <p>Diagram 66 illustrates an exceedingly Pretty fancy run-through cannon. Four belts are used in this stroke, the cue ball cannoning Onto the fourth ball after running through two object balls. The stroke requires no side, but Plenty of strength must be used, and the cue ball must be struck high up in order that as Inuch forward rotation as possible may be im- Darted to it. In playing this stroke the cue ball should be placed in such a position that it makes a considerable angle with the first object ball and the cushion (as shown on the diagram), for Should the str)ke be attempted with the cue ball too much behind the first object ball tin- cue ball after running through the first .ball Will (if the stroke be well played cling to the cushion the way to the second object ball, and b catching that ball quite full Cr almost ftill in its rear, will drive on to the third object ball and the kiss will, of course, spoil the stroke. lnste.acl, by playing the stroke with the cue ball Well away from the cushion, the combined effect of top and strength will cause the cue ball to describe a slight curve after striking the first cibieet ball. The second object ball, by reason of this curve, is thus struck at a slight angle, SO that instead of running along the cushion and kissing the next ball it comes away from the enfiltion and leaves a clear path for the cue bell te cannon on to the third object ball. By plac- tug the last ball a ball's distance from the tUshion the chances of making this fancy stroke are greatly increased, for even should the cue ball not cling to the cushion after running through the second object ball the cannon may still be made. In fact, as the cue ball will sel- dom cling jo the cushion after running through the second 'object ball, the third object ball may with advantage be placed rather more thaii a ball's distance from the cushion. In playing this fancy run-through stroke the elle ball will sometimes make such a curve that El. will miss the second object ball altogether and.</p>
        <p>cannon direct on to the third object ball, Zllld sometimec, the curve may be 6o exceptic=ily pronounced that both the belie will, be mi•(3,7d, and the cue ball will strike the some distance beyond the last ball. this -111 posibihity exists of thecn ii fl4_,ven. going into the top pocket after curvi:,g it the two balls. . . _</p>
        <p>(To he. Continu3d.)</p>
        <p>Vol. I. of "Billiards: The Strokes of the Game." 100 illustrations. By RISO LEVI. i iaow ready, Is. net.</p>
