      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS.</head>
        <p>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>A Sisson (Huddersfield). If during the course of a game the referee spots the red ball and the striker, thinking the ball is not properly spotted,takes it up and spots it himself, his opponent can claim the next shot as a foul, in case any score is made. The player who has made the foul can, however, play for what he likes and in this case he would, of course, play for safety. Such a foul is, however, seldom claimed, but, on the other hand, few players in an important game would touch the red. If in the opinion of the striker the ed has no t been broperly spotted he can request the referee to place the ball properly on the spot. C. A. Ingram (Rotherham).—F. Lawson, the Sheffield professional, has made several breaks of over 200. A few weeks ago he made a break of 209 playing against John Roberts, and still more recently in a game with A. Raynor, to whom he was giving 1,200 in 9,000, he made a break of 228, which is his record. This match was played with Crystalate balls, and though Lawson played rather indifferently at the start, as soon as he got used to the balls he played better than he has done before, and after having a great deal the worst of the game, he won by 349. How well he played may be judged by the following particulars :—On the Thursday afternoon he scored 1,012, onthe Friday afternoon 1,152, on the Saturday afternoon b 172, and at the closing session, on Saturday evening, 1,104. These scores would have been good ones, even for Stevenson or Dawson. Speaking from memory, no less than nine professionals have beaten their records with Crystalate balls during the last eighteen months, which says a great deal for the excellence of these balls. . _</p>
        <p>T. Moffit (Ayr).—The red only goes on the pyramid spot when it will not spot on the billiard spot. A. Naylor (Southport).--Balls 24- in. diameter are over size. The standard size is 2 1-16 in. to 2 3-32 in.</p>
        <p>W.N. (Rochdale).—Lancashire meet Yorkshire at Leeds on Saturday, February 25th, in the inter-county championship. The teams are three aside, and the games are 500 up, and victory goes to the team that scores most points. N. Drummond (Glasgow).—lf you will forward me a stamped addressed envelope I will answer your questions by post. I cannot reply to them in this column. RISO LEVI.</p>
        <p>ST. CLEMENT'S V. VOLUNTEER SERGEANTS.— Played at St. Clement's Club. IST. V.R. (P.W.V.) SOUTH ST. CLEMENT'S LANCS. REG T. SERG TS. J. Wolfenden lOO Sergeant Allardice ... 35 W. Holmes lOO Sergeant Potter E. Jones _ 73 Sergeant Goulding... 100 _ -</p>
        <p>E. Critchley H. C. Rhoden lOO Sergeant Russell P. 11, Simkin lOO Sergeant Wilcock 6B</p>
        <p>Majority for St. Clement's, 176. Played at the Sergeants Mess Drill Hall. SERGEANTS 2ND. ST. CLEMENT'S 2ND. Sergeant Owen lOO W. Carter Sergeant Fern Sergeant Bebbington... 100 T. Higham .,. 21 Sergeant Houghton... .. 100 A. Evans B4 Sergeant Spencer lOO R. Pattison 63 Sergeant Connor • 591 376 . Majority for Sergeants 2nd, 215.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>VICAR'S EXPERIENCE.</head>
        <p>A VICAR'S EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>Tells How Bile Beans Cured Him.</p>
        <p>Advice to Sufferers from Sick-Headache and Biliousness.</p>
        <p>In thousands of instances every day throughout this land of ours the visit of a kindly vicar brings comfort and consolation to sufferers. When, however, a vicar can offer advice which relieves bodily suffering as well as mental, welcome indeed are his visits. Such is the happy position of the Rev. H, Minton- Senhouse, Vicar of St.Cuthbert's Church, Birmingham. He has personally proved the great value of Bile Beans for biliousness and sick-headache, and is now able to point those parishioners who suffer from these ailments to a means of cure.</p>
        <p>- Not only so, but he has availed himself of the means of speaking to a wider congregation by permitting a statement of the facts of his cure to be published. In a letter addressed to the Bile Bean Co., he states briefly, but clearly? how he came to try Bile Beans ; and he tells also how highly satisfactory was the result. The follow- ing is a copy of his letter :- 155, City Road, Birmingham Nov. 11th, 1001,</p>
        <p>"To Messrs. The Bile Bean Co., Dear Sirs, I have derived so much benefit from Bile Beans and have known so many cures wrought by them that I feel ought to testify to their worth. For several years I suffered from sick-headaches and biliousness. One afternoon I was feeling so ill from these ailments, that my looks were noticed by several people I visited. At two houses I heard of how Bile Beans had effected cures in cases similar to my own. In one case, the man had been unable to work for some' time; had tried various remedies in vain,and had been cured ultimately by Bile Beans. I resolved to try this remedy. At the end of a week I was feeling better, and after a short course of the Beans I found myself quite cured. Now I am never without the remedy in the house. In the interests of other sufferers you have my permission to publish this letter. Faithfully yours, (Signed) H. Minton-Senhouse, Vicar of St. Cuthbert's, Birmingham." The above is by no means an isolated case of a clergy- man publicly expressing his appreciation of the merits of Bile Beans. Scientists, clergy, nurses, and medical men'all unite in praisingthis great vegetable household remedy. Bile Beans, while mild in their operation, are at the same time wonderfully effective. They act directly upon the liver and stomach, and tone up these organs to carry out their natural functions. Liver and stomach disorders are the root cause of most of the common ailments; and by their action on these vital organs, Bile Beans cure biliousness, sick headache, indigestion, wind, dizziness, debility, constipation (yet cause no griping), piles, anmmia, female ailments, bad breath buzzing and ringing noises in the head, etc. All chemists stock Bile Beans zit Is. I id. or 2s. 9d. per box (2s. 9d. box contains three times is. lid. size.) Avoid all sub- stitutes. There is nothing "just as good." Bile Beans are not sold loose—only in sealed boxes.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. On Tuesday night at the Old Academy Mr. F. V. L. Mathias presided over a good number of members gathered together to hear Mr. C. H. L. Jackson, of the Rotary Company, lecture on methods of printing and developing the various papers and tissues of that firm. Tr-colour photography was-dealt with in an exhaustive and ipteresting manner, many beautiful examples being shown. She lecturer drew special attention to the latest achievement in the carbon pro- cess; the rotary carbon stripping films by means of which the process is much simplified; the gelatine and pigment is coated on thin celluloid; the advan- tage of this is obvious. By putting the celluloid side of the tissue in contact with the negative a single transfer print is obtained without reversal. The lecturer also demonstrated the beautiful simplicity of obtaining various colours of lantern slides without resource to toning. Mr. Jackson was accorded a hearty vote of thanks for his very able lecture. The Secre- tary anunounced that at the next meeting, March 7th, be P. N. Prize slides would he shown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
