      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>INTERVIEW WITH THE REV. THOMAS LAW.</head>
        <p>INTERVIEW WITH THE REV. THOMAS LAW.</p>
        <p>The meetings of what may well be termed the Parliament of the Free Churches are to take place in Manchester on March 6th, 7th, Bth, and 9th, and Free Church representatives from overy part of the country are already preparing to visit the Cotton City to take their place at the gatherings. A representative of this paper was able to secure an interview with the Rev. Thomas Law, who, as Secretary of the National Free Church Council, is in charge of the arrangements for the Annual Gatherings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>NATIONAL REPRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL REPRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>Our reporter asked if the attendances at the Council would be as large as usual. "I think I can safely say that they will be larger. At the present moment the number of delegates registered at our office as coming to the meetings is actually in excess, of the number attending any previous Council. Man- chester is such a central city, and the pro- gramme is Pl 3 important, that Free Church Councils everywhere, have recognised the necessity of being represented. I may say that whilst we have of late years had to curtail the number of personal members, there is still the opportunity for a few to attend the meet- ings in this way. The conditions are member- ship of an Evangelical Free Church Council, and a subscription of ;is. This entitles the holder to sit with the representatives at all the Sessions, and to a'recluction of the railway charges to a faro and a quarter for the return journey from any part of the kingdom."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>IMPORTANT DEBATES.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT DEBATES.</p>
        <p>"What is the most important feature on the programme, Mr. Law p", "That would be very difficult to say. Both last year and in 1003 the Education Question greatly predominated, and of course it will be an important feature this year. But a matter of perhaps equal importance will be the con- sideration of a cOnstructive policy with regard to Temperance Reform. Some momentous pro- posals will be laid before the Assembly on March S. Another question which will excite interest arises out of the Scottish Church entanglement. Mr. J. Compton Rickett, M.P., D.L., will introduce the subject of The Crisis in Free Church Trust Deeds.' Dr. Rainy, the United Free Church champion, is preaching the Council sermon. Then we ace to discuss The Sunday School Problem '—Dr. Monro, Gibson and Mr. J. C. Meggitt, J.P., are to deal with this—and The Institutional' Church,' by Rev. C. Silvester Horne, M.A., and Rev. J. E. Rattenbury. Mr. R. W. Perks, M.P., and Rev. R. Haerison (President of the Primitive Methodist Conference) are dealing with the important question, of 'The Growth of Monastic Establishments in Great Britain."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>PRESIDENT'S. PRONOUNCEMENT.</head>
        <p>PRESIDENT'S. PRONOUNCEMENT.</p>
        <p>" Of course you know," continued Mr. Law, "that Dr. R. F. Horton is our new President. He succeeds the Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., who has done more in his year of office than perhaps any previous President. This is saying a great deal, for all our Presidents have rendered splendid service. Dr. Horton is expected to give an important Presidential pronouncement. As he takes a great interest in foreign mission work, I believe he wall make this one of the features of his address. Dr. Charles Wenyon is also bringing mission work before the Assembly; he is to read a paper on The National Council and Foreign Missions, with special reference to the Federation of Mission Work.' ''</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>DEVOTIONAL TOPICS.</head>
        <p>DEVOTIONAL TOPICS.</p>
        <p>"I suppose, Mr. Law, that the devotional side of the movement will be well to the front ?" "Most decidedly. In addition to the Council sermon, we are having sermons by Rev. F B. Meyer, 8.A., Rev, Dinsdale T. Young, Rev. J. G. Greenhough, M.A., and on Wednesday evening a series of preaching services will be held, at which the preachers are to be Rev. W. Y. Fullerton, Rev. George Jackson, 8.A., Rev. J. D. Jones, M.A., 8.D., Rev. J. H. Jowett, M.A., Mr. W. R. Lane, Rev. Dr. Campbell Morgan, Rev. J. Tolefree Parr, and Rev. C. Ensor Waters. At the Devotional Session on Thursday afternoon—which is to be held at the. Central Hall—addresses are to be given by Rev. S. Chadwick and Rev. Charles Brown," while the Con,munion Address will be delivered by Rev. H. Arnold Thomas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SOCIAL GATHEItINOS.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL GATHEItINOS.</p>
        <p>"Will the social side of the meetings be as pronounced as usual F" " Well, we are having quite a number of re- ceptions. First, there is the reception by the Lord Mayor at the Town Hall. 'Pie Man- chester Town Hall is perhaps the finest building in the city. Delegates to the Council mill no doubt eagerly embrace the opportunity of pay- ing a visit to the celebrated John Itylands' Library, where Mrs. Rylands is giving a recen- otion to the delegates on 'March 7. Then in addition to &amp;reception to Council Seetetaries given at the Midland Hotel by . the Mayor of Salford, Mrs. Jesse Haworth is giving a re- ception to Girls' Guild workers at Cavendish Chapel."</p>
        <p>NEW DEVELOPMENTS.</p>
        <p>"I must not omit to mention a few of the new developments Nwhich the National Conned is entering upon. For instance, the question of the Young n. Churchmen's Auxiliary is to be brought before the Dotice of the Assembly by the Rev. R. J. Campbell, M.A., and Bev. Thomas Phillips, 8.A., whilst Dr. Campbell Morgan, who has ananged a setits-of visits in connection with our local Councils, is to intro- duce the special subject he will deal with on these occasions—viz., "The place of the Bible in the Work of the Chur-zhes." You will see that these ale only a selection of the topics at the meetings, and that others of equal import- ance will be introduced."</p>
        <p>Mr. Law handed our representative a pro- gramme.. This shows that the Sessions will be packed with interesting items. Among those taking part we notice names of such well- known men as Rev. Dr. Clifford, Mr. D. Lloyd George, M.P., Dr. Rendel Harris, Mr. W. P. Hartley, J.P., Rev. W. Hardy Harwood, Roy. Joseph Hocking, Rev. Silas K. Hocking, Rev. C.. Silvester Horne, ; Rev. E. Griffith Jdnes, 8.A., Rev. J. H. Jowett, M.A., Rev. C.' H. Kelly, Rev. H. Elvet Lewis, Rev. W. Scott Lidgett, M.A., Rev. J. Gregory Mantle, Rev. John McNeill, Dr. Hope Moulton, Mr. E. C. Rawlings, Rev. G. Johnston Ross, M.A., Gipsy Smith, Mr. W. T. Stead, Rev. J. G. Stevenson, Rev. Dr. Townsend, Rev. James Travis, Mr. Robert Whyte, Alderman George White, M.F, c..:1 Rev. John Wilson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Small Coasideration for Cyclists.</head>
        <p>Small Coasideration for Cyclists.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woodward presided over the fortnighly meet- ing of this Council which was held at Dutton Work- 'house on 'Tuesday. There were also present :—Messrs. G. Pilling (vice-chairman),C. Rovlance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, G. Egerton Warburton, J. 'Stokes, T. Baxter,H.M. Davies, C. E. Linaker, J. H. Davies,J. Smith, Taright, T. Guest, S. Woodward, W. Harrison, S. Horton, J. L. Jones, E. Bretherton, C. Horton, J. Spruce. J. Hallftws, R. Billington, J. Mountield, W. Turner, A. G. Guest, J. Whitley, W. E. Hough (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medi- cal officer of health), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (sur- veyors), and J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances).</p>
        <p>THE HELSBY WATERWORKS. —PEC ULI Ali SITUATION It was reported that at the meeting of the fielsby Parochial Committee the Clerk read the following resolution of the Council :—" That it be an Instruction to the Helsby Parochial Commitee that the resolution of the Council passed on December 27th with respect to the Helsby Waterworks No. 1 contract be adopted." It was unanimously resolved that the Corumiitee having heard from two of Mr. Thomas Guest's principal sup- porters at the recent election of a District Councillor that theelection was not fought on the grounds set out in the newspaper report of the meeting of the District Council of January 24th, and being of opinion that the Council's resolution with regard to the contract was passed under a misapprehension, they (the Committee) would take no action in the matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Guest moved that the Helsby Committee's minute 3 be confirmed with the exception of the resolu- tion above quoted. Mr. A. G. Guest seconded. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>—. —.— The Chairman did not think it would be in the interests of the dignity and standing of the Council to adopt that course. He suggested that the Council should accept the recommendation, and then proceed to deal with the situation themselves. Ultimately the minutes were confirmed and the chair- man, vice-chairman, and Mr. Linaker were appointed a sub-committee to wait upon the chairman and vice- chairman of the Parochial Committee with the object of coming to an amicable arrangement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ALLEGED NETI67d.NCE.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED NETI67d.NCE.</p>
        <p>Messrs.Thomas-iiiii;i:ml§CM—Lim—ited, of Frodsham Bridge. wrote complaining of a nuisance at thelfront of the Aston Arms hotel and suggesting a remedy. It was suggested that the clerk should write to the owner of the property on the matter, but Mr. Linalter intimated that it should have attention. CYCLISTS' WARNING NOTICES. COUNCIL DECLINE TO TA Kr: RESPONSIBILITY.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Cyclists' fouring Club wrote with reference to the danger and caution boards. The club had, he said. for many years past erected through- out the United Kingdom, often at the request of the local governing bodies and the recommendation of coroners' juries, warning notices upon dangerous hills as well as upon hills and at abrupt turnings which required caution in negotiating with the result that a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>TY, INDIGESTION, &amp;c.</head>
        <p>TY, INDIGESTION, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>the prefent time the club had over 4,000 such notices in position. Now that the legislature nad seen fit to place the responsibility of erecting notices upon public authorities the time had, in the opinion of his Council, arrived when the club might with fairness ask to be relieved of the cost of maintaining such notices out of the funds. He would be glad for the matter to be brought before the Rural Council and learn whether or not they were disposed to assume responsibility for the upkeep of thirteen boards in that district. Mr. G. Egerton-Warborton moved that the Clerk should intimate to the C.T.C. that all the notice boards must be put in good order before the Council could consider the matter of assuming responsibility. A member remarked tbAt seeing that cyclists did so little towards keeping the roads in repair they might, lat least, protect themselves without calling on the Council to do so.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker : If the notices are on the highways I suggest that the C.T.C. be told to put them in order or remove them. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Baxter: I don't see what we have to do with them. Cyclists don't contribute anything towards the maintenance of the roads and I don't think we ought to begin putting up anything for the benefit of cyclists. I object to them altogether. (Laughter.) Ultimately the Council decided not to accede to the C.T.C.'s request. —lt was resolved that tenders be invited for the re- painting of finger posts in the Council's district. REALTII\ OF THE DISTRICT. . . .</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams reported two cases of diphtheria at Stockton Heath, one at Frodsham, and one at Frodsham Lordship. In connection with the outbreak of scarlet fever and diphtheria at Weston and Weston Point, two additional cases of fever and one of diphtheria had been notified. At Astmoor there was one fresh case of scarlet fever. The smallpox hospital at Moore had been closed after thorough disinfection. STHE PROPOSED MORTUARY AT WESTON. It was reported that at a meeting of the Weston Parochial Committee on the nth, Mr. H. S. Bamber presiding,a letter was received from Mr. Saner,engineer to the Weaver Navigation Trustees, suggeiting certain alterations to the plan and specification of the proposed mortuary. It was decided that the alterations be made and that Mr. Saner be asked to indicate the position in which the mortuary might be erected so that the drainage at fall might be decided upon. The recommendation was approved. VENTILATION AND LIGHTING OF COWSHEDS. The Clerk read a letter from the clerk to the Cheshire • County Council (Mr, Reginald Potts) calling the atten- tion of the Council to the importance of the proper ventilation and lighting of all cowsheds and requesting that special instructions be given to the sanitary inspectors to bring the same under the notice of owners and occupiers thereof. The matter was referred to the Inspector.</p>
        <p>FORTHCOMING ELECTIONS. The Clerk road an order from the County Council fixing Saturday, April Ist as the day for the election of Urban District Councillors and Guardians and Nionclay April 3rd for the election of Rural District Councillors.</p>
        <p>• 7</p>
      </div>
