o the useful work which the Progressive Labour this Sugar Convention, and Mr. Fearneley (to suit his League is doing among the working classes in the own case of course) tsys to make the working man I constituency. Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield presided, and believe that this rise in the price of sugar is I I was supported on the platform by Mr. James not due to the Sugar Convention or the sugar Samnelson,of Liverpool,Mr. C. E. Parker(president of ' tax, but is due to the failure of the beet ! crop. If such is the case, then why has the the Liberal Association), the Rev. H. Dunnico, Coun- price of sugar continued to steadily advance since 1901 1 cillors Evans and Burton, Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, (except for a short period at the end of 1903, (when a T. Barnes, E. Osborne, W. Haigh, Joseph Howes, S. ; quantity of sugar was imported from Russia) until it 1 Wright, J. Moston, .I. Elwell, jun., F. Dilby, ' has almost doubled what it was. Sir G. Clarke in a Hitchen, J. Heywood, Wilkinson, and Joseph speech at the London Constitutional Club,admitted that Howes, jun. the use in price of sugar was paid owing to the Sugar I , Convention. Will Mr. rearneley contradict what Sir Mr. CROSFIELD, who was cordially received said he I was very sorry indeed that Mr. C. Fenwick, NI.P., who 1 Edward said ? had promised to attend the meeting and speak to them, Mr. Fearneley makes a great point in his statement had been prevented from coming to \Varrin,gton that that he wants to'see a fair market that cannot be covered i evening, but they had this consolation, they knew that or manipulated. Sorely anyone with a fair amount of , ~, ~,,„ doing 1 his best to stand by their Irish friends in intelligence, must be able to see that this convention ' ""-' the House of Commons at a time that was very critical. is just what will produce an unfair market, in that it I He did not think that if they were very public spirited has practically compelled us to rely sorely on the ' they la cou complain very m