scribed as," A chiel to corner the sugar and so bring about a state of affairs a' takin' notes." (Laughter). Some good fellow was that existed in the cotton trade. Would he, then, posing watching his comings in and goings out. He hoped it as a Labour leader, continue to advocate the Sugar amused him but he was sure it did not worry him Convention, or would he go over to the side of the (the speaker') a little bit. (Laughter.) It might occur capitalist? I would remind Mr. Fearneley, that such a to them that their friends on the otherside must after state of affairs is quite within the bounds of possibility all be getting a little hard up when they had to waste for these West Indian financiers hare been so entirely their time and the time of the public,as well as his own successful in hypnotising the Government into the time, in Convention that no surprise need be excited at their EMPTY PEURILITIES AND PERSONALITIES audacity in attempting to impose further on our of that kind. (Applause.) lie had been invited to take the credulity. chair,and he thanked his friends for the invitation though But as British consumers are now paying pretty he was bound to say he felt rather sorry for them because smartly for the extraordinary success of these astute they in turn would undoubtedly be called over the engineers of the Brussels Convention, it is to be hoped coals by the same individual for choosing such that their tactics may now receive a little more mitten- an improper chairman. (Laughter.) The other day tion, and that the mad career of Mr. Chamberlain and he had the pleasurs of kickiag off at a foot- the present Government will soon be stopped.—Yours ball match, and whatever might be the advantages or respectfully. disadvantages attached to the position of what was called the prospective candidate, he had at least learned this, that one got in that position a better chance to work up his game at "footer" than perhaps he had ever had since his school days. (Laughter.) He had the temerity to kick off at the football match, as he had done lately at some other football matches, and it was his intention, in spite of what his friend the "Critic" said, to kick off at one or two more —(laughter and applause). " But!, " said this vigilant observer—(laughter)—" this is the last man in Warrington who should have been invited to assist at a match of this kind, the object of which is to provide funds for the memorial to those who have fallen in South Africa." He was not going to detain them at any length on that very sad chapter in our past history, but as there had been this little discussion raised on the point, he would just say that he had had pretty good facilities and opportunities for ascertaining or at least trying to ascertain the real facts of the South African war, a ncl what preceded it. And having done so he condemned in pretty strong language the abuse of capitalism, the sordicliwork on the Rand, which first fomented the Jameson Raid, and in the next place, working upon English public opinion in precisely the sense unscrupnlus way, was mainly instrumental in bringing about the war. And so, having ascertained what, to the best of his belief, was the real truth of the matter out there, he had said before, and he repeated i again that the whole history of what had gone on n the Transvaal and the Orange Free State during the last ten years, struck him as about the worst example they could find in these modern Tory times of what Mr. John Burns had summed up as a policy of "blundering and plundering." (Applause.) In reply to anything they had had from the pen of his critical friend he could assure them he was prepared to substantiate all he had written in the Warrington press. (Hear, hear.) He had NOTHING TO WITHDRAW, and he was entirely unrepentant and unashamed. (Laughter and Cheers.) He dare say they had been amused, in following the debates pretty closely, at the agility and remarkable skill which the present Premier displayed while performing this wonderful dance on the " tight rope." It was nothing less, and he did it very cleverly. (Laughter). He thought the Premier forgot that his unfortuate followers in the country, who had not this marvellous dialectic skill, were left in a sad dileruma.A certain amount of dust might be thrown and in fact had been thrown in the eyes of the people in some parts of the country, hut, fortunately, in Warring- ton the issue had not been confused. it had been pre- seoted there very clearly to them, because as represent- ing Mr. Pierpoint's opinions, they had had a visit from the great high priest of Protection, himself, and as it chanced he happened to meet that gentleman a short time before he came to Warrington. He (Mr. Crosfleld) did sometimes amuse himself on the golf links. and so did Sir Howard Vincent and they met on certain links only a few weeks before he came to Warrington, to give them the pure milk of the word of protection. Sir Howard was playing in front of him, and he drove off first and after they had played three holes—he (the Chairman) noticed that Sir Howard got into a very stiff bunker, weere he " hashed " away in considerable difficulties. Several friends were watching, and as Sir Howard had been chaffing him previously by saying he was coming to Warrington "to put a spoke in his wheel " he Mr.Crosfleld could not resist the opportunity, for a little retaliation—(laughter)—and so ho shouted out, " Well, Sir Howard, when you come down to speak at Warrington I hope you won't get as badly bunkered there as you are now." (Much laughter and applause.) Really, the way things were going on it struck him in all seriousness they were VERY BADLY BUNKERED INDEED</p>
        <p>OSW,ILD BUCKLEY, Sec., L.R.C.</p>
        <p>"The Conspiracy of Evidence." Dear sir,—The question whether this country should return to Protection like a dog to its vomit, has now been:under consideration for nearly 1 year and 9 months. Evidence from various sources was asked for, to prove the Cassandra-like assertions of the decay of industries, and the desire of the people to substitute the imposi- tion of a tariff on goods cooling into this country, instead of admitting these goods duty free. This evidence has been given and seems in every direction to have conspired to belie the assertions and expectations of the advocates of this suggested change. Many unworthy attempts have been made to warp the people's judgement, but these have failed as they deserved to do. Their failure to confirm the pessimistic assertions of so-called Tariff Reformers, caused them to be discarded, and othe- means equally unworthy have been substituted, but these have failed also and have brought discredit on many of the propounders of this "Tariff Gospel." Men who had occupied a very high positionas councillors to the people, and had won their I confidence, have forfeited that position and confidence I by allowing themselves to be dazzled by the false Chamberlain glamour. The people so far as they I have been allowed, have spoken with no uncertain voice 1 in this matter, by registering an unmistakeable verdict against any change of the nature suggested, in the bye- elections. When Chamberlain struck them momentarily dumb with astonishment by proclaiming himself an ' advocate, nay, a missionary for the taxation of food in order, forsooth, to bind our colonies to us. he caused to be compiled a special book known as the Fiscal Blue Book, giving records of the foreign trade of this coun- try. This book has since been published, but the evidence it contains is so directly opposed to any re-imposition of import duties and so strongly favours the present policy of free importations that it has been discreetly dropped by the Chamherlainites, and so far as they are concerned, remains a dead letter, or like the words in the old song they, in effect, say : "Oh ! no, we never mention it It's name is never heard, Our lips are now forbid to speak,</p>
        <p>That (ince familiar word." 4 In his public utterances Nit.. Chamberlain tried to make the well-known sufferings of the people sub- servient to his purpose by intensifying them. fie told them what many of them knew by a more intimate acquaintance with the matter than he could possibly have, that trade was stagnated, and tried to persuade them that this stagnation was caused by Free Trade, his object of course being to obscure the real cause, which he had been mainly instrumental in producing, and urged that an inquiry was wlnted. This the advocates of Free Trade at once agreed to, providing it was thorough and impartial. But this would have been too much like the leiscal Blue Book, and did not suit Mr. Chamberlain. He therefore usurped the functions of the Crown, by appointing a commission himself, which is composed, no doubt, of some honourable men, but almost, if no entirely all, avowed disciples of his. This commission has come to be known as" My com- mission," and it is impossible that its findings will be free from bias. Nloreover, in his attempts to cajole, he produced some figures which showed a lofty disdain for accuracy and which were afterwards proved to be entirely wrong, but when challenged he not only ignored the challenge, but with inimitable effrontery;repeated his false statements. Foiled in all other attempts, he tried to play on the fears of the people by asserting that various industries had gone, that is, that they were absolutely dead. That others were going, and that others would go. Here also he proved a false prophet, for judged byall the best evid- ence available, we do not only seem to be prosperous, but are really so, and those industries that watts said to be dead are very much alive, whilst those which were said to be declining are very progressive. He stands before us a monument of falsehood, though consistent in his inconsistency, for whatever he has said, he has said, and however much he may be wrong, however far from the truth his assertions may be, or whoever they may wrongfully injure or hurt, he admits no possibility of mistake, as he withdraws nothing, neither does he apologise. Ills greatest claim to the acknowledgement of posterity will be as one whose whole public career Was alibel, and the iconoclast of parties.—Yours fai The mount,Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>and he fancied their good adversary in Warrington was very well aware of it too. (Hear, hear.) But although a short time since he (Mr. Pierpoint) was nailing the Protectionist colours to the mast very vigorously more recently there had boen much less waving of the flag than usual in Mr. Pierpoint's speeches. (Laughter). No ; Mr. Pierpoint had probably found that the trend of opinion in Warrington was very much the same as throughout England generally, and above all in Lanca- shire. (Applause.) If there was one county more than another in England which, having led the way inopinion at other times, was going to lead the way now, it was Lancashire. They had made up their minds that the policy of monopoly, dear food, and dear living, was no policy for the County Palatine. Turning to another matter Mr. Crostield observed that when one occupied the position of prospective candidate, one nnickly dis- covered one had a certain number of candid friends. He did not mind that in the slightest degree. But they ought to keep within the mark, and when he was told by the same critic that he had been making frantic efforts to secure the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>SOO CAL EST VOTE</head>
        <p>SOO CAL EST VOTE</p>
        <p>in Warrington, he thought somebody was overshooting his mark a good deal. Candidly, having had the advan- tage and pleasure of some intercourse with, for instance, Councillor Stott, he was certain that as a fair-minded, and very frank-minded man, he would endorse what he (Mr.Crosfield) told them, namely, that he had made no frantic efforts of that kind to secure either the Socialist vote or any other kind of vote. (Applause.) He believed in trying to state one's opinion as frankly and as honestly as he could, and it was, perhaps, a little infra dig to go cap in hand to people asking them for their votes to be given against their will. (Hear, hear.) It was better to have fair and square discussions and arguments, and secure the support of those who could honestly give them, their support. As for others they were entitled to hold their own opinions, and to vote as seemed right to them,and if one were beaten under those circumstances he would still have the satisfaction of knowing that he had tried to fight his battle on the square. It would be a very sad pity if the most advanced wing of the Progressive Party, which was so powerfully represented in the meeting that night, did not give them, he would not sah; only a cordial, but a</p>
        <p>DETERMINED AND INFLEXIBLE RACING this time. (Applause.) It was a good mpiny years since he read the arguments which Mr. Blatchford. not only with ability, but with genius, had put forth, He</p>
        <p>was not going to pretend to say what might be possible or practicable a century or two hence, or even sooner, for the world moved fast nowadays, but, he said frankly, that, having endeavoured to examine with an open and independent mind Mr. Blatchford's arguments, it did seem to him (Mr. Crosfield) that the writer was attempting the impossible ; and that if they laid them- selves out to fight for that programme they would only encourage the forces which, unfortunately, were only too strong, the forces of re-action that were against them. He must say that he thought it was a pity for any of them to waste their time in quarrell- ing about terms. It was impossible to say how far State interference could be carried on with advantage in their commercial life in this country. He would in that respect submit one remark, which had been made by a woman who was one of the best lovers of her country, and one of the best patriots in th truest and real sense of the term they had in England. He would quote the remark of Mrs. Humphrey Ward, that whatever was done in the direction of State intervention in our time, and for a long time to come, they must still leave a very considerable scope, for the enterprise of the individual. (Hear hear, and applause). It would be affectation to deny or ignore a fact that was pressed upon him so often now-a-days, that his name had been brought forward as the prospective candidate in the great fight that was shortly before them. He wished to say this in refer- ence to friends of theirs, who thought it was a pity that a man who was tarred with the capitalistic brush should be shoved into that position, that he did not think any friends of his on the executive would accuse him of having tried to unduly push himself forward—(applause)—because he could honestly tell them that he had not done so. (Renewed applause.) He did not mean to say that he was devoid of personal lambition. He thought that a certain amount of honour- able ambition was rather ra,good thing to have, and he should not be ashamed of that at all. He did not mean to say either that he had not had a habit engrained in him from his very earliest youth of watching politics very closely and taking a keen interest in home and foreign politics, because his father took such a deep interest in the politics of the country, and particularly the politics of his own native town of Warrington. (Applause.) Naturally he had grown up in an atmos- phere that had developed a very keen interest in political life, and if he was chosen as candidate, and they did him the lifelong honour to return him as the</p>
        <p>MEMBER, FOIL THE CONSTITUENCY of course he should deeply appreciate it. (Applause.) In conclusion, he would honestly assure every man pre- sent that he should not only consider it an honour, but he should most certainly feel it a very deep responsi- bility. (Hear, hear, and applause.) It had been said by the" Critic "to whom he had alluded to that he was no speaker. NVell, he had never pretended to be, an orator, and he was not sure that the flow of Parliamen- tary eloquence might not sometimes be rather overdone. (Hear, hear.) It was said there was a lack of ability in the House of Commons, but that was all nonsense. It was not that. There was so much ability there that it was very difficult for any one, unless a man of exceptional genius to rise above his fellows and make a mark for himself, and they had almost a plethora of political talent in the House. They had one of the most brilliant members, one of the most splendid fighters who ever entered the House talking to them the other night. (Applause.) When they had men of that calibre it was quite safe he thought, if they returned some people to the House who would be content to vote straight and solid. (Applause.) He was glad, in addressing so many members of the Progressive Labour League tb have the opportunity of telling them that, without at all professing any oratorical gifts, which he had never pretended to possess, that he had this advan- tage—the friendship of some very fine Labour members in the House of Commons; not very many, he granted, but he hoped to add to the number, and to have the advantage of their guidance and instruction when Labour questions came up for consideration. (Applause.) Ard if any of the most advanced sections of the party thought that because he was, without being a very wealthy man which he was not—(laughter)—one of the so-called capitalistic class, he would put the interests of his own pocket on any single question before the interest of the nation at large, and particularly what he believed to be the best interests of the great bulk of the nation, he could assure them that they misjudged the kind of education he had had and the political atrnesphere and influence in which he had been brought up. (Hear, hear, and applause.) If a man represented a great con- stituency like Warrington, it was his duty to endeavour to</p>
        <p>STUDY THE INTERESTS OF ALL CLASSES, , and to the best of his very limited abilities, he should always do so. (Applause.) Certainly he should never forget in home politics that grand maxim of Bentham, which was often quoted, and would bear repetition without ever getting stale, the maxim that legislation should always tend to secure the" Greatest good of the greatest number." (Applause.) Taking a still wider survey, as indeed they needed to do in these later days, as Mr. Burns said,. they had seen too much of "The Jingo Rake's Progress." He would like to say that looking at foreign and home polities all round, whether as a candidate or as a cordial and sincere supporter of Liberalism in Warrington, he should al ways endeavour to bear in mind John Locke's far reaching and noble maxim that" the end of Government is the good of mankind." (Loud cheers.) "WHAT I HAVE SAID." MR. SAMUELSON QUOTES SOME OF</p>
        <p>MR. CHAMBERLAIN'S FORMER UTTERANCES</p>
        <p>nations. If we commenced a tariff war, did they think Mr. Chamberlaim was the only man who was not going "to take it lying down " ? Did they not think that foreign countries would turn round on us and say "We have made you the most favoured nation,and we are not prepared to do so any more if you attack us." (Applause) There was rising up in almost every large commercial country a strong Free Trade party. Were we going to sot them a bad example by becoming Pro- tectionists? Mr.l Samuelson proceeded to speak of the complicating effects which Protection would have on trade. As soon as one industry secured Protection another would demand it and, to quote Mr. Ritchie, the consequences would be that they would tax bread, meat and ever thing we used. The speaker alluded to the conditions in continental nations. How would the Englishman like to be a Russian worker, driven about by Cossacks and oppressed as he was? The bloody revolution which had taken place there was a workmen's revolution, brought about by the attempts to keep the poor poor and the rich rich. (Applause.) Incidentally he alluded to the progress of the social democratic movement which had gained so much for the working classes of Germ my. The Chairman he observed, need not have troubled to defend himself from the charge of Socialism. Nothing would do this country more good than a good, big, sound dose of Socialism. (Applause). Of course, that was his own opinion,and nor, their candid ite's. In Prance the work- ing classes in the towns were worse off than in England, and in Germany and Russia, black bread was eaten. As to the United States, when Protection was first adopted undoubtedly the wages of the working classes were raised, and until the immense trusts and the monoplist combinations of employers were formed the men had to a very large extent the power to defand themselves.</p>
      </div>
