      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 25, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 25, 1905.</p>
        <p>-EVERYTHING- HAD RISEN IN PRICE</p>
        <p>in the United States through Protection, just as it would increase in England if we had Protection. While wages had, if anything, been rather lower, the prices of the necessaries of life had risen. Mr. Samuelson urged his hearers to do their utmost to stamp out Protection and to return Mr. Crosfleld by such a majority as would set an example to other constituencies and help to show Mr. Chamberlain that the game was up. (Applause.) They must be united as workingmen. He remembered the time when it was absolutely impossible to return workingmen to Parliament except with the aid of the Liberal party, and he had always been in favour of Labour Representation. They were perfectly justified and in fact it was an absolute necessity that the working- men should have a thoroughly independent party, but they must see that they</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>USED THEIR POW Eft WISELY.</head>
        <p>USED THEIR POW Eft WISELY.</p>
        <p>It was not wise, for instance, to bring forw-a.rd a candidate merely to show their strength, and knock out a man whom they knew would support them when he got into the House. (Applause). Mr. Crosfield had assured him that he was in favour of an extension of Labour repre- sentation and of the payment of election expenses out of the rates. Ilia was also in fav)ur of the payment of a salary to workingmen representatives in Parliament who required it. Did they know of the Tories ever help- ing to put a workingroaniinto Parliament? Never. They might as well try to change the spots of the leopard. Where the Liberal candidate was prepared to support labour representation and an extension of labour repre- sentation—like Mr. Crosfield was—they could not do better than return him. In conclusion he sincerely hoped they would be successful in returning a worthy son of a worthy sire as their representative in the House of Commons. (Applause). MR. HOWES ON THE IRISH QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSEPH HOWES, whom the chairman alluded to as one of the ablest and most powerful speakers they had ever listened to in Warrington, said it was a severe blow when he received the letter from Mr. Fenwick stating that he could not attend. Mr. Fenwick assured him that he would at the earliest possible opportunity redeem the pledge he had given to address a Warrington audience on the political situation. A strongly-worded telegram was sent to Mr. Herbert Gladstone who wired expressing his personal sorrow at having to disappoint the Warrington people, but his duty as Liberal Whip was to keep ir. Fonwick at his post owing to the important division to take place that night. The division was, continued Mr. Howes, one of the most important points of the present political situation. Their Tory friends frequently charged them with making exaggerations, but they could not exaggerate when speaking of the crimes of the present Government. The British language was all too feeble to do justice to the theme, and as for the exposure recently made in regard to Ireland, it was one of the meanest tricks that ever the Tory Party had beea gailty of, and the wore past masters in that kind of work. (Laughter.) Liberals had been accused of truckling to the Irishman, of wanting to sell the position they ought to occupy as British patriots becaose they had recommended that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>IRISHMEN SHOULD :I_I.A.NAGE THEIR OWN AFFAIRS</head>
        <p>IRISHMEN SHOULD :I_I.A.NAGE THEIR OWN AFFAIRS</p>
        <p>as they-cfaimed the liberty- ' Englishmen should manage theirs. What was good for an Englishman could not be' bad for an Irishman, and if the position had been reversed and there had been a Parliament in Ireland making laws for England and Englishmen had been called upon to obey laws made in Ireland for WO years, they would all have been Home Rulers and they would never have rested until they had smashed up the power of that Irish Parliament. Though they had taken great pains to insist on imperial unity, and though no British Home Ruler had ever suggested any act of separation between the English and the Irish people, while the most advanced Home Rulers had always pleaded that there must boa unity of interest and general work, and a. unity for all practical, external purposes, of English, Irishmen, Scotchmen and every class of men forming the great British family, they asked that each part should have the liberty of manag- ing its own local and internal affairs. (Applause). The Tory party had lately been showing themselves willing, if they could get the support of Irish voters, to give Ireland Home Rule if they could call it by some other name, if they could smuggle it through, and deceive their own people in order to do it. Fie believed they could get anything from the Tory party if there was sufficient pressure and sufficient " red light," and if there was danger on the line. To sit on the minister- ial benches they would wade through any measure, and they had been cooking a Home Rule scheme for Ireland Such TRICKEB,Y, DECEPTION AND DOUBLE DEALING as had been exposed was beneath a statesman's position and no British workingman could keep his character for five minutes if he played a trick like the Tories had played. It might not be possible to bring tho Govern- ment to the ground in the debate that night, but tloy would have another exposure of the tactics of the Government,and they would see how many gentlemen of England were likely to remain connected with such a discredited party after the revelations of the past few days. (Hear, hear.) They had reached a critical period in the history of the nationand they now had some idea of the work which lay in front of the Radical party. He deprecated division emot'e-, the Progressive ranks, because he knew what a lot there was to do, and he knew how short life was. The Irish problem was one which the Radical party would have to face and solve. It was is bigger question than Home Rule. It was a question that would touch almost every one of the social questions of this land. At the bottom of the Present Irish difficulty was the great land question; and in dealing with that there must be an alteration of our own</p>
        <p>Mr. James SA.MCELSON then addressed the meeting. Alluding to the Chairman's remarks as to the ability displayed by members of Parliament, he said there was a very great lack of principle in Parliament. (Applause). It was because his friend the Chairman was not a per- vert, but a good, sound Radical, as his father was, that he (Mr. Samuelson) was present for the second time to stand upon a Warrington platform in support of a Crosfield. (Applause.) The Tory Government, con- tinued the speaker. had finished the savings they found in the treasury after the Liberals had been in office, they had laid on taxation, 'and by the detestable war in South Africa they had saddled the country with an expenditure of £250,000,000 which, we were told by those who knew best, had better be written off as a bad debt unless they could get white men into the country. Mr. Chamberlain, he hoped, would live long and repent—(laughter)—and he hoped he would come to say like the old Scotch farmer who was dying : "Honesty's the best policy. I've tried baith." (laughter). Mr. Chamberlain had tried both. (Laughter). In 1882, Mr. Joseph Chamberlain was president of the Board of Trade and unless he was an arrant fool, which he was not, it was high time that he had made up his mind whether it was desirable to stick to Free Trade or depart from it, because the Corn Laws had been repealed for a very considerable time. ON MARCH 24Th, 1882, MR. CHAMBERLAIN SAID : "If we are to retaliate upon foreign countries by impos- ing a duty on food imported from those countries into England as a means of inducing them to alter their tariffs and effect a transfer of the supply of food to the colonies (that was what he was now proposing) I do not see where the food of the country is to come from, because it is impossible that in anything like a reason- able time our colonies can produce an amount of food at all approaching the quantity required." Mr. Chamberlain added: " I say in the first instance that —•- they cannot produce the amount of food required, and 'LAND SYSTEM. then that they cannot take in return our exports Land would always keep those who worked on it, hut it to the amount indicated," and he also made the could not keep regiments of men who did not work on very significant remark " A tax would go neither to the it. They could not face the land question with the farmer nor to the labourer,but it would go to the landed present House of Lords, neither could they do interest to enable them to keep up their rents." Justice to Ireland or to themselves. How were (Applause). That was what Mr. Chamberlain, the they going to deal with the House of President of the Board of Trade, said in 1882, and in Lords? There was no legislative enactment which 1885 he made precisely the same statement at Ipswich, created that assembly ; like Topsy it " growed." and he added : "It is said that this is the paradise of (Laughter.) The policy of Mr. Chamberlain had been the rich. We should take care and not make it the dumped upon them to direct their attention from more purgatory of the poor." (Applause). That was what serious matters. Mr. Chamherlaia had now to rely they were trying to do now. People with a past did entirely upon the lords, dukes, and earls, and it was a not like being reminded of what they had said in form. lamentable position for any man to be in. Mr. Howes er years, although they had a nice way of getting out alluded satirically to the way in which Mr. Balfour re- of the matter by saying that things had changed since. Peatedly evaded the question as to whether he supported (Laughter). So they had, but for the worse' for Mr. Mr. Chamberlain's policy. It was too late in the day Chamberlain's principle. He did not believe Mr. for statesmen to pretend to have open minds on such an Criamberlain thought they were changed in the sense important question. They must make up their minds or in which he told the public ; lie believed that, to put it the public would make them up for them. (Laughter.) in a Parliamentary way, was a pure Chaniberlainism. Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman had asked "When will (Laughter). As to the farce of it end ?" But , THE SUPPLIES FEOM THE COLONIES, WHEN WOULD TIER IMMORTALITY OF IT END? in 1903,51 million tons of bread stuffs were imported to Surely we ought to have a cleaner awl more consistent feed the people of this country. How much come from Political life than we had to-day in the House of Corn- Canada ?-600,000 tons, where: 3,000,000 tons came moos. (Hear, hear.) The day of retribution was fast from the United States, And about 1,900,000 came from approaching, and in a great and important centre places most of which Mr. Chamberlain proposed to tax of industry like Warrington, where they }mew the supply of corn from. If they turned to the meat that Protection was not for the benefit of the imports, they would find that andada and Australia workers but of the shirkers, let them stand by the side e sent us about one-fifth of what the United States and of those who by their industry built up the prosperity of the River Plate supplied. For the sake of 600,000 tons the country. In the fight they would know no creed of corn—and it would be a long time before Canada or colour, or nationality. If they were in favour of could increase that quantity—we were to quarrel with pulling down '` this iniquity of modern times " and those who supplied nearly 5,000,000 tans. Mr. Chamber- building upa clean Government, let them go to the poll lain said his opponents were exaggerating, as he with the fixed determination of doing a big thing at the proposed to put only a 2s. per quarter duty on corn. If next election which should break down the Tory air. Chamberlain was content to impose 2s. Mr.Chaplia monopoly which had reigned for so long in this centre, would not be content—(hear, hear)—and neither would and emancipate all the workers in order that the battle Mr. David Mclver. The landlords, into whose pockets of life might be easiet for their children than they according to Mr. Chamberlain. the tax would go, would themselves had found it. (Lout applause.) not be content. (Applause.) In Germany they began REV. ff. DUNNIC) AND THE I.L.P. with is. 2d. per quarter,and the duty had now reached The Rev. II DUNIC ) proposed it vote of thanks to us. In Prance they began with 7d. and it had increased the speakers. Mr. Ssminelson's rernarks concerning Mr. to 12s. 2d. The only change which had actually taken Chamberlain's utterances in 1882 as contrasted with his place in the conditions was the change in the attitude speeches to-day had suggested ti him that he might and aspirations of Mr. Chamberlain. He now hob- l ? prepare a lecture entitled "Mr. Chamberlain as he was nobbed with the lords and the large-landed proprietors 1r Chamberlain EIS he is, and Mr. Cnamb 1 ' ' on the platform in different parts of the country. Headmired Mr. Crosaeld thought he was making use of them and they (ought . to be." (Laughter.) Flo (". ain " he for his statement that he did not p lacier for the Socialist knew they were making use of him. (Laughter and vote or any other vote. They wanted a man as their applause.) Rich men who wanted to be a little richer, representative who knew his own mind and who was iu who were never satisfied, were coming to him, and he I sympathy with the working classes, and he 1 (Ur Dunnico) was prepared to give his cordial siipport to. was also surrounded by people who thought that by hanging on to him they would revive trades which they such a candidate. Every man must admire Mr. Cros- ' field all the more because he would not pander to any were actually unable to carry on because other Indus- arty or creed. He (Mr. Dunnico) was a socialist, but tries had taken their place. (Applause.) When Mr. I 'there was socialism and socialism (Hear, hear.) A Chamberlain spoke to them of the small effect which a tax on food would have they should say "Sugar" to , socialist was amen who desired to .see the social rder lin which the good of the individual should be sub- him. (Laughter and applause.) Mr. Balfour said that ordinated to the good of the come:lenity. Taking that people were putting on duties against us, and therefore definition, they were all Socialists (Applause.) He we must retaliate, but Protectionists did not tell them ! admired many they in Warringt.en He hid had a the truth about that. The duties were not imposed personal difference with Councillor StOtt, and both of against us alone ;_ they were put on against other i ct them ilittiodr hit o tht aarsd ,evbeart he odwid.h e Tthheoreu gwh at sa so one eucthhli thing countries,oand England IN CONSEQUENCE OF HER FREE TRADE POLICY, I had been placed in all cases among the most favoured</p>
