      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>however, Councillor Stott had said which he hoped he would not repeat, and that was that "as the Labour Party could not bring out'a candidate of their own"— or words t this effect—"l have no interest whatever in the Warrington election, I am going to spend my time in the Newton constituency." Warrington a working class constituency, was represented by an enemy to the working men and yet they ' had Cannallor Stott, an advanced Socialist saying he had no interest in the election here. If they had a Socialist candidate and Councillor Stott felt bound to support him he (Mr. Dunnico) would say " All honour to him." But here they had a clear fight between a progressive candidate and a retrogres- sive candidate, and yet, they had an advanced progressive speaking in the way he had stated. He made that appeal not as a Liberal speaking to Liberals, only, but as a workingman, he said it would be to the everlasting disgrace of the Labour and Socialist Party if, through their apathy, this election was lost. (Applause.) He appealed to them sincerely as a man, if they would not join the Liberals officially, to help individually by their votes to unseat the present member and send Mr. Croslield to the House of Commons. (Applause.) Mr. C ROSE IE LD observed that in this centre of reaction —and some of them during the past twenty-five years had known what the forces of reaction were—they wanted every accession of energyand ability they could get, and they must particularly value the distinguished services which upon many occasions Mr. Dunnico had r endered. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>WORD TO TRADE UNIONISTS.</head>
        <p>A WORD TO TRADE UNIONISTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILKINSON HAIGH, in seconding, said he stood there as a thorough-going Trade Unionist in support of Mr. Crasfield, who was prepared to stand by Labour. What more could they want as working men than Mr. Crosfleld had promised? Personally he was perfectly satisfied and only wished fight was nearer than it seemed to be. • (Applause.) The Government would put it off as long as they could, but when the contest came, they in Warrington were determined to follow the tide of the country and return Mr. Crosfield. (Applause.) Mr. CROSIPIELD remarked that he had had the advan- tage of discussing with Mr. Haigh and other able leaders of the labour pa -ty in this town the questions which primarily and most seriously concerned the wage-earners, aud he had greatly benefited by the practical instruc- tion he had received from thorn. (Hear, hear. The motion was heartily carried. _ _ .</p>
        <p>In responding, Mr. SA MUELSON said he was convinced that every additional day and week the election was postponed would be to the discredit and disadvantage of the government. Lie strongly recommended work- men to join the League.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>CHAIRHAN THANKED.</head>
        <p>THE CHAIRHAN THANKED.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. ELWELL, jun., moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman, and remarked that the time would come when all differences among Progressives should be eliminated and they should stand as a united party for the benefit of the workers of the country. Dur- ing the last few years KINN had been dazzled by the phantom of imperialism and now the To:ies wanted to lead them into a retrograde movement which would land them back in the days of slavery such as existed in the early partof the 19th century. fie hoped when the election came workingmen would be united and return Mr. Crosfield at the head of the poll. (Applause). Mr. T. BARNES, the newly-elected president of the league, seconded and said he intended to do his utmost for the prosperity of the league but his efforts would be useless unless they were backed up by all the progres- sive workingmen of Warrington. (Applause.) The League was composed. of earnest and determined ' Radicals, not of apathetic men, who would pay atten- tion to political affairs. The resolution was carried with enthusiasm. MR. CROSFIELD AD THE OBLITERATION OF LOCAL DIFFERENCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFIELD, in responding, thanked Mr. Samuelson for the friendly way in which he had volunteered, like o:her friends of his (Mr.Crosfteld's) late father, to lend a helping hand when the time came to light quite the most momentous battle there would have been in the history of Warrington politics for perhaps more than a generation. He would allude to Mr. Elwell's speech as to the necessity or unity. They know how sadly the town had been divided on municipal matters of late years. He did not know whether their Conservative friends had taken any part as a party in that movement, but certainly the president of their association most properly decided that the Liberal Party as such should take no side whatever in municipal differences such as those we wished to allude. Now, however, those things had passed away, and 114 did think it most important that in view of the great national issue they had before them—monopoly and dear food as against the good of the country, together with many other questions—whatever local differences there I might have been in the past should be sunk—(applause)— and that all who were at heart progressives and who really wanted to see their country better when they left it than when they came into it, should stand shoulder to shoulder. (Applause.) He was very happy and pleased to have his friend Mr. Henry Roberts,—(Applause,) telling him that, along with others, he intended to put his shoulder to the wheel, as did also his brother. (Hear, hear.) Unfortunately his old friend Mr. William Pierpoint had not been all that might have been of late, but they hoped he would be able to take his part in the battle. He sincerely trusted that little differences there had been in the past would be obliterated in face of the momentous contest they had before them. Many of them who hal been brought up in the traditions of progessive, advanced Liberalism, had seen their town in not a very encouraging condition politically for a great many years. Ihey knew there were on the other side plenty of people who were counting on the opportunity of another generation of the same sort of thing if only they could draw a little red herring here and throw a little dust in the eyes there, and tide over the great 1 issue that was coming. He hoped, that having seen as many red herrings drawn across the political path as they had seen during the last few years,none of them were going to he misled by by any side current °raid.) isms thtt might ha brought forward, but that they would think out the great issues for themselves and take advantage of the finest oppor- tunity they had had for quite a generation, whether it came in a few weeks or in a few months. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ANNUAL HEErING OF THZ LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>ANNUAL HEErING OF THZ LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>There svaian enconrag,ing attend.tuce—the largest, in fact, since tha inauguration of the league—at the first annual meeting which was held at the Liberal Club on Nionday evening, Mx. Jeseph Hawes 'president) occupy- ing the chair. The gathering included Messrs. John Morton and J. Howes, jun., hon. secretaries... .</p>
        <p>Mr. MORTON presentei the antut ti rvort, which stated that the first meeting was called for • November 18th. 1901, 2) invitations being sent out. The total member- ship was now 22' 5 the lictures for the various wards being :—Town Hall, 11 ; Whitecross, 51. ; Bewsey, 11; Orford, 19 ; St. John's 27 ; Fairfield, 53 ; Howley, 21 : St. Austin's, 46 ; Latchford, 26. Weekly meetings had been held,and the League had assisted in the promotion of the recant Parr Hall demonstrations.</p>
        <p>Addressing the members Mr. dowcs said they ought to feel proud at the saccess which the league had obtained, although it had been in existenes but a com- paratively short timo. Ele had decided to resign the position of president, as he thought a local 'man ought to be appointed. The meeting was desirous that Mr. Thomas Barnes shonld succeed him, and Mr. Barnes accepted the office on the understanding that Mr. Howes would allow him- self to be appointed one of the vice-presidents. To this Mr. Howes consented, and the other vice-presidents appointed were Messrs. J. Elwell, illrl,Wilkinson Haigh, E. W. Walker, S. Wright and the Rev. H. Mannino, with Mr. E. Osborne as treasurer, Messrs. John Horton and J. HOWB9,jun.. 49 q,?Cretaries, and Messrs. 0 alby, Flit chen, Heywood, J. H. Morton, J. Willett and Thomas Knowles 119 the Execntive Committee. The arrangements to be made in connection with the f )rthcorning election were discussed, and, on the motion of Mr. Morm)N, se loaded by Mr. J. ELWELL, jun., a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the late president, and the officers, and also to the gentlemen who have submitted papers at the weekly debates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>SCENE AT THE POLICE COURT. Mr. Henry Roberts and the Mayor.</head>
        <p>SCENE AT THE POLICE COURT. Mr. Henry Roberts and the Mayor.</p>
        <p>There was a little scene at the Warrington police court yesterday between the Mayor and Mr. Henry Roberts. It occurred during the hearing of a case in which Amy Lleath,l3s, Ellesmere street, summoned her husband for persistent cruelty. Mr. Browne and Mr. Steel as representing the parties held a consultation as a result of which it was announced that the defendant agreed to his wife having a separation order with 7s, per week alimony. Mr. Steel on behalf of the com- plainant applied for costa together with an advocates, fee, an application which was opposed by Mr. Browne. Eventually the Mayor said the magistrates would make an order for the court costs but withouttbe advocates' fee.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel contended that the decision was contrary to the usages of the court. In such cases as that before the court it was usual to grant the advocate's fee. The Mayor : We have decided by a majority of the Bench.</p>
        <p>gr. ff. Roberts : With all due deference to the Mayor I must protest. I think it a very bad precedent to establish to refuse the advocate's fee in cases of this kind. What woman can possibly put her case properly If the other side has a solicitor. I say it is contrary to the precedent of the court to refuse the application. Mr. Steel: I would like to associate myself with thc remarks made bf Mr. Roberts. In my short experience it has not been the practice to refuse the advocate's fee in such cases. The Mayor : lam chairman and the majority of the Bench has decided.</p>
        <p>Mr. Roberts : lam senior magistrate here you are only chairman as chief magistrste for the time being. I have had twelve times the experience you have had and I say it is not the precedent for the advocate's fee to be refused.</p>
        <p>The Mayor: The majority are against you. Next case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
