      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL GATHERINGS.</p>
        <p>Cairo Street Chapel.</p>
        <p>A WORD TO APATHETIC CHURCH-GOERS.</p>
        <p>The members of the Cairo Street Unitarian Chapel held their annual congregational gathering on Monday evening in the schoolroom. The proceedings com- menced with the provision of an excellent tea to which about 120 persons sat down, the tables being presided over by Mrs. Madeley, Mrs. Hill, Mrs. Hollingsworth, Mrs. Chapman Hollingsworth, Mrs. Rathbone and Mrs. C. Pemberton. At the subsequent 1 meeting Mr. J. Rymer Young presided over a large attendance. He was supported on the platform by the Rev. C. Harvey Cook (pester), the Rev. Deady Agate, of Altrincham the Rev. C. Peach of Manchester, and Mr. F. W. Monks. The CHAIRMAN, in opening the proceedings, observed that he got the idea from the Rev. Dendy Agate that this might be an opportunity for chiding them, or those who were not present, for their singular apathy in the matter of church-going. He (Mr. Young) was not one who believed that because a person did not go to chapel or church he was necessarily bad, They would admit he thought, there were excellent people who never went Inside a place of worship. Nor was he prepared also to assert that because a matt did go to church he was necessarily a saint. Going to chapel or church was not, to his mind, in itself essential, but, he suggested, they were meaured rather by works than by faith. If their services had so little in them that they did not attract, what must be the effect on the outer world of people who saw them and knew them? It might be that some people did not like preaching ; others went to chapel or church because they liked' the sermons. Then some people went to chapel or church because they liked music ; others stopped away because they did not like the music. Unless they could makeup their minds to go to chapel on principle and ,with mutual forbearance they would not get on with their services. Altogether the position of a minister was not an enviable one. Personally, he (Mr. Young) would not be one for a thousand ayear. (Laughter.) It was an impossibility to please everybody, and he wondered how a minister got through the troubles and trials by which he was surrounded. It was not altogether a matter of liking or disliking a minister; it was how far they adhered to a principle. They at Cairo-street could only be regarded as a collection of oddities. {Laughter.) They did agree on broad main outlines, but they were not so bound as to be unable to exercise and think out things from an individual point of view. They might carry it to excess. He would urge them to use their influence on those not present and let them understand what a "killing" thing it was for a minister or professor to try nod instruct people who did not want instructing or who were too apathetic to understand. A minister of any denomination had to do the best he could from his own point of view. No one would desire that a minister should attempt to please everybody. It was a grand thing to have a man who had the fire, energy and courage to assert his own opinions. Mr. F. IV. PURSE superintendent of the Sunday school gave an encouraging report on the Sunday school work In 1902, he said, they had 252 scholars, in 1903 they had 250, in 1901 they had 256, while at the preseet time there were 269. (Hear, hear.) The average attendance last year was 160 in the morning, and 195 in the after- noon. They had maintained their previous records and he SW no reason why they should go back. The institutions connected with the school also kept up— the Boys' Club, Girls' Club, School Choir, and Penny Bank. The number of depositors in the bank was on the increase and there were signs that it was appreciated. He (Mr. Purse) would very much like to see a bazaar organized, because he thought by that means they would be able to raise about .2.50 of the £l5O owing on the scViol extension fund. The Rev. DENDY AGATE said those of them who had been born in the Unitarian fold,as well as some of those who had•come in flora without and understood some- thing of their traditions, knew that Warrington was a kind of Mecca among them. They looked back to the history of the Old academy; they remembered the splendid man who had ministered in their old chapel ; and they knew how widely the influence of those who had worshipped in that place as years had gone by had made itself felt, not only in their own town but in the various other places. It was very encouraging to hear such a report as they had heard from Mr. Purse about the Sunday school and it was encouraging to see such a large number of young people gathered together. In spite of what they were sometimes told of the in- 1 difference of a large number of persons to the forms of I faith and to religious works if they had faith in God I and in His divine order they would find it was impossi- ble if they themselves were in earnest to despair of the I future, either of religion at large or of that faith which was the inspiration and the joy and strength of their own lives. There were two things which seemed to him to be essential both for the individual and the religious community in this matter of religion. They ought to have a profound earnestness and an abounding and all-inclusive charity. If they could combine the earnestness which marked the reformers of 400 years ago with a charity in which the great bulk of them were so sadly lacking, then surely they would have reached the po.:sibilities of the finest results both in their own lives and in the religious world. Might God help them to be earnest in their devotion to that which they felt to be the truth, and at the same time to remember that the truth of God was far greater than any limited human interpretation of it. (Applause). The Rev. CHARLES PEACH gave an address on behalf of the Manchester Unitarian Home Missionary Cellege, and pointed out the good work it was doing in supply- ing ministers for the Unitarian Churches. This year he said, in order to celebrate its jubilee they had launched a scheme for the establishment of new and magniflceut residental premises for the students. The cost would be £20,000, of which £13,000 had already been raised, and he appealed to them to do what they could to help to wipe off the debt. Mr. F. W. MONKS moved a resolution pledging the support of the Cairo-street congregation to the scheme I mentioned by Mr. Peach. He (Mr. Monks) had been on 1 the committee of the College for 14 or 15 years. and the committee had done him the honour of electing him vice-chairman. There was no doubt the Collette had filled a distinct want in the North of England. The Cairo. street Chapel had benefited by it, in the way of being supplied with students to conduct the services in the, absence of the minister. The resolution was seconded by Mr. J. H. KNOWLES, and carried. The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK thanked the workers in conection with the church and schools fortheir services , during the year. On the church roll and in his visit book he had the names of 200 families belonging to , chapel and school, and that represented at least from I I' 600 to 700 people. He wished to speak seriously on the matter of church attendance. He did not want to run away from Cairo-street, because he felt it was growing to his heart. Their lives were becoming to him a source of pleasure and real interest. He per- sonally hoped he would be there for years i but at the same time, he expetted the con- gregation to rally round him. If Cairo-street stood by , him he would stand by Cairo-street. The congregation I was becoming more and more a democratic congrega- tion. It did not consist of people who had great means. t The financial report was not as good as twelve months . ago. He noticed when people were absent for three or four Sundays they did not always make a point of i i making up n the collection for the time they were away- He thanked Mr. Muir (the organist) and choir I for their admirable and conscientious work. He (Mr. Cook) wanted it to be clearly understood that to con- tinue the ministry one must have the support, not only I financially but sympathetically, of the congregation. I At the same time he did not ask them to come and listen , to him, but to come and worship. 1 The speeches were pleasingly interspersed with songs ,by Mrs. Booth and Mr. H. Bennett, Mr. Muir accom- panying on the piano. St. Peter's Church. Presentation to the Vicar.</p>
        <p>I The annual congregational gathering in connection ' with the above church was held in the schoolroom on Monday evening and was in every way a saccess. About 200 sat down to tea, the tables baing presided over by Mesdames Percival, James, Bamforth, Lingley, Cowden, ' Taylor, Jeuson, Skelland, Grounds, and Greaves. After tea, the usual concert was held. The Vicar presided, and was supported by the Rev. D. R. James (curate), Councillor John Roberts, and Messrs. Joseph Taylor, W. Stanley, John Taylor, Joseph Heron, Peter Edgar, and T. S. Steel. The VICAR said he had noticed that the trade of the country was increasing, both exports and imports having gone up considerably during the last few months. He hoped that the improvement in trade would reach War- rington, and that their works would soon be on full time again. (Hear, hear.) He was pleased to be able to tell them that the six weeks he had been away had built him up again, and he had come back very much strengthened. It was a comfort to know that while he had been away the work of the parish had been in good hands. Ho knew he had most excellent churchwardens, and an admirable curate while the sidesmen and all the officials connected with the church also did their duty nobly. His wife, also, he had been told, had worked very hard, and it was gratifying to think that the work had not suffered through his absence—thanks to the loyalty of his officers. (Applause.) At a later stage, Councillor ROBERTS, on behalf of the church °Moors, officially welcomed the Vicar back, and presented him with a handsome and elaborate easy chair, in recognition of the completion of his ten years' service as vicar. He said they were all pleased to see Mr. Percival back again, and he hoped the time was far distant when it would be necessary for him to again leave them on account of his health. (Hear, hear.) They did not begrudge him a holiday at any time, because they all knew he deserved it. (Hear. hear.) He did his best for the parish, and they were proud of him. They wished him and his dear wife and family every happiness and prosperity. The VICAR, in reply, thanked them for the .token of</p>
        <p>esteem which the easy chair evidently represented. He was glad that his work met with their approval, though he was sorry he had not ,been able, owing to his ill-health, to do quite what he wished to do. They had been verr kind to him, and he could assure them he would heartily appreciate that gift. (Applause.) The concert was a very enjoyable one. Instrumental trios were given by the Misses Chesworth, and songs were rendered with much tact by Miss Eckersley and Messrs. T. Humphreys and Reddish. Mr. A. E. Burdett created much amusement with his humorous sketches,. while Mr. H. M. Ashton won high encomiums with his choice readings. Mr. F. Webster officiated at the piano. Holy Trinity Church. The annual congregational gathering in connection with the above church was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening, when about 250 ladies and gentlemen sat down to an excellent tea. The subse— quent proceedings were of an enjoyable character. The chair was occupied by the VICAR, the Rev. E. C. E Carleton, who thanked those who had worked so hard in getting up the tea-party and entertainment and bringing it to so successful an issue. He also thanked those who had collected subscriptions, those who had presided at the table, and others for arranging such an attractive programme. Mr. Joseph, Hesketh, the secretary of the Sunday school, had had chief control of the arrangements and had relieved him, (the speaker) of all responsibility. Ho (the chairman) could not allow that opportunity to pass without refer ring once more to the heavy loss they had sustained in the death of their organist, Mr. Percy Randall, without whom gatherings of that kind had never been complete. His death came as a blow to them in the church, and also to a large circle of friends in the town. He was I glad to be able to announce that, on the unanimous I recommendation of the committee, Mr. Bertram Randall, his youngest son, had been appointed in his place, and Mr. Amos the choirmaster and general I superintendent of the music. If they were to judge from the services they had held under their management during the last few Sundays, he thought they might look forward with great confidence to the future. ( Applause.) He thanked the members of the choir for their loyal support and sympathy during what had been every trying interval through which they had lately passed. Continuing, the Chairman said the Corporation were busy making a roadway round Trinity Church, and he thought they would agree with him that when the work of Market-gate was completed, the church, as a building, would be quite worthy of its prominent posi- tion. (Hear, hear.) It was interesting as a build- ing, but apart from that, it was not without its value as one of the historical landmarks of the town. Trinity Church was founded int 1709, and he thought, might be fairly regarded as one of many great monuments of a great religious revival. The country had begun to recover itself after the conflicts of the Great Rebellion and the personal rule of Cromwell. They had a monument of the Christian charity of the period in the pension fund of the widows and orphans of the clergy, which had been, as they knew, associated with Warrington for many years. As for the missionary revival, they knew the S.P.C.K. and the S.P.G. were founded, one in 1698, and the other in 1701. He thought they would agree with him that Trinity Church was an important historical landmark in t 'ova, a monument I of a great religious and educational I agival, which took place at the beginning of the 18th century, (Applause.) An excellent musical programme was given,nearly all the items being encored. The opening item was given with Miss Leigh's orchestra, and later in the evening they again appeared, the instrumental music being greatly enjoyed. Miss A. Longdin sang " The spring has come," in a pleasing manner, while Mr. W. Barnes was heartily applauded for his renderings of" The little hero," and "The sailor's wife." Fancy dances were cleverly given by the Misses A. Jones and A. Bow- cock, while Miss Kate Beeslev's recitals were encored. Miss Polly Houghton was well received, her renderings of "April morn ' and " Star of twilight" being of an excellent character. " Excelsior" was the title of the duet sung by the Misses Houghton. Another feature of the entertainment was the humorous sketches by Mr. E. A. Sanders, who amused the company with" The Liverwing testimonial," The one hair," and " Lecture on Physiognomy." Further amusement was provided by Mr. Alfred Grounds who created much mirth with his songs.</p>
      </div>
