      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT BAPTIST UNION. -- Remarkable " Revival."</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT BAPTIST UNION. -- Remarkable " Revival."</p>
        <p>• On Thursday afternoon and evening a series of meet- ings in connection with the above Union was held in the Latchford Baptist Church, when the ministers, delegates, members, and friends connected with the Churches of Latchford. Hill Cliff, Golborne, Little Leigh, Milton (Acton Bridge), Lymm, and Warrington (Golborne-street), assembled in goodly numbers. AFTERNOON MEETINGS. At 3.15 there was a meeting of the delegates, presided over by Mr. William Hough jun. of Acton Bridge' one of the vice-presidents, at which the delegates from Golborn were specially welcomed, this Church having just joined the Union and the secretary, Mr. Fearnley, announced that the Lymm Church had also decided to join the Union. During the meeting the Rev. A. Hum of Little Leigh stated that it was the desire of his Church that the Association should assist them in forming a Baptist Church at Baruton near Northwich there being a lack of Free .31:torches in that district and after discussion it was decided to accede to the request and a sum of money was voted as a preliminary step towards the object. On the invitation of the delegates from the Golborne Church it was decided to hold the May meetings of the Union at Golborne, the Rev. J. S. Hughes and Mr. E. England being appointed as the principal speakers for the occasion with one in additionsto be chosen by the Golborne Church.</p>
        <p>-------- At 4.30 a prayer meeting and conference presided over by the Rev. J. S. Hughes, the President of the Associ- tion, was held and afterwards an interesting paper on the subject "Is there need for more Baptist Churches in the country districts? " wo rend by Mr. Thomas Rigby,. of Acton Bridge, this being followed by a discussion. At 5.30 tea was provided in the schoolroom adjoining the church, the tables being presided over by several) ladies attached to the Latchford Church.</p>
        <p>EVENING MEETING. EXTRAORDINARY SCENE. At 7.30 there was a public meeting, at which there was an increased attendance, the Rev. J. F. Matthews, the pastor of the church, occupying the chair. Stirring addresses were given by Mr. Edward Wynne-Jones, of Warrington. The Rev. A. Hum, of Little Leigh, And the Rev. .1. W. Varley, of St. Anne's on the Sea during the course of the meeting. An anthem was ably rendered by the Choir,Mr. Peter Harrison officiating at the organ, and Mr. Wilkinson conducting. Just prior to the close of the meeting anextraordinary scene took place, the congregation present breaking into praise, thanksgiving and prayer, apparently having been: affected by the description of the scenes witnessed and testified to by one of the speakers, viz.. the Rev. J. W. Varley, who had been present at several of the Revival services now being held in South Wales and various parts of England.</p>
        <p>Concert in the Co-operative Hall. A highly successful and enjoyable concert pro- moted by the choir of Leh-street Primitive Methodist Chapel was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey- street, on Thursday evening, and was largely attended. The choir wliich, it will be remembered, carried off the prize in the chapel choir contest at the Warring— ton Eisteddfod, rendered several glees, part songs, and choruses with much taste and expression, and the creditable treatment of each item spoke volumes for the training receive:: from the conductor (Mr. A. Spruce) and organist (Mr. F. Podmore, •In addition the .programme was contributed to by several well-known local artistes. Miss Pollie Houghton's sweet and flexible soprano voice was heard to distinct advantage in " The Waltz Song " (from " Romeo and Juliet ") and " April Morn "and she was heartily encored for tbe latter. Mr. A. Griffiths (tenor) gave a feeling interpretation of "River of Dart " and Mr,‘E. H. Pearce sang "Off to Philadelphia " with. such spirit as to call forth ad encore. A duet " Love. and war " by Messrs. Griffiths and Pearce was a very acceptable item. Mr. A. Bennett had the honour of' a recall for a skilfully-phyed violin solo and Mr. A. White mistilied the ;.udience with sleight-of-hand. Humorous recitations by Mr. E. A. Sanders were, as usual, splendidly received and to comply with the- unmistakable desire of the audience he gave one or two extra sketches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-02-25_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>MOTOR CYCLIST KILLED.</head>
        <p>MOTOR CYCLIST KILLED.</p>
        <p>A motor cycle accident, attended with fatal results. occurred at Cockermouth, on Thursday afternoon. Mr. Robert Brown, builder. Workington, was riding down Embleton-avenne. and in trying to evade an approach- ing vehicle wns thrown, from his motor cycle death taking place in a very short time.</p>
      </div>
