wen, seconded by Mr. Vere B. Davies, and Mr. W. IL J. Jenkins, -seconded by Mr. John Hesketh. Mr. Bolton was pro- Posed by Mr. W. D. Jeans, sea., seconded by Dr. ;Langdale, Mr. W. E. Fowler, seconded by Dr. Lead' 'better and the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, seconded by Mr. Joseph Potter. Effect of a Distress Warrant. On Saturday afternoon Mr. Thomas Cowdell, estate agent, called at the house of Mrs. Mary G-oulding,West- atreet, to collect certain arrears of rent. Mrs. Gould- ing pleaded that she was unable to meet Mr. Cowdell's &lt;slemands where upon the estate agent threatened to 'lvy a distress upon her goods. Mrs. Goulding .. threatened to commit suicide if such a step were taken and rushing into the back kitchen slued a razor which she drew across her throat, Mr. Cowdell to lo ed and prevented her doing any further injury , wouerself. P.C. Lewis was called in and dressed the _n d, Mrs. Goulding was taken to the infirmary and o Wednesday was brought before the magistrates and .cha , her rirel with' attempting to commit suicide. Upon • • allowed go. husband promising to look after her she was a s ..... PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER THE CLOCK Port BEST GOOTSIN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Boy's Sad Discovery. _ Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquiry at the •Poelice Station on Monday, touching the death of I.l_o rge Edge, gas stove fitter, aged 33 years, of 47, . Marbury-street.— Thomas Percy Edge, son of the deesased, said be and his father retired to bed at 9.30 en Thursday night. His father had had some drink, -and appeared rather strange. On the following morning about six o'clock his father rose, but for some reason returned to bed. About 7.30 witness awoke' ~, d missed hls father. He went downstairs and saw n nini hanging by a rope attached to a hook in the kitchen wall. He rushed out and told a man who was 'on passing. Witness's mother was away from home a visit to her father in Cheshire, and during her absence • his father did not go to work, and commenced I. drinking, and scarcely partook of food. He had his .`ieast injured some years ago, and since then when in -drink had been strange in his mind.—Thomas Morris, Carter, of 65, Chorley-street, said he was called in to the house, and found dsceased hanging'by a rope. lie at once cut li'm down and sent for Dr. Smith.—A 'verdict of " • Suicide whilst of urisound mind " was 'returned.</p>
        <p>football just yet. However; youth will be served, and there is no doubt that he will get a hearty welcome from the crowd. Harris also comes in again, ishile ' Brookes and Kenyon are relegated to the "A " team. The latter pair have deterior- ated somewhat of late, and the change may do them good. J. Davies supplants his namesake, and on last Saturday's form ought to in- crease the effectiveness of the rear division. The forwards will be uncommonly light, for Preston, Boardman, and Shugars are all absentees, the places of the latter pair being taken by Morrison and Harmer. It is expected that the first three will be all right for next week. The teams for to-day are : Warrington : Hallam ; Fish, Isherwood, Berry and Harris; J. Davies and R. Bate; Thomas, Belton, Cook, Jolley, Naylor,Swift, Morrison and Harmer, forwards. -- Swinton : Dixon; G-artrell, A. Valentine,Wallivork, Flynn; Holyneux and Kewin ; Barnett, Cheetharn, Jones, Pomfret, Massey, Coletrup, Cooper, dan Larkin, forwards. Warrington "A" : Knight;; Kenyon, Brooks, Dickenson and Short; D. Davies and W. Bate; Foster, Lunt, Coleman, Fairhurst and Owens, forwards. The cup-draw has left the League clubs admirably placed, and nearly all have easy journeys to the second round. Warrington entertain Morecambe and should have little trouble in disposing of the seasiders. Leigh v. Wigan will be a deadly affair, while Widnes v. Wakefield gives promise of a close game. Runcorn are again unfortunate in having to go to Broughton. The other draws are published elsewhere in this PaPec- Hockey. [By R.G.P.] Last week Stockton Heath first eleven entertained Chester at Grappenhall-road, and as the visitors had been defeated the previous week by no less than 13-1, the hornesters were fairly confklert of victory. In the first ten minutes of the game, to everybody's surprise, the visitors had considerably the better of the play, and only weak play by their forwards in the circle kept them from taking the lead early on. Then the Stockton Heath forwards took up the attack,and after several smart raids on the Chester goal, W. Longdin put on the first goal, and this proved to be all tha scoring at the interval. During the first half the Chester team may be said to have had most of the play, their efforts being full of dash, and with a little more combination they would have crossed with a substantial lead. As it was the home side were now playing with the wind, and it was a case of how many goals the Stockton Heath forwards would put on before the end. During the last twenty minutes four goals were added to the score, S. Baxter obtaining three and C. H. Bond one. The final score of 5-0 hardly represents the play,and had Chester obtained three goals, which they ought to have done, this would have been as much as the home side deserved, viz., a victory of 5-3. The second team visited Chester, and were defeated 3-1, after having the bet4er of the exchanges, only weak shooting by the Stockton Heath forwards enabling the home side to claim the victory. The goal for Stockton Heath was scored by W. Howard. This week most of the hest teams have cancelled their fixtures, so as to be present at the North v. South match, which takes place at Bowdon this afternoon, and for this reason both the Ist and 2nd team engagements, namely, Eccles and lVrexbam, have been abandoned. Latchford entertained the soldiers on Saturday and won by no less than 14-0, a score which would have been much larger but for the good form of the Barracks goal-keeper. The " Tommies ' are a bit new to Hockey. Latchford second sustained their third defeat of the season at Sale on Saturday,when Sale Moor beat [them by 3 goals to 2. The score about represents the run of the play, the unevenness of the ground handicapping the Latchford men. PHYLLIS THE ELUSIVE. , An Unliteral Rendering. _ [By Godfrey Dean.] Peace of mind is not for the lover. If he had it, he would not be in love. Doubts, indeed are the logic of love. Yet he is ceaselessly striving to dispel them— Love's Labour Lost " ! This moralising was inspired by Phyllis. She is a psychological study— to me. I had left her with the prospect—a definite arrange- ment--.of seeing her on the followin z day. By the morning's post comes a letter. " Will you stay away. . . . . lam not myself. I ought not to see you. And more to the same effect. But a short note only, If Phyllis, then, did not wish to see me, she could not really love me. The conclusion, drawn from my own feelings, was inevitable. heft for Cadogan square, fall oF a desperate pur- pose, which I lost sight of eventually. In my sub- consciousness, however, was the intention to discover her reason. Phyllis's cheek was warm with colour. And I wondered how long it had been there. Hy chair was placed invitingly for me; if it was not deliberately placed there, then the azcident was remarkable. This might mean that if, despite her letter, I came she would feel it necessary to tell me of —what? An I unsettled mind only, ? I groaned in ,spi -it but preserved an impassive demeanour. Phyllis's eyes rested szravely on me. "I expected you,' she said nodding in the direction of the chair. I Was this to be my hour of agony? Whatever Phyllis had to say—to confess come spontaneously from her. I would not assist—l would not lead her—in arty way. " I thought not." " What did you think ?" I asked. Phyllis gave a slight start of surprise. " I did not , know I spoke." " You did not—clearly. I read your lips and ex- pression." " I was sure you would come," she said. " Were you ?' Phyllis gave me one of those quick glances which seem to drag my heart out. " Was I not justified ?" " Yes, you are, by the result." ' " Wouldn't you wish me to be certain, then ? " Of course, in the game of love the man should never let the woman be quite sure of anything. But though one's theory may be good, this, alas ! is a matter of practice. "Isn't it too late now," I asked, in a tone of infinite regret. "Not," she said—with slightly averted face—" if you really wish it." "How am Ito know that it will not always be so, that you will not calculate on my doing what you wish, whether it be the opposite to what you ask or no? Disappointment, disillusion must follow." " You cannot know," she said. "There is no guarantee." "Then I sin afraid that some day, Phyllis, you may be grieved to find me apparently changed." "If you really changed—yes. ' Where the point is not important, my purpose would disappear before her smile as snow before the summer sun. But all Phyllis's charms could not melt the iron of my resolve were the question of moment." "I suppose the first time I take you literally I shall have changed ?" Phyllis appeared to be amused. " Yes." " Why should you not write exactly what you mean ?" " Why should I ?" she retorted. " Because, Phillis, I wish it. Do you never think of the anxiety you cause me ?" "Do you get anxious, then ?"—with an innocent ex- pression. "I thought you were sure "—she stopped. "Sure—what of ?" " Of —never being anxious." I regarded Phyllis sternly ; for this was not even imagination—simply assumption.</p>
